window.CMA_QS=[{"id":"v2-su14-q334","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) A large manufacturer’s forecast of total sales revenues for a\nyear is least likely to be influenced by\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget","options":["The seasonal pattern of sales revenues throughout the year.","Anticipated interest rates and unemployment rates.","Expected shortages of key raw materials.","Input from sales personnel."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A large manufacturing firm will be well familiar with the\nseasonal patterns to which demand for its products is subject.\nThe forecast is being prepared for an entire year, so the\ncyclical effects of varying demand will be taken into account.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q335","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) A company’s sales budget for the coming year is as follows.\nItem\nVolume\nin Units\nSales\nPrice\nSales\nRevenue\n1 200,000 $50 $10,000,000\n2 150,000 10 1,500,000\n3 300,000 30 9,000,000\nTotal sales revenue $20,500,000\nItems 1 and 3 are different models of the same product. Item 2\nis a complement to Item 1. Past experience indicates that the\nsales volume of Item 2 relative to the sales volume of Item 1 is\nfairly constant. The company is considering a 10% price\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nincrease for the coming year for Item 1, which will cause sales\nof Item 1 to decline by 20%, while simultaneously causing\nsales of Item 3 to increase by 5%. If the company institutes the\nprice increase for Item 1, total sales revenue will decrease by","options":["$1,050,000","$850,000","$750,000","$550,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"First, the changes in sales volume can be calculated:\nItem\nUnit Volume\nBefore Price\nChange\nUnit\nChange\nFactor\nUnit Volume After\nPrice Change\n1 200,000 × 0.80 = 160,000\n2 150,000 × 0.80 = 120,000\n3 300,000 × 1.05 = 315,000\nNext, the changes in unit price:\nItem\nPrice Before\nPrice Change\nPrice\nChange\nFactor\nPrice After Price\nChange\n1 $50 × 1.10 = $55\n2 10 × 1.00 = 10\n3 30 × 1.00 = 30\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nThird, the total sales revenue resulting from the changes in\nvolume and price:\nItem\nUnit Volume\nAfter Price\nChange\nPrice After\nPrice Change\nSales Revenue\nAfter Price Change\n1 160,000 × $55 = $ 8,800,000\n2 120,000 × 10 = 1,200,000\n3 315,000 × 30 = 9,450,000\n$19,450,000\nThe decrease in the company’s total revenue after the price\nchange will therefore be $1,050,000 ($20,500,000 –\n$19,450,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q336","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) Which of the following factors is least likely to influence the\nsales forecast of a company?","options":["Elasticity of product demand.","Government monetary policy.\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget","Pricing strategies of competitors.","Product cost structure."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The cost structure of products has a direct impact on the\nforecast of costs, not sales.\nFact Pattern: Niro Company is a printer manufacturer. Niro’s sales\nbudget for the year is as follows:\nProduct Volume (units) Price\nInkjet printers 5,000 $120\nInk cartridge set 12,000 100\nLaser printer 3,000 300\nNiro is planning to increase the price of one product while keeping the\nprices of the other two products unchanged. To facilitate the forecast,\nthe effects of every 5% change in the price of a product on the\nquantity demanded (in absolute percentages) of the three products\nare calculated, as summarized below. The ink cartridge set is a\ncomplementary product to inkjet printers, while laser printers are a\nsubstitute of inkjet printers.\nEffect on inkjet\nprinter\nEffect on ink\ncartridge set\nEffect on laser\nprinter\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\n5% increase\nin inkjet\nprinter price\n7.5% 12.5% 5.0%\n5% increase\nin ink\ncartridge\nset price 5.0% 15.0% 2.5%\n5% increase\nin laser\nprinter price\n20.0% 20.0% 10.0%","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q337","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) If Niro increases the price of its inkjet printers, total sales\nrevenue would","options":["Decrease by $212,250.","Increase by $87,750.","Decrease by $122,250.","Increase by $272,250."],"answer":"C","explanation":"If the price of inkjet printers increases, the quantity of inkjet\nprinters demanded would decrease. Inkjet printers and ink\ncartridges are complements (i.e., their demands change in the\nsame direction); therefore, as the price of inkjet printers\nincreases, the quantity of ink cartridges demanded decreases.\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nInkjet printers and laser printers are substitutes (i.e., their\ndemands change in the opposite direction); therefore, as the\nprice of inkjet printers increases, the quantity of laser printers\ndemanded increases. Thus, the percentage changes in the\nquantity demanded for the three products given the price\nincrease are as follows:\nEffect on inkjet\nprinter\nEffect on ink\ncartridge set\nEffect on laser\nprinter\n5%\nincrease\nin inkjet\nprinter\nprice -7.5% -12.5% 5.0%\nThe sales revenue before the price change can be calculated as\nfollows:\nProduct Volume (units) Price Sales revenue\nInkjet printer 5,000 × $120 = $ 600,000\nInk cartridge set 12,000 × 100 = 1,200,000\nLaser printer 3,000 × 300 = 900,000\nTotal $2,700,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nThe sales revenue after the price change can be calculated as\nfollows:\nProdu\nct Volume (units) Price\nSales\nrevenu\ne\nInkjet\nprinter\n4,625 [5,000 × (1 –\n7.5%)]\n× $126 [$120 ×\n(1 + 5.0%)]\n=$ 582\n,750\nInk\ncartridge\nset\n10,500 [12,000 ×\n(1 – 12.5%)]\n×100 = 1,050,\n000\nLaser\nprinter\n3,150 [3,000 × (1 +\n5.0%)]\n×300 =945,00\n0\nTotal $2,577\n,750\nSales revenue would decrease by $122,250 ($2,700,000 –\n$2,577,750).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q338","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) If Niro increases the price of its ink cartridges, total sales\nrevenue would","options":["Decrease by $181,500.\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget","Decrease by $136,500.","Increase by $241,500.","Increase by $301,500."],"answer":"B","explanation":"If the price of ink cartridge sets increases, the quantity of ink\ncartridge sets demanded would decrease. Inkjet printers and ink\ncartridges are complements (i.e., their demands change in the\nsame direction); therefore, as the price of ink cartridge sets\nincreases, the quantity of inkjet printers demanded decreases.\nInk cartridge sets and laser printers are substitutes (i.e., their\ndemands change in the opposite direction); therefore, as the\nprice of ink cartridge sets increases, the quantity of laser\nprinters demanded increases. Thus, the percentage changes in\nthe quantity demanded for the three products given the price\nincrease are as follows:\nEffect on inkjet\nprinter\nEffect on ink\ncartridge set\nEffect on laser\nprinter\n5%\nincrease\nin ink\ncartridge\nset price -5.0% -15.0% 2.5%\nThe sales revenue before the price change can be calculated as\nfollows:\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nProduct Volume (units) Price Sales revenue\nInkjet printer 5,000 × $120 = $ 600,000\nInk cartridge set 12,000 × 100 = 1,200,000\nLaser printer 3,000 × 300 = 900,000\nTotal $2,700,000\nThe sales revenue after the price change can be calculated as\nfollows:\nProdu\nct Volume (units) Price\nSales\nrevenu\ne\nInkjet\nprinter\n4,750 [5,000 × (1\n– 5%)]\n×$12\n0\n=$ 570\n,000\nInk\ncartridge\nset\n10,200 [12,000 ×\n(1 – 15%)]\n× 105 [$100 × (1 + 5%)] = 1,071,\n000\nLaser\nprinter\n3,075 [3,000 × (1\n+ 2.5%)]\n× 300 =922,50\n0\nTotal $2,563\n,500\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nSales revenue would decrease by $136,500 ($2,700,000 –\n$2,563,500).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q339","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) If Niro increases the price of its laser printers, total sales\nrevenue would","options":["Decrease by $409,500.","Increase by $297,000.","Increase by $310,500.","Increase by $499,500."],"answer":"C","explanation":"If the price of the laser printers increases, the quantity of laser\nprinters demanded would decrease. Inkjet printers and laser\nprinters are substitutes (i.e., their demands change in the\nopposite direction); therefore, as the price of laser printers\nincreases, the quantity of inkjet printers demanded increases.\nInk cartridge sets and laser printers are also substitutes. As the\nprice of laser printers increases, the quantity of ink cartridge\nsets demanded increases. Thus, the percentage changes in the\nquantity demanded for the three products given the price\nincrease are as follows:\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nEffect on inkjet\nprinter\nEffect on ink\ncartridge set\nEffect on laser\nprinter\n5%\nincrease\nin laser\nprinter\nprice 20.0% 20.0% -10.0%\nThe sales revenue before the price change can be calculated as\nfollows:\nProduct Volume (units) Price Sales revenue\nInkjet printer 5,000 × $120 = $ 600,000\nInk cartridge set 12,000 × 100 = 1,200,000\nLaser printer 3,000 × 300 = 900,000\nTotal $2,700,000\nThe sales revenue after the price change can be calculated as\nfollows:\nProdu\nct Volume (units) Price\nSales\nreven\nue\nInkjet\nprinter\n6,000 [5,000 × (1 +\n20.0%)]\n×$1\n20\n= $ 72\n0,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nInk\ncartridge\nset\n14,400 [12,000 ×\n(1 + 20.0%)]\n×100 =1,440,\n000\nLaser\nprinter\n2,700 [3,000 × (1 –\n10.0%)]\n×315 [$300 × (1 + 5.0%)] = 850,5\n00\nTotal $3,01\n0,500\nSales revenue would increase by $310,500 ($3,010,500 –\n$2,700,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q340","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) The budget that is usually the most difficult to forecast is the","options":["Production budget.","Expense budget.","Sales budget.","Manufacturing overhead budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Following the preparation of the sales budget, all other\nbudgets are prepared based on the assumptions used in the\nsales budget. For this reason, the sales budget is the most\ndifficult to prepare because there are no internal figures to use\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget\nas a guide. Sales are based on the desires of consumers and\nthe current business climate.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q341","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) When sales volume is seasonal in nature, certain items in the\nbudget must be coordinated. The three most significant items\nto coordinate in budgeting seasonal sales volume are","options":["Direct labor hours, work-in-process inventory, and sales\nvolume.","Production volume, finished goods inventory, and sales\nvolume.","Raw material inventory, direct labor hours, and manufacturing\noverhead costs.","Raw material inventory, work-in-process inventory, and\nproduction volume.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\n14: (175) Budgeting -- Calculations and Pro Forma\nFinancial Statements\n2: (43) Operating Budget Calculations -- Production and\nDirect Materials\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations -- Production\nand Direct Materials\nFact Pattern:\nCompany A manufactures a toy rabbit\nwith moving parts and a built-in voice box.\nProjected sales in units for the next\n5 months are as follows:\nMonth\nProjected\nSales in Units\nJanuary 30,000\nFebruary 36,000\nMarch 33,000\nApril 40,000\nMay 29,000\nEach rabbit requires\nbasic materials that A\npurchases from a\nsingle supplier at $3.50\nper rabbit. Voice boxes\nare purchased from\nanother supplier at\n$1.00 each. Assembly\nlabor cost is $2.00 per\nrabbit, and variable\noverhead cost is $.50\nper rabbit. Fixed\nmanufacturing\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\noverhead applicable to\nrabbit production is\n$12,000 per month. A’s\npolicy is to\nmanufacture 1.5 times\nthe coming month’s\nprojected sales every\nother month, starting\nwith January (i.e., odd-\nnumbered months) for\nFebruary sales, and to\nmanufacture 0.5 times\nthe coming month’s\nprojected sales in\nalternate months (i.e.,\neven-numbered\nmonths). This allows A\nto allocate limited\nmanufacturing\nresources to other\nproducts as needed\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nduring the even-\nnumbered months."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The most important items that need to be coordinated in a\nseasonal business are sales volume and production. The\nsales budget is the basis for other budgets. The sales\nprojection determines how much needs to be purchased and\nproduced. In turn, projected sales and production (or\npurchases) must be coordinated with existing quantities on\nhand (inventory) and with amounts to be held in the future. If a\nmanufacturer faces sharp variations in demand, this\ncoordination becomes especially crucial.\n\nStatements | Subunit 1: Sales Forecasts and the Sales Budget","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q342","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) Company A’s unit production budget for toy rabbits for January\nis","options":["45,000 units.","16,500 units.","54,000 units.","14,500 units."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The production budget for January is 54,000 units (36,000\nprojected February sales × 1.5).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q343","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) Company A’s dollar production budget for toy rabbits for\nFebruary is","options":["$327,000","$390,000","$113,500","$127,500\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The units to be produced in February equal 50% of March\nsales, or 16,500 units (33,000 × .5). The unit variable cost is\n$7.00 ($3.50 + $1.00 + $2.00 + $.50), so total variable costs\nare $115,500 (16,500 × $7). Thus, the dollar production\nbudget for February is $127,500 ($115,500 variable + $12,000\nfixed).\nFact Pattern:\nMountain Corporation manufactures cabinets\nbut outsources the handles. Eight handles are\nneeded for a cabinet, with assembly requiring\n30 minutes of direct labor per unit. Ending\nfinished goods inventory is planned to consist\nof 50% of projected unit sales for the next\nmonth, and ending handles inventory is\nplanned to be 80% of the requirement for the\nnext month’s projected unit output of finished\ngoods.\nMountain’s projected\nunit sales:\nOctober 4,600\nNovember 5,000\nDecember 4,200\nJanuary 6,000\nMountain’s ending\ninventories in units at\nSeptember 30:\nFinished\ngoods\n3,800\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nHandles 16,000","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q344","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) The number of units that Mountain finished during December\nis","options":["3,000","5,100","4,200","5,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The company will need 4,200 finished units to meet the sales\nestimate for December. In addition, 3,000 finished units (6,000\nunit sales in January × 50%) should be in inventory at the end\nof December. The total requirement is therefore 7,200 units\n(4,200 + 3,000). Of these units, 2,100 (4,200 unit sales in\nDecember × 50%) should be available from November’s\nending inventory. Consequently, production in December\nshould be 5,100 units (7,200 – 2,100).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q345","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) The number of handles Mountain should purchase in October\nis\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","options":["39,840","76,800","40,000","36,800\nFact Pattern:\nJordan Auto has\ndeveloped the following\nproduction plan:\nMonth Units\nJanuary 10,000\nFebruary 8,000\nMarch 9,000\nApril 12,000\nEach unit contains 3 pounds of direct\nmaterials. The desired direct materials\nending inventory each month is 120% of the\nnext month’s production, plus 500 pounds.\n(The beginning inventory meets this\nrequirement.) Jordan has developed the\nfollowing direct labor standards for\nproduction of these units:\nDepartment 1 Department 2\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nHours per unit 2.0 0.5\nHourly rate $7.25 $12.00"],"answer":"A","explanation":"To determine the correct number of handles purchased for\nOctober, the projected unit output of finished goods for\nOctober and November must be calculated.\nProjected sales of cabinets in October 4,600\nAdd: Required ending inventory\n(50% of projected November sales) 2,500\nLess: Beginning inventory (3,800)\nTotal cabinet production for October 3,300\nProjected sales of cabinets in November 5,000\nAdd: Required ending inventory\n(50% of projected December sales) 2,100\nLess: Beginning inventory\n(Ending inventory from October) (2,500)\nTotal cabinet production for November 4,600\nThe calculation to determine the number of handles to be\npurchased is as follows:\nHandles for October production\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\n(3,300 × 8 handles per cabinet) 26,400\nHandles for November production\nrequired in October ending inventory\n(4,600 × 8 × 80%) 29,440\nLess: Beginning inventory (16,000)\nTotal handles to be purchased in October 39,840","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q346","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) How much direct materials should Jordan Auto purchase in\nMarch?","options":["27,000 pounds.","32,900 pounds.","36,000 pounds.","37,800 pounds."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Jordan needs 27,000 pounds (3 × 9,000 units) of materials for\nMarch production. It also needs 43,700 pounds {[(3 ×\n12,000 units to be produced in April) × 120%] + 500} for\nending inventory. Given a beginning inventory of\n32,900 pounds {[(3 × 9,000 units to be produced in March) ×\n120%] + 500}, required purchases equal 37,800 pounds\n(27,000 pounds + 43,700 pounds – 32,900 pounds).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q347","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) A company’s budget calls for the following production:\nQtr 1 -- 45,000 units\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nQtr 2 -- 38,000 units\nQtr 3 -- 34,000 units\nQtr 4 -- 48,000 units\nEach unit of product requires three pounds of direct material.\nThe company’s policy is to begin each quarter with an\ninventory of direct materials equal to 30% of that quarter’s\ndirect material requirements. Budgeted direct materials\npurchases for the third quarter would be","options":["114,600 pounds.","43,200 pounds.","38,200 pounds.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","30,600 pounds."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Beginning inventory should be 30,600 pounds (34,000 units of\nbudgeted sales × 3 pounds × 30%). Ending inventory should\nbe 43,200 pounds (48,000 units of budgeted sales for Quarter\n4 × 3 pounds × 30%). Because BI plus purchases minus EI\nequals Quarter 3 budgeted sales, purchases must be 114,600.\n30,600 + X – 43,200 = 3 × 34,000\nX – 12,600 = 102,000\nX = 114,600","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q348","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) A sales budget shows quarterly sales for the next year as\nfollows:\nQuarter Units\n1 10,000\n2 8,000\n3 12,000\n4 14,000\nCompany policy is to have a finished goods inventory at the\nend of each quarter equal to 20% of the next quarter’s sales.\nBudgeted production for the second quarter of the next year\nwould be","options":["7,200 units.","8,000 units.","8,800 units.","8,400 units.\nFact Pattern: Superflite expects April sales of its deluxe model\nairplane, the C-14, to be 402,000 units at $11 each. Each C-\n14 requires three purchased components shown below.\nPurchase Cost\nNumber Needed\nfor Each C-14 Unit\nA-9 $0.50 1\nB-6 0.25 2\nD-28 1.00 3\nManufacturing direct labor and variable overhead per unit of C-14\ntotals $3.00. Fixed manufacturing overhead is $1.00 per unit at a\nproduction level of 500,000 units. Superflite plans the following\nbeginning and ending inventories for the month of April and uses\nstandard absorption costing for valuing inventory.\nPart No. Units at April 1 Units at April 30\nC-14 12,000 10,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nA-9 21,000 9,000\nB-6 32,000 10,000\nD-28 14,000 6,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The finished units needed for sales (8,000), plus the units\ndesired for ending inventory (12,000 units to be sold in the\nthird quarter × 20% = 2,400), minus the units in beginning\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\ninventory (8,000 units to be sold in the second quarter × 20%\n= 1,600) equals budgeted production for the second quarter of\n8,800 units.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q349","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) Superflite’s C-14 production budget for April should be based\non the manufacture of","options":["390,000 units.","400,000 units.","402,000 units.","424,000 units."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Sales are expected to be 402,000 units in April. The beginning\ninventory is 12,000 units, and the ending inventory is expected\nto be 10,000 units, a decline in inventory of 2,000 units. Thus,\nthe budget should be based on production of 400,000 units\n(402,000 units to be sold – 12,000 units BI + 10,000 units EI).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q350","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) Assume Superflite plans to manufacture 400,000 units in April.\nSuperflite’s April budget for the purchase of A-9 should be","options":["379,000 units.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","388,000 units.","402,000 units.","412,000 units.\nFact Pattern: Simpson, Inc., is in the process of preparing its annual\nbudget. The following beginning and ending inventory levels (in units)\nare planned for the year ending December 31.\nBeginning Ending\nInventory Inventory\nRaw material* 40,000 50,000\nWork-in-process 10,000 10,000\nFinished goods 80,000 50,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\n*Two units of raw material are needed to produce each unit of finished\nproduct."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Each of the 400,000 units to be produced in April will require\none unit of A-9, a total requirement of 400,000 units. In\naddition, ending inventory is expected to be 9,000 units.\nHence, 409,000 units must be supplied during the month. Of\nthese, 21,000 are available in the beginning inventory.\nSubtracting the 21,000 beginning inventory from 409,000\nleaves 388,000 to be purchased.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q351","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) If 500,000 finished units were to be manufactured for the year\nby Simpson, the units of raw material that must be purchased\nwould be","options":["1,000,000 units.","1,020,000 units.","1,010,000 units.","990,000 units.\nFact Pattern: Paradise Company budgets on an annual basis for its\nfiscal year. The following beginning and ending inventory levels (in\nunits) are planned for the fiscal year of July 1 through June 30:\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nJuly 1 June 30\nDirect material* 40,000 50,000\nWork-in-process 10,000 20,000\nFinished goods 80,000 50,000\n* Two units of direct material are needed to produce each unit of\nfinished product."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The 500,000 finished units to be manufactured require\n1,000,000 units of raw material (2 × 500,000). In addition, the\ninventory of raw material is planned to increase by 10,000\nunits. Consequently, 1,010,000 units of raw material should be\npurchased.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q352","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) If Paradise Company plans to sell 480,000 units during the\nfiscal year, the number of units it will have to manufacture\nduring the year is","options":["440,000 units.","480,000 units.","510,000 units.","450,000 units."],"answer":"D","explanation":"If the company sells 480,000 units with an ending finished\ngoods inventory of 50,000 units, 530,000 units must be\navailable. Given 80,000 units are in beginning inventory,\nproduction will have to be 450,000 units (530,000 – 80,000).\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q353","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) If 500,000 complete units are to be manufactured during the\nfiscal year by Paradise Company, the number of units of direct\nmaterials to be purchased is","options":["1,000,000 units.","1,020,000 units.","1,010,000 units.","990,000 units.\nFact Pattern: Scarborough Corporation manufactures and sells two\nproducts, Thingone and Thingtwo. Scarborough’s budget department\ngathered the following data to project sales and budget requirements:\nProjected Sales\nProduct Units Price\nThingone 60,000 $ 70\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nThingtwo 40,000 100\nProjected Inventories -- in units\nExpected Desired\nProduct January 1 December 31\nThingone 20,000 25,000\nThingtwo 8,000 9,000\nTo produce one unit of Thingone and Thingtwo, the following raw\nmaterials are used:\nRaw Material Unit Thingone Thingtwo\nA lb. 4 5\nB lb. 2 3\nC each 0 1\nProjected data for the year with respect to raw materials are as\nfollows:\nAnticipated Expected Desired\nRaw Purchase Inventories Inventories\nMaterial Price 1/1 12/31\nA $8 32,000 lb. 36,000 lb.\nB 5 29,000 lb. 32,000 lb.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nC 3 6,000 each 7,000 each\nProjected direct labor requirements and rates are as follows:\nThingone -- 2 hours per unit at $3 per hour\nThingtwo -- 3 hours per unit at $4 per hour\nOverhead is applied at the rate of $2 per direct labor hour."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The total direct materials needed for production are\n1,000,000 units (500,000 units × 2 units of direct materials). In\naddition, direct materials inventory is expected to increase by\n10,000 units. Thus, direct materials purchases will be\n1,010,000.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q354","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) What is Scarborough’s production budget in units for each\nproduct for the year?\nThingone Thingtwo","options":["55,000 39,000","85,000 49,000","60,000 40,000","65,000 41,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"To calculate the production required in units, determine the\nprojected sales, desired inventories, and expected\ninventories at the beginning of the cycle.\nThingone Thingtwo\nProjected sales 60,000 40,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nDesired inventories, December 31 25,000 9,000\nTotal needed 85,000 49,000\nMinus: Expected inventories,\nJanuary 1\n(20,000) (8,000)\nProduction required (units) 65,000 41,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q355","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) What is Scarborough’s raw materials budget in quantities?\nA B C","options":["533,000 314,000 54,000","469,000 256,000 42,000","Thingone and Thingtwo require different proportions of\neach item. Once production requirements are established, add\ndesired ending inventory and subtract the beginning inventory\nof each raw material to arrive at purchases required.\nA B C\nThingone (65,000 units\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nprojected to be\nproduced)\n260,000 130,000 0\nThingtwo (41,000 units\nprojected to be\nproduced)\n205,000 123,000 41,000\nProduction\nrequirements\n465,000 253,000 41,000\nAdd: Desired\ninventories, 12/31\n36,000 32,000 7,000\nTotal requirements 501,000 285,000 48,000\nLess: Expected\ninventories, 1/1\n(32,000) (29,000) (6,000)\nPurchase\nrequirements\n(units)\n469,000 256,000 42,00","465,000 253,000 41,000\nD. 501,000 285,000 48,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nFact Pattern: Wellfleet Company manufactures recreational\nequipment and prepares annual operational budgets for each\ndepartment. The Purchasing Department is finalizing plans for the\nfiscal year ending June 30, Year 2, and has gathered the information\nregarding two of the components used in both tricycles and bicycles.\nWellfleet uses the first-in, first-out inventory method.\nA19 B12 Tricycles Bicycles\nBeginning inventory, July 1,\nYear 1 3,500 1,200 800 2,150\nEnding inventory, June 30,\nYear 2 2,000 1,800 1,000 900\nUnit cost $1.20 $4.50 $54.50 $89.60\nProjected fiscal year unit sales -- -- 96,000 130,000\nComponent usage:\nTricycles 2/unit 1/unit -- --\nBicycles 2/unit 4/unit -- --"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The raw materials budget consists of raw materials A, B, and","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q356","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) The budgeted dollar value of Wellfleet Company’s purchases\nof component A19 for the fiscal year ending June 30, Year 2 is","options":["$309,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","$538,080","$540,600","$2,017,800\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nFact Pattern: Berol Company plans to sell 200,000 units of finished\nproduct in July and anticipates a growth rate in sales of 5% per month.\nThe desired monthly ending inventory in units of finished product is\n80% of the next month’s estimated sales. There are 150,000 finished\nunits in inventory on June 30. Each unit of finished product requires 4\npounds of direct materials at a cost of $1.20 per pound. There are\n800,000 pounds of direct materials in inventory on June 30."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The inventory of tricycles is expected to increase from 800\nunits to 1,000 units, an increase of 200 units. Adding this 200-\nunit inventory increase to the projected sales of 96,000 results\nin total production of tricycles of 96,200 units. The inventory of\nbicycles is expected to decline from 2,150 to 900, a decrease\nof 1,250 units. Subtracting this inventory decline from the\n130,000 units of projected sales results in expected production\nof 128,750 units. Given that each tricycle and bicycle requires\ntwo units of A19, the necessary units of the component can be\ncalculated by adding the 96,200 tricycles to the 128,750\nbicycles, a total production of 224,950. Multiplying this total\nproduction level times the two components required results in\na total of 449,900 components. Combining the 449,900 units\nof A19 needed for production with the desired inventory\ndecrease of 1,500 units (3,500 – 2,000) indicates that 448,400\ncomponents must be purchased. At $1.20 per unit, the total\ncost of 448,400 units is $538,080.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q357","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) Berol Company’s production requirement in units of finished\nproduct for the 3-month period ending September 30 is","options":["712,025 units.","630,500 units.","638,000 units.","665,720 units."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sales are expected to increase at the rate of 5% per month.\nGiven that July sales are estimated to be 200,000 units,\nAugust, September, and October sales are expected to be\n210,000 units (200,000 × 1.05), 220,500 units (210,000 ×\n1.05), and 231,525 units (220,500 × 1.05), respectively.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nMoreover, September ending inventory must be 80% of\nOctober’s estimated sales, or 185,220 units (231,525 × 80%).\nConsequently, the production requirement for the 3-month\nperiod is 665,720 units (200,000 + 210,000 + 220,500 +\n185,220 September EI – 150,000 July BI).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q358","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) Assume Berol Company plans to produce 600,000 units of\nfinished product in the 3-month period ending September 30,\nand to have direct materials inventory on hand at the end of\nthe 3-month period equal to 25% of the use in that period. The\nestimated cost of direct materials purchases for the 3-\nmonth period ending September 30 is","options":["$2,200,000","$2,400,000","$2,640,000","$2,880,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Production of 600,000 units will require 2,400,000 pounds of\ndirect materials (600,000 units × 4 lbs.). In addition, ending\ninventory will be 25% of the period’s usage, or 600,000\npounds (2,400,000 × 25%). Thus, 3,000,000 total pounds will\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nbe needed. However, given 800,000 pounds in inventory,\npurchases will be only 2,200,000 pounds. At $1.20 per pound,\nthe cost will be $2,640,000.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q359","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) A company has budgeted sales of 24,000 finished units for the\nforthcoming 6-month period. It takes 4 pounds of direct\nmaterials to make one finished unit. Given the following:\nFinished\nunits\nDirect materials\n(pounds)\nBeginning inventory 14,000 44,000\nTarget ending\ninventory\n12,000 48,000\nHow many pounds of direct materials should be budgeted for\npurchase during the 6-month period?","options":["48,000","88,000","92,000","96,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Required production of finished units is 22,000 units (target\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nending inventory of 12,000 + sales of 24,000 – beginning\ninventory of 14,000 lb.). Thus, 88,000 pounds of direct\nmaterials (22,000 × 4 lb. per unit) must be available. Required\npurchases of direct materials equal 92,000 pounds (target\nending inventory of 48,000 + usage of 88,000 – beginning\ninventory of 44,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q360","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) A company prices its products by adding 30% to its cost. The\ncompany anticipates sales of $715,000 in July, $728,000 in\nAugust, and $624,000 in September. The company’s policy is\nto have on hand enough inventory at the end of the month to\ncover 25% of the next month’s sales. What will be the cost of\nthe inventory that the company should budget for purchase in\nAugust?","options":["$509,600","$540,000","$560,000","$680,000\nFact Pattern: Streeter Company produces plastic microwave\nturntables. Sales for the next year are expected to be 65,000 units in\nthe first quarter, 72,000 units in the second quarter, 84,000 units in\nthe third quarter, and 66,000 units in the fourth quarter. Streeter\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nusually maintains a finished goods inventory at the end of each\nquarter equal to one half of the units expected to be sold in the next\nquarter."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first step is to determine the cost of goods sold for each\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nmonth. Since sales are 130% of cost, cost of goods sold can\nbe calculated as follows:\nJuly: $715,000 ÷ 130% = $550,000\nAugust: 728,000 ÷ 130% = 560,000\nSeptember: 624,000 ÷ 130% = 480,000\nPurchases for August can now be calculated as follows:\nProjected sales at cost $560,000\nAdd: Required ending inventory\n($480,000 × 25%) 120,000\nTotal goods needed $680,000\nLess: Beginning inventory (140,000)\nPurchases $540,000","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q361","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) Due to a work stoppage, the finished goods inventory at the\nend of the first quarter is 8,000 units less than it should be.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nHow many units should Streeter produce in the second\nquarter?","options":["75,000","78,000","80,000","86,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Streeter’s required production for the second quarter can be\ncalculated as follows:\nSales for quarter 72,000\nAdd: Buffer for next quarter (84,000 × 50%) 42,000\nLess: Buffer from previous quarter (72,000 × 50%) (36,000)\nProjected production 78,000\nAdd: Shortfall from previous month buffer 8,000\nRevised required production 86,000","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q362","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) A company produces one product and budgeted 220,000 units\nfor the month of August with the following budgeted\nmanufacturing costs:\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nTotal Costs Cost Per Unit\nVariable costs $1,408,000 $ 6.40\nBatch set-up cost 880,000 4.00\nFixed costs 1,210,000 5.50\nTotal $3,498,000 $15.90\nThe variable cost per unit and the total fixed costs are\nunchanged within a production range of 200,000 to 300,000\nunits per month. The total for the batch set-up cost in any\nmonth depends on the number of production batches that is\nrun. A normal batch consists of 50,000 units unless production\nrequires less volume. In the prior year, the company\nexperienced a mixture of monthly batch sizes of 42,000 units,\n45,000 units, and 50,000 units. The company consistently\nplans production each month in order to minimize the number\nof batches. For the month of September, the company plans to\nmanufacture 260,000 units. What will be the company’s total\nbudgeted production costs for September?","options":["$3,754,000","$3,930,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","$3,974,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","$4,134,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Since variable costs are constant across the relevant range,\nthe total variable cost for September will be $1,664,000\n(260,000 units × $6.40). Since the normal production run is\n50,000 units, and no indication is given that the company’s\nmachinery can handle a larger run, we can conclude that five\nbatches were needed in August (220,000 units total\nproduction ÷ 50,000 units per batch = 4.4 batches). The setup\ncost for a batch must therefore be $176,000 ($880,000 ÷ 5\nsetups). Six setups will be required for September (260,000\nunits total production ÷ 50,000 units per batch = 5.2 batches),\nfor a total of $1,056,000 in setup costs ($176,000 × 6 setups).\nFixed costs of $1,210,000 are unchanging within the relevant\nrange. Total budgeted production costs for September are\ntherefore:\nVariable costs $1,664,000\nBatch set-up cost 1,056,000\nFixed costs 1,210,000\nTotal $3,930,000","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q363","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) A company has budgeted sales at 6,300 units for the next\nfiscal year and desires to have 590 good units on hand at the\nend of that year. Beginning inventory is 470 units. The\ncompany has found from past experience that 10% of all units\nproduced do not pass final inspection and therefore must be\ndestroyed. How many units should the company plan to\nproduce in the next fiscal year?","options":["6,890","7,062","7,133","7,186"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The company’s required production for the year can be\ncalculated as follows:\nProjected sales 6,300\nAdd: Projected ending inventory 590\nLess: Beginning inventory (470)\nProduction 6,420\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nSince 10% of all units produced do not pass inspection, the\ncompany must produce 7,133 units (6,420 ÷ 90%).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q364","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) A corporation assembles backup tape drive systems for home\nmicrocomputers. For the first quarter, the budget for sales is\n67,500 units. The corporation will finish the fourth quarter of\nlast year with an inventory of 3,500 units, of which 200 are\nobsolete. The target ending inventory is 10 days of sales\n(based upon 360 days). What is the budgeted production for\nthe first quarter?","options":["75,000","71,700","71,500","64,350"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 67,500 of sales for the quarter average 750 per day for 90\ndays. Thus, production for the quarter can be calculated as\nfollows:\nBudgeted sales 67,500\nAdd: Required ending inventory (750 × 10 days) 7,500\nTotal units needed 75,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nLess: Beginning inventory (3,500 – 200) (3,300)\nBudgeted production 71,700","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q365","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) Data regarding a budget are shown below.\nPlanned sales 4,000 units\nMaterial cost $2.50 per pound\nDirect labor 3 hours per unit\nDirect labor rate $7 per hour\nFinished goods beginning inventory 900 units\nFinished goods ending inventory 600 units\nDirect materials beginning inventory 4,300 units\nDirect materials ending inventory 4,500 units\nMaterials used per unit 6 pounds\nThe production budget will show total units to be produced of","options":["3,700\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","4,000","4,300","4,600"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The required production for the year can be calculated as\nfollows:\nSales for year 4,000\nAdd: Ending finished goods inventory 600\nLess: Beginning finished goods inventory (900)\nRequired production 3,700","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q366","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) A company is in the process of developing its operating budget\nfor the coming year. Given below are selected data regarding\nthe company’s two products, laminated putter heads and\nforged putter heads, that are sold through specialty golf shops.\nPutter Heads\nForged Laminated\nRaw materials:\nSteel 2 lbs. at $5/lb. 1 lb. at $5/lb.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nCopper None 1 lb. at $15/lb.\nDirect labor 1/4 hr. at $20/hr. 1 hr. at $22/hr.\nExpected sales (units) 8,200 2,000\nSelling price per unit $30 $80\nEnding inventory target (units) 100 60\nBeginning inventory (units) 300 60\nBeginning inventory (cost) $5,250 $3,120\nManufacturing overhead is applied to units produced on the\nbasis of direct labor hours. Variable manufacturing overhead is\nprojected to be $25,000, and fixed manufacturing overhead is\nexpected to be $15,000.\nThe estimated cost to produce one unit of the laminated\nputter head is","options":["$42","$46","$52","$62\nFact Pattern: Tidwell Corporation sells a single product for $20 per\nunit. All sales are on account, with 60% collected in the month of sale\nand 40% collected in the following month. A partial schedule of cash\ncollections for January through March of the coming year reveals the\nfollowing receipts for the period:\nCash Receipts\nJanuary February March\nDecember receivables $32,000\nFrom January sales 54,000 $36,000\nFrom February sales 66,000 $44,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nOther information includes the following:\n Inventories are maintained at 30% of the following month’s\nsales.\n Tidwell desires to keep a minimum cash balance of $15,000.\nTotal payments in January are expected to be $106,500,\nwhich excludes $12,000 of depreciation expense. Any\nrequired borrowings are in multiples of $1,000.\n The December 31 balance sheet for the preceding year\nrevealed a cash balance of $24,900."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The costs of direct materials and direct labor are given.\nDetermining per-unit overhead costs involves computing the\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\ntotal number of direct labor hours projected for the month (for\nboth products).\nForged Laminated\nBudgeted sales 8,200 2,000\nLess: Beginning inventory (300) (60)\nAdd: Ending inventory target 100 60\nBudgeted production 8,000 2,000\nTimes: DL hours × 0.25 × 1.00\nBudgeted DL hours 2,000 2,000\nTotal direct labor hours for the month are therefore 4,000\n(2,000 + 2,000). Per-unit overhead costs can now be\ncalculated:\nVariable overhead costs $25,000\nFixed overhead costs 15,000\nTotal projected overhead $40,000\nDivided by: DL hours ÷ 4,000\nPer-unit overhead cost $ 10.00\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nTotal per-unit cost for a laminated putter head can now be\ncalculated as follows:\nDirect materials -- steel $ 5\nDirect materials -- copper 15\nDirect labor 22\nManufacturing overhead 10\nTotal per-unit cost $52","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q367","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) March sales total $150,000. The number of units Tidwell must\npurchase in February is","options":["3,850","4,900","6,100","7,750"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Projected sales for February total $110,000 ($66,000 +\n$44,000). Unit sales are thus expected to be 5,500 ($110,000\n÷ $20). March unit sales are projected at 7,500 ($150,000 ÷\n$20). February purchases can thus be calculated as follows:\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nNeeded for February sales 5,500\nAdd: 30% of March sales 2,250\nTotal units needed 7,750\nLess: Beginning inventory (1,650)\nFebruary purchases 6,100","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q368","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) A company manufactures electronic components used in\nautomobile manufacturing. Each component uses two raw\nmaterials, G and C. Standard usage of the two materials\nrequired to produce one finished electronic component, as well\nas the current inventory, are shown below.\nStandard\nMaterial Per Unit Price Current Inventory\nG 2.0 pounds $15/lb. 5,000 pounds\nC 1.5 pounds $10/lb. 7,500 pounds\nThe company forecasts sales of 20,000 components for the\nnext two production periods. Company policy dictates that 25%\nof the raw materials needed to produce the next period’s\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nprojected sales be maintained in ending direct materials\ninventory.\nBased on this information, the budgeted direct material\npurchases for the coming period would be\nG C","options":["$450,000 $450,000","$675,000 $300,000","$675,000 $400,000","$825,000 $450,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The company’s budgeted direct materials purchases can be\ncalculated as follows:\nG C\nProjected unit sales 20,000 20,000\nTimes: Pounds per unit × 2.0 × 1.5\nInputs required 40,000 30,000\nAdd: Required ending inventory 10,000 7,500\nLess: Beginning inventory (5,000) (7,500)\nUnit purchases 45,000 30,000\nTimes: Unit cost × $15 × $10\nCash purchases $675,000 $300,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q369","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) A company is a maker of men’s slacks (pants). The company\nwould like to maintain 20,000 yards of fabric in ending\ninventory. The beginning fabric inventory is expected to\ncontain 25,000 yards. The expected yards of fabric needed for\nsales is 90,000. Compute the yards of fabric that the company\nneeds to purchase.","options":["85,000","90,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","95,000","135,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The company’s fabric purchase requirements can be\ncalculated as follows:\nNeeded for sales 90,000\nAdd: Ending inventory 20,000\nLess: Beginning inventory (25,000)\nTotal purchases 85,000","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q370","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) A gift shop maintains a 35% gross profit percentage on sales\nand carries an ending inventory balance each month sufficient\nto support 30% of the next month’s expected sales.\nAnticipated sales for the fourth quarter are as follows:\nOctober $42,000\nNovember 58,000\nDecember 74,000\nWhat amount of goods should the gift shop plan to purchase\nduring the month of November?","options":["$40,820","$51,220","$52,130","$62,800"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The gift shop’s sales and ending inventory requirements at\ncost can be calculated as follows:\nProjected Cost Sales\nSales % At Cost\nOctober: $42,000 × 65% = $27,300\nNovember: 58,000 × 65% = 37,700\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nDecember: 74,000 × 65% = 48,100\nNovember’s purchase requirements can now be determined:\nNeeded for sales $37,700\nAdd: Required ending inventory ($48,100 × 30%) 14,430\nLess: Projected beginning inventory ($37,700 ×\n30%)\n(11,310)\nTotal purchases $40,820","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q371","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) In preparing the direct material purchases budget for next\nquarter, the plant controller has the following information\navailable:\nBudgeted unit sales 2,000\nPounds of materials per unit 4\nCost of materials per pound $3\nPounds of materials on hand 400\nFinished units on hand 250\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nTarget ending units inventory 325\nTarget ending inventory of pounds of materials 800\nHow many pounds of materials must be purchased?","options":["2,475","7,900","8,700","9,300"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The calculation of pounds of raw material needed to be\npurchased is as follows:\nBudgeted unit sales 2,000\nAdd: Required finished goods ending inventory 325\nLess: Beginning finished goods inventory (250)\nRequired production 2,075\nTimes: Pounds per unit × 4\nPounds required for production 8,300\nAdd: Target ending raw materials inventory 800\nLess: Beginning raw materials inventory (400)\nPounds to be purchased 8,700\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q372","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":32,"question":"32) An entity estimates that it will sell 200,000 dolls during the\ncoming year. The beginning inventory is 12,000 dolls; the\ntarget ending inventory is 15,000 dolls. Each doll requires two\nshoes, which are purchased from an outside supplier. The\nbeginning inventory of shoes is 20,000; the target ending\ninventory is 18,000 shoes. The number of shoes that should\nbe purchased during the year is","options":["396,000","398,000","402,000","404,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The calculation of the number of shoes that must be\npurchased is as follows:\nShoes needed for dolls to be sold 400,000\nLess: Beginning inventory of shoes on dolls (24,000)\nAdd: Target ending inventory of shoes on dolls 30,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nLess: Beginning inventory of shoes (20,000)\nAdd: Target ending inventory of shoes 18,000\nShoes to be purchased 404,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q373","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":33,"question":"33) A corporation has a policy of maintaining inventory at 15% of\nthe next month’s forecast sales. The cost of the corporation’s\nmerchandise averages 60% of the selling price. The inventory\nbalance as of May 31 is $63,000, and the forecast dollar sales\nfor the last seven months of the year are as follows:\nJune $700,000\nJuly 600,000\nAugust 650,000\nSeptember 800,000\nOctober 850,000\nNovember 900,000\nDecember 840,000\nWhat is the budgeted dollar amount of the corporation’s\npurchases for July?","options":["$355,500\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","$360,000","$364,500","$399,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nFact Pattern: Polk Retailers is developing cash and other budget\ninformation for July, August, and September. At June 30, Polk had\ncash of $6,600, accounts receivable of $524,000, inventories of\n$371,280, and accounts payable of $159,666. The budget is to be\nbased on the following assumptions:\nSales\nEach month’s sales are billed on the last day of the month. Customers\nare allowed a 2% discount if payment is made within 10 days after the\nbilling date. Receivables are booked gross. 65% of the billings are\ncollected within the discount period, 20% are collected by the end of\nthe month, 10% are collected by the end of the second month, and\n5% prove uncollectible.\nPurchases\n60% of all purchases of materials and selling, general, and\nadministrative expenses are paid in the month purchased and the\nremainder in the following month. Each month’s ending inventory in\nunits is equal to 120% of the next month’s units of sales. The cost of\neach unit of inventory is $25. Selling, general, and administrative\nexpenses, of which $3,000 is depreciation, are equal to 20% of the\ncurrent month’s sales.\nActual and projected sales are as follows:\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nDollars Units\nMay $424,000 10,600\nJune 436,000 10,900\nJuly 428,000 10,700\nAugust 408,000 10,200\nSeptember 432,000 10,800\nOctober 440,000 11,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The corporation’s budgeted purchases for July are calculated\nas follows:\nProjected sales at retail $600,000\nTimes: Cost percentage × 60%\nProjected sales at cost: $360,000\nProjected sales at cost $360,000\nTarget percentage × 15%\nLess: 15% of estimated sales (54,000)\nFollowing month projected sales $650,000\nCost percentage × 60%\nFollowing month sales at cost $390,000\nTarget percentage × 15%\nAdd: 15% of following month sales 58,500\nProjected purchases at cost $364,500","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q374","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":34,"question":"34) Polk’s budgeted purchases for July and August are","options":["$236,000 and $242,500","$247,500 and $260,000","$252,500 and $273,000","$275,000 and $292,500"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Each month’s units of EI equal 120% of the next month’s units\nof sales. Thus, the purchases each month are equal to the EI,\nplus the sales of the current month, minus the BI.\nJuly August\nSales 10,700 10,200\nEnding inventory 12,240 12,960\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nTotal needed 22,940 23,160\nLess: Beginning inventory (12,840) (12,240)\nPurchases 10,100 10,920\nUnit price × $25 × $25\nPurchase cost $252,500 $273,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q375","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":35,"question":"35) The budgeted number of units of inventory Polk will purchase\nduring September is","options":["13,200","10,560","10,800","11,040"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The budgeted units of inventory to be purchased during\nSeptember equal the EI of September (120% of the unit sales\nin October), plus the sales units in September, minus the BI of\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nSeptember. BI of September is equal to 120% of September\nsales.\nEnding inventory (11,000 × 120%) 13,200\nSeptember sales 10,800\nTotal needed 24,000\nLess: Beginning inventory (10,800 × 120%) (12,960)\nUnits to be purchased (September) 11,040","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q376","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":36,"question":"36) A firm desires a finished goods ending inventory equal to 25%\nof the following month’s budgeted sales. January sales are\nbudgeted at 10,000 units and February at 12,000 units. Each\nunit requires 2 pounds of Material X, which costs $4 per\npound. The company has a just-in-time system and materials\nare delivered daily just prior to use, so no raw materials\ninventories are maintained. Materials are paid for in the month\nfollowing purchase. The January 1 finished goods inventory is\n2,500 units. In February, what amount should the company\nexpect to pay as a cash outflow for raw materials?","options":["$21,000","$40,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","$42,000","$84,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The firm will need 10,000 units for January sales plus 3,000\n(12,000 × 25%) for ending inventory. The production quota for\nJanuary is therefore 10,500 (13,000 needed – 2,500 beginning\ninventory). Each unit requires 2 pounds of materials, or 21,000\npounds. At $4 per pound, the materials will cost $84,000,\nwhich will be paid in February.\nFact Pattern:\nRokat Corporation is a manufacturer of\ntables sold to schools, restaurants,\nhotels, and other institutions. The table\ntops are manufactured by Rokat, but the\ntable legs are purchased from an outside\nsupplier. The Assembly Department\ntakes a manufactured table top and\nattaches the four purchased table legs. It\ntakes 20 minutes of labor to assemble a\ntable. The company follows a policy of\nRokat’s sales budget in\nunits for the next quarter\nis as follows:\nJuly 2,300\nAugust 2,500\nSeptember 2,100\nRokat’s ending inventories\nin units for June 30 are\nFinished\ngoods\n1,900\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nproducing enough tables to ensure that\n40% of next month’s sales are in the\nfinished goods inventory. Rokat also\npurchases sufficient direct materials\ninventory to ensure that direct materials\ninventory is 60% of the following month’s\nscheduled production.\nDirect\nmaterials\n(legs)\n4,000","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q377","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":37,"question":"37) The number of tables to be produced by Rokat during August\nis","options":["1,400 tables.","2,340 tables.","1,440 tables.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","1,900 tables."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The company will need 2,500 finished units for August sales.\nIn addition, 840 units (2,100 September unit sales × 40%)\nshould be in inventory at the end of August. August sales plus\nthe desired ending inventory equals 3,340 units. Of these\nunits, 40% of August’s sales, or 1,000 units, should be\navailable from beginning inventory. Consequently, production\nin August should be 2,340 units.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q378","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":38,"question":"38) Assume Rokat’s required production for August and\nSeptember is 1,600 and 1,800 units, respectively, and the July\n31 direct materials inventory is 4,200 units. The number of\ntable legs to be purchased in August is","options":["6,520 legs.","9,400 legs.","2,200 legs.","6,400 legs.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some of the legs needed for August’s production are already\nincluded in the 4,200 units in direct materials. Legs needed for\nAugust are 6,400 (1,600 × 4), but there are already 4,200 legs\nin the July 31 direct materials inventory. Therefore, 2,200 legs\nshould be purchased for August production. Rokat needs\n4,320 legs (1,800 × 4 × 60%) for September production. Thus,\nthe total amount of legs to be purchased is 6,520 (2,200 +\n4,320).","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q379","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":39,"question":"39) A firm is in the process of preparing budgets for the upcoming\nperiod. The firm manufactures wooden toy trucks. Sales vary\nsignificantly, peaking in the holiday season. Management\nwishes to maintain an ending inventory equal to 20% of the\nnext month’s sales. Following is the firm’s anticipated sales for\nthe upcoming period.\nOctober 2,000 toy trucks\nNovember 2,500 toy trucks\nDecember 8,500 toy trucks\nJanuary 1,200 toy trucks\nFebruary 850 toy trucks\nHow many trucks should the firm manufacture in November?","options":["1,300","1,700","3,700","4,200"],"answer":"C","explanation":"November truck production can be calculated using the\nfollowing formula: Beginning inventory + Production – Sales =\nEnding inventory. Beginning inventory can be determined by\nfinding the last month’s ending inventory. October ending\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\ninventory is equal to 20% of November sales, which is 500\n(2,500 × 20%). November ending inventory is equal to 20% of\nDecember sales, which is 1,700 (8,500 × 20%). Using the\nabove formula, we can determine that November truck\nproduction is 3,700 (1,700 + 2,500 – 500).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q380","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":40,"question":"40) A corporation sells a single product for $20 per unit. All sales\nare on account, with 60% collected in the month of sale and\n40% collected in the following month. A partial schedule of\ncash collections for January through March of the coming year\nreveals the following receipts for the period.\nCash Receipts\nJanuary February March\nDecember receivables $32,000\nFrom January sales 54,000 $36,000\nFrom February sales 66,000 $44,000\nOther information includes the following:\n Inventories are maintained at 30% of the following\nmonth’s sales.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\n Assume that March sales total is $150,000\nThe number of units to be purchased in February is","options":["3,850 units.","4,900 units.","6,100 units.","7,750 units."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The units to be purchased in February total 6,100 units as\nshown below.\nFebruary unit\nsales\n= ($66,000 + $44,000) ÷ $20 = 5,500\nunits\nMarch unit sales = $150,000 ÷ $20 = 7,500\nunits\nFebruary purchase = (5,500 × 0.7) + (7,500 ×\n0.3)\n= 6,100\nunits","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q381","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":41,"question":"41) There are various budgets within the master budget cycle. One\nof these budgets is the production budget. Which one of the\nfollowing best describes the production budget?","options":["It summarizes all discretionary costs.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","It includes required direct labor hours.","It includes required material purchases.","It is calculated from the desired ending inventory and the sales\nforecast."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A production budget is based on sales forecasts, in units, with\nadjustments for beginning and ending inventories. It is used to\nplan when items will be produced. After the production budget\nhas been completed, it is used to prepare materials\npurchases, direct labor, and factory overhead budgets.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q382","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":42,"question":"42) A company uses a comprehensive budgeting system in\nplanning its annual operations. Which of the\nfollowing best describes the information needed to determine\nthe budgeted cost of circuit boards to be purchased for use in\nbuilding its laptop computer? Assume one circuit board is used\nin each laptop.","options":["Begin with budgeted laptop sales in units, add the desired\nending inventory of circuit boards, deduct the expected\nbeginning inventory of circuit boards, and multiply the resulting\namount by the budgeted purchase cost per circuit board.\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials","Begin with budgeted laptop sales in units, deduct the desired\nending inventory of circuit boards, add the expected beginning\ninventory of circuit boards, and multiply the resulting amount\nby the purchase cost per circuit board.","Begin with budgeted laptop production in units, deduct the\ndesired ending inventory of circuit boards, add the expected\nbeginning inventory of circuit boards, and multiply the resulting\namount by the purchase cost per circuit board.","Begin with budgeted laptop production in units, add the\ndesired ending inventory of circuit boards, deduct the expected\nbeginning inventory of circuit boards, and multiply the resulting\namount by the budgeted purchase cost per circuit board."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Since each laptop requires exactly one circuit board, the\nbeginning figure of the calculation equals the number of\nfinished products to be produced:\nUnits needed for production X,XXX\nAdd: Desired ending inventory XXX\nLess: Beginning inventory (XXX)\nRaw materials to be purchased X,XXX\n\nStatements | Subunit 2: Operating Budget Calculations --\nProduction and Direct Materials\nTimes: Per-unit purchase price × $ X.XX\nTotal raw materials cost $X,XXX","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q383","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":43,"question":"43) Which one of the following would cause a company’s\nproduction budget to decrease?","options":["An increase in direct labor costs per unit.","A decrease in units produced per direct labor hour.","A decrease in required ending inventory.","An increase in beginning direct labor inventory.\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\n14: (175) Budgeting -- Calculations and Pro Forma\nFinancial Statements\n3: (19) Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Production budgets are based on sales in units plus or minus\ndesired inventory buildup or reduction. Products to be\nmanufactured = desired ending inventory + budgeted sales\nunits – beginning inventory. Therefore, a decrease in required\nending inventory will cause the production budget to decrease.","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q384","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) A company pays out sales commissions to its sales team in\nthe month the company receives cash for payment. These\ncommissions equal 5% of total (monthly) cash inflows as a\nresult of sales. The company has budgeted sales of $300,000\nfor August, $400,000 for September, and $200,000 for\nOctober. Approximately half of all sales are on credit, and the\nother half are cash sales. Experience indicates that 70% of the\nbudgeted credit sales will be collected in the month following\nthe sale, 20% the month after that, and 10% of the sales will\nbe uncollectible. Based on this information, what should be the\ntotal amount of sales commissions paid out by the company in\nthe month of October?","options":["$8,500","$13,500","$17,000","$22,000\nFact Pattern:\nMountain Corporation manufactures cabinets\nbut outsources the handles. Eight handles are\nneeded for a cabinet, with assembly requiring\n30 minutes of direct labor per unit. Ending\nfinished goods inventory is planned to consist\nof 50% of projected unit sales for the next\nmonth, and ending handles inventory is\nplanned to be 80% of the requirement for the\nMountain’s\nprojected unit sales:\nOctober 4,600\nNovember 5,000\nDecember 4,200\nJanuary 6,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nnext month’s projected unit output of finished\ngoods.\nMountain’s ending\ninventories in units\nat September 30:\nFinished\ngoods\n3,800\nHandles 16,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cash sales for the company for the month of October are\nbudgeted at $100,000 (half of $200,000 overall sales).\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nProjections for collections of credit sales in August indicate\nthat 20% will be cash inflows in October, or ($150,000 × 20%)\n= $30,000. Projections for collections of credit sales in\nSeptember indicate that 70% will be cash inflows in October,\nor ($200,000 × 70%) = $140,000. Therefore, total cash inflows\nprojected for the month of October equal $100,000 + $30,000\n+ $140,000 = $270,000. Because sales commissions are set\nat 5% of monthly cash inflows, the sales commissions for\nOctober equal ($270,000 × 5%) = $13,500.","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q385","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) Given that a full-time employee works 160 hours per month, no\novertime is allowed, and part-time employees may be used,\nhow many full-time equivalent employees does Mountain need\nto assemble the output of finished units in November?","options":["14.375","28.75","15.625","31.25\nFact Pattern:\nRokat Corporation is a manufacturer of\ntables sold to schools, restaurants, hotels,\nand other institutions. The table tops are\nmanufactured by Rokat, but the table legs\nare purchased from an outside supplier.\nThe Assembly Department takes a\nmanufactured table top and attaches the\nfour purchased table legs. It takes 20\nminutes of labor to assemble a table. The\ncompany follows a policy of producing\nenough tables to ensure that 40% of next\nmonth’s sales are in the finished goods\ninventory. Rokat also purchases sufficient\ndirect materials inventory to ensure that\nRokat’s sales budget in\nunits for the next\nquarter is as follows:\nJuly 2,300\nAugust 2,500\nSeptember 2,100\nRokat’s ending\ninventories in units for\nJune 30 are\nFinished\ngoods\n1,900\nDirect\nmaterials\n(legs)\n4,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\ndirect materials inventory is 60% of the\nfollowing month’s scheduled production."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Projected unit sales for November equal 5,000, of which 2,500\n(5,000 × 50%) should come from October’s ending inventory.\nEnding inventory for November should be 2,100 units (4,200\nunit sales projected for December × 50%). Accordingly, output\nfor November should be 4,600 units (5,000 – 2,500 + 2,100),\nand these units will require 2,300 direct labor hours for\nassembly [4,600 units × (30 minutes ÷ 60 minutes per hour)].\nThe full-time equivalent number of employees needed to\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nassemble 4,600 units is therefore 14.375 (2,300 hours ÷\n160 hours per full-time employee).","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q386","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) Assume that Rokat Corporation will produce 1,800 units in the\nmonth of September. How many employees will be required\nfor the Assembly Department? (Fractional employees are\nacceptable since employees can be hired on a part-time basis.\nAssume a 40-hour week and a 4-week month.)","options":["15 employees.","3.75 employees.","60 employees.","600 employees."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Each unit requires 20 minutes of assembly time, or 1/3 of an\nhour. The assembly of 1,800 units will therefore require 600\nhours of labor (1,800 × 1/3). At 40 hours per week for 4\nweeks, each employee will work 160 hours during the month.\nThus, 3.75 employees (600 ÷ 160) are needed.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q387","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) Which one of the following statements regarding selling and\nadministrative budgets is most accurate?\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others","options":["Selling and administrative budgets are usually optional.","Selling and administrative budgets are fixed in nature.","Selling and administrative budgets are difficult to allocate by\nmonth and are best presented as one number for the entire\nyear.","Selling and administrative budgets need to be detailed in order\nthat the key assumptions can be better understood."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sales and administrative budgets are prepared after the sales\nbudget. Like the other budgets, they constitute prospective\ninformation based on the preparer’s assumptions about\nconditions expected to exist and actions expected to be taken.\nFact Pattern:\nJordan Auto has\ndeveloped the following\nproduction plan:\nMonth Units\nJanuary 10,000\nFebruary 8,000\nMarch 9,000\nEach unit contains 3 pounds of direct\nmaterials. The desired direct materials\nending inventory each month is 120% of the\nnext month’s production, plus 500 pounds.\n(The beginning inventory meets this\nrequirement.) Jordan has developed the\nfollowing direct labor standards for\nproduction of these units:\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nApril 12,000 Department 1 Department 2\nHours per unit 2.0 0.5\nHourly rate $7.25 $12.00","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q388","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) Jordan Auto’s total budgeted direct labor dollars for February\nusage should be","options":["$164,000","$174,250","$184,500","$221,400"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The standard unit labor cost is $20.50 [($7.25 × 2 hours in\nDepartment 1) + ($12 × .5 hour in Department 2)], so the total\nbudgeted direct labor dollars for February equal $164,000\n(8,000 units × $20.50).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q389","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) For the month of December, a bottling company expects to sell\n12,500 cases of water at $24.80 per case and 33,100 cases of\ncola at $32.00 per case. Sales personnel receive 6%\ncommission on each case of water and 8% commission on\neach case of cola. In order to receive a commission on a\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nproduct, the sales personnel team must meet the individual\nproduct revenue quota. The sales quota for water is $500,000,\nand the sales quota for cola is $1,000,000. The sales\ncommission that should be budgeted for December is","options":["$4,736","$82,152","$84,736","$103,336"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The sale of 12,500 cases of water at $24.80 per case\nproduces revenue of $310,000, an amount that does not\nqualify for commissions. The sale of 33,100 cases of cola at\n$32 per case produces revenue of $1,059,200. This amount is\ngreater than the minimum and therefore qualifies for a\ncommission of $84,736 ($1,059,200 × 8%). This calculation\nassumes that commissions are paid on all sales if the revenue\nquota is met.","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q390","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) Each unit of Product XK-46 requires three direct labor hours.\nEmployee benefit costs are treated as direct labor costs. Data\non direct labor are\nNumber of direct employees 25\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nWeekly productive hours per employee 35\nEstimated weekly wages per employee $245\nEmployee benefits (related to weekly wages) 25%\nThe standard direct labor cost per unit of Product XK-46 is","options":["$21.00","$26.25","$29.40","$36.75\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The standard direct labor unit cost equals 3 hours times the\ncost per DLH. This amount is determined by adding employee\nbenefits to weekly wages and dividing by hours per week.\nWeekly wages $245.00\nAdd: Benefits ($245 × .25) 61.25\nWeekly total compensation $306.25\nDivided by: Hours/week ÷ 35\nCost per DL hour $ 8.75\nTimes: DL hours per unit × 3\nUnit DL cost $ 26.25","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q391","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) For the month of June, a company expects to sell 12,500\ncases of small cherries at $25 per case and 33,000 cases of\nlarge cherries at $32 per case. Sales personnel receive a 6%\ncommission on each case of small cherries and an 8%\ncommission on each case of large cherries. To receive a\ncommission on a product, the sales personnel team must meet\nthe individual product revenue quota. The sales quotas for\nsmall cherries and large cherries are $500,000 and $1 million,\nrespectively. What are the sales commissions budgeted for\nJune?","options":["$109,440","$84,480","$82,110","$4,480\nFact Pattern: Scarborough Corporation manufactures and sells two\nproducts, Thingone and Thingtwo. Scarborough’s budget department\ngathered the following data to project sales and budget requirements:\nProjected Sales\nProduct Units Price\nThingone 60,000 $ 70\nThingtwo 40,000 100\nProjected Inventories -- in units\nExpected Desired\nProduct January 1 December 31\nThingone 20,000 25,000\nThingtwo 8,000 9,000\nTo produce one unit of Thingone and Thingtwo, the following raw\nmaterials are used:\nRaw Material Unit Thingone Thingtwo\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nA lb. 4 5\nB lb. 2 3\nC each 0 1\nProjected data for the year with respect to raw materials are as\nfollows:\nAnticipated Expected Desired\nRaw Purchase Inventories Inventories\nMaterial Price 1/1 12/31\nA $8 32,000 lb. 36,000 lb.\nB 5 29,000 lb. 32,000 lb.\nC 3 6,000 each 7,000 each\nProjected direct labor requirements and rates are as follows:\nThingone -- 2 hours per unit at $3 per hour\nThingtwo -- 3 hours per unit at $4 per hour\nOverhead is applied at the rate of $2 per direct labor hour."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The sale of 12,500 cases of small cherries at $25 per case\nproduces revenue of $312,500. Because this amount is below\nthe $500,000 minimum, no commissions should be budgeted\nfor small cherries. The sale of 33,000 cases of large cherries\nat $32 per case produces revenue of $1,056,000. This amount\nis greater than the $1 million minimum and therefore qualifies\nfor a commission of $84,480 ($1,056,000 × 8%). The problem\ndid not state that a commission would be paid only on\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\namounts exceeding the minimum. Hence, all sales qualify\nonce the minimum is reached.","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q392","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) What is Scarborough’s direct labor budget in dollars?\nThingone Thingtwo","options":["$390,000 $369,000","$520,000 $492,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others","$390,000 $492,000","$492,000 $390,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The direct labor budget in dollars is the estimated unit\nproduction times the hours per unit times the expected rate,\nwhich gives the direct labor dollars for each product.\nProjected\nProduction Hours Total\n(units) per Unit Hours Rate Total\nThingon\ne\n65,000 2 130,000 $\n3\n$390,00\n0\nThingtwo 41,000 3 123,000 4 492,000","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q393","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) What is Scarborough’s budgeted finished goods inventory for\nThingtwo in dollars?","options":["$108,000","$306,000","$522,000","$684,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The budgeted FG inventory includes DM, DL, and O/H\nassociated with Thingtwo times the desired inventory.\nRaw materials:\nA (5 pounds × $8) $40\nB (3 pounds × $5) 15\nC (1 each × $3) 3 $ 58\nDirect labor (3 hours × $4) 12\nOverhead (3 hours × $2) 6\nPer unit cost $ 76\nUnits in EI × 9,000\nEI value $684,000","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q394","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) The following direct labor information relates to the\nmanufacture of televisions.\nNumber of workers 60\nNumber of product hours per week, per worker 40\nHours required to make 1 unit 3\nWeekly wages per worker $600\nEmployee benefits treated as direct labor costs 20% of wages\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nWhat is the standard direct labor cost per unit?","options":["$54","$36","$45","$18"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The hourly wage per worker is $15.00 ($600 ÷ 40 hours). The\ndirect labor cost per hour is $18.00 [$15.00 × (1.0 + benefits\nequal to 20% of wages)]. Consequently, the standard direct\nlabor cost per unit is $54 ($18 × 3 hours).","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q395","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) A firm budgeted the following amounts for the coming year:\nBeginning inventory, finished goods $ 10,000\nCost of goods sold 400,000\nDirect material used in production 100,000\nEnding inventory, finished goods 25,000\nBeginning and ending work-in-process inventory 0\nOverhead is estimated to be two times the amount of direct\nlabor dollars. The amount that should be budgeted for direct\nlabor for the coming year is","options":["$315,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others","$210,000","$157,500","$105,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The firm’s cost of goods manufactured can be calculated as\nfollows:\nCost of goods sold $400,000\nAdd: Ending finished goods inventory 25,000\nGoods available for sale $425,000\nLess: Beg. finished goods inventory (10,000)\nCost of goods manufactured $415,000\nThe $415,000 is composed of materials ($100,000), direct\nlabor, and overhead. Therefore, labor plus overhead equals\n$315,000. Since overhead is two times labor, the calculation\nwould be DL + 2DL = $315,000, or DL = $105,000.","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q396","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) A company’s selling and administrative costs for the month of\nAugust, when it sold 20,000 units, were as follows.\nCosts\nPer Unit Total\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nVariable costs $18.60 $372,000\nStep costs 4.25 85,000\nFixed costs 8.80 176,000\nTotal selling and\nadministrative costs $31.65 $633,000\nThe variable costs represent sales commissions paid at the\nrate of 6.2% of sales. The step costs depend on the number of\nsalespersons employed by the company. In August there were\n17 persons on the sales force. However, two members have\ntaken early retirement effective August 31. It is anticipated that\nthese positions will remain vacant for several months. Total\nfixed costs are unchanged within a relevant range of 15,000 to\n30,000 units per month. The company is planning a sales price\ncut of 10%, which it expects will increase sales volume to\n24,000 units per month. If the company implements the sales\nprice reduction, the total budgeted selling and administrative\ncosts for the month of September would be","options":["$652,760","$679,760","$714,960","$759,600"],"answer":"A","explanation":"If the $372,000 of variable cost is 6.2% of sales, then sales\nwere $6,000,000. Current unit price must be $300 ($6,000,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\ntotal sales ÷ 20,000 units). A 10% price cut will lower it to $270\n($300 × 90%), leading to total projected sales after the price\ncut of $6,480,000 (24,000 × $270). The sales commissions on\nthat level of sales will be $401,760 ($6,480,000 × 6.2%).\nBecause two salespersons will be gone in September, the\nstep costs will be reduced to $75,000 [$85,000 × (15 ÷ 17)].\nFixed costs remain at $176,000. Total anticipated S&A costs\nfor September are therefore $652,760 ($401,760 + $75,000 +\n$176,000).","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q397","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) A manufacturer of men’s t-shirts had the following information\nfor last year.\nNumber of shirts sold and produced 125,000\nSale price per shirt $40\nDirect manufacturing $10/shirt\nSetup cost $100/setup hour\nSetup hours 10,000\nShipping costs $200/shipment\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nNumber of shipments 4,000\nAdministrative cost $8/shirt\nThe company’s operating profit last year was","options":["$950,000","$1,950,000","$2,750,000","$3,750,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Total income is $5,000,000. Total expenses are $4,050,000\n[($10 × 125,000) + ($100 × 10,000) + ($200 × 4,000) + ($8 ×\n125,000)].","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q398","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) A retailer uses flexible budgeting as a planning tool. The\ncompany’s original budget for the upcoming year is shown\nbelow.\nSales $90,000,000\nCost of goods sold 36,000,000\nAdministrative expenses (all fixed) 21,000,000\nAdvertising expense 9,000,000\nSales commissions 6,750,000\nOther marketing expenses (all fixed) 9,250,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nOperating income $ 8,000,000\nThe manager of the retailer’s Marketing Department believes\nsales volume will increase by 10% if the advertising budget is\nincreased by $5,000,000. Should the retailer approve the\nincreased advertising request?","options":["Yes, because the increase in sales is $4,000,000 greater than\nthe increase in advertising costs.","No, because advertising is 10% of sales, so the maximum\nincrease in sales would be $900,000.","Yes, because operating income would increase by $400,000.","No, because operating income would decrease by $275,000."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Advertising expense increases by $5,000,000, and sales\nvolume would increase by 10%. When sales volume\nincreases, sales, cost of goods sold, and sales commission all\nincrease. Sales would increase by $9,000,000, cost of goods\nsold would increase by $3,600,000, and sales commission\nwould increase by $675,000. The net change from increasing\nadvertising would be a decrease of $275,000 ($9,000,000 –\n$5,000,000 – $3,600,000 – $675,000).\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q399","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) A company receives an order from a major customer on the\nfirst day of every quarter and has to produce an extra 10% to\ncover for product defects. Each finished product requires two\npounds of direct material and three direct labor hours. Finished\ngoods inventory as of January 1 is 300 units, which can be\nused to fulfill the order. The orders for the first quarter are\nshown below.\nJanuary 2,000 units\nFebruary 2,300 units\nMarch 2,500 units\nBudgeted direct labor hours for the first quarter are","options":["19,500","20,400","21,450","22,440"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The demand for the first quarter is 6,800 units (2,000 + 2,300\n+ 2,500), but since there are 300 units in beginning inventory,\nonly 6,500 will need to be produced. However, since an\nadditional 10% must be manufactured to allow for product\ndefects, 650 units must be added to the production schedule,\nfor a total of 7,150 units. At 3 hours of direct labor per unit,\nbudgeted direct labor hours will equal 21,450.\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q400","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) The budget for a product is as follows.\nSales $10,000,000\nMaterials and labor 4,000,000\nFixed manufacturing overhead 1,500,000\nSales commissions 500,000\nAdvertising (fixed) 200,000\nOther marketing costs (fixed) 800,000\nAllocated administrative costs 2,000,000\nIncome $ 1,000,000\nThe budgeted contribution margin for this product is","options":["$5,500,000","$5,300,000","$4,500,000","$3,000,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Contribution margin is the net sales revenue minus all variable\ncosts (both manufacturing and S&A). Materials and labor and\nsales commissions are the only variable costs listed in the\nbudget. Thus, the contribution margin is equal to $5,500,000\n($10,000,000 Sales – 4,000,000 Materials and labor –\n500,000 Sales commissions).\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q401","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) A company that manufactures furniture is establishing its\nbudget for the upcoming year. All of the following items would\nappear in the overhead budget except for the","options":["Overtime paid to the workers who perform production\nscheduling.","Cost of glue used to secure the attachment of the legs to the\ntables.","Fringe benefits paid to the production supervisor.","Freight charges paid for the delivery of raw materials to the\ncompany."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Freight charges for raw materials delivery are included in the\ncosts of direct materials.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q402","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) A company is developing its annual budget. The company’s\nproducts are made to order and shipped immediately. The\ncontroller has information on the following accounts:\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\nRaw materials\nDirect labor\nAdvertising\nResearch and development (R&D)\nTrade discounts\nFactory indirect labor\nFactory overhead\nSelling costs\nWhich of the above accounts should not be included in the\ncost of goods sold budget?","options":["Advertising, R&D, and selling costs, only.","R&D and trade discounts, only.","Advertising and selling costs, only.","Advertising, R&D, trade discounts, and selling costs, only."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The cost of goods sold budget shows all of the manufacturing\ncosts of producing the company’s product, including raw\nmaterials, direct labor, and factory overhead (including factory\nindirect labor). These are all costs incurred in the factory\nduring the production process. Cost of goods sold does not\n\nStatements | Subunit 3: Operating Budget Calculations -- Others\ninclude advertising, research and development costs, trade\ndiscounts, or other types of selling costs.\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n14: (175) Budgeting -- Calculations and Pro Forma\nFinancial Statements\n4: (40) The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q403","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) Which one of the following is the best characteristic\nconcerning the capital budget? The capital budget is a(n)","options":["Plan to ensure that there are sufficient funds available for the\noperating needs of the company.","Exercise that sets the long-range goals of the company\nincluding the consideration of external influences caused by\nothers in the market.","Plan that results in the cash requirements during the operating\ncycle.","Plan that assesses the long-term needs of the company for\nplant and equipment purchases."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Capital budgeting is the process of planning expenditures for\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nlong-lived assets. It involves choosing among investment\nproposals using a ranking procedure. Evaluations are based\non various measures involving the IRR.","sectionId":"14.3"},{"id":"v2-su14-q404","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) A company has developed the following sales projections for\nthe calendar year.\nMay $100,000\nJune 120,000\nJuly 140,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nAugust 160,000\nSeptember 150,000\nOctober 130,000\nNormal cash collection experience has been that 50% of sales\nare collected during the month of sale and 45% in the month\nfollowing sale. The remaining 5% of sales is never collected.\nThe company’s budgeted cash collections for the third\ncalendar quarter are","options":["$427,500","$422,500","$414,000","$450,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"If 50% of sales are collected in the month of sale and 45% in\nthe next month, with the balance uncollectible, collections\nduring the third quarter will be based on sales during June,\nJuly, August, and September. As calculated below, total\nbudgeted collections are $414,000.\nJune: $120,000 × 45% = $ 54,000\nJuly: 140,000 × (50% + 45%) = 133,000\nAugust: 160,000 × (50% + 45%) = 152,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nSeptember: 150,000 × 50% = 75,000\nTotal $414,000","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q405","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) A company budgeted sales on account of $120,000 for July,\n$211,000 for August, and $198,000 for September. Collection\nexperience indicates that 60% of the budgeted sales will be\ncollected the month after the sale, 36% will be collected the\nsecond month, and 4% will be uncollectible. The cash receipts\nfrom accounts receivable that should be budgeted for\nSeptember would be","options":["$169,800","$147,960","$197,880","$194,760\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nFact Pattern:\nHistorically, Pine Hill Wood Products has\nhad no significant bad debt experience with\nits customers. Cash sales have accounted\nfor 10% of total sales, and payments for\ncredit sales have been received as follows:\n40% of credit sales in the month of the sale\n30% of credit sales in the first subsequent\nmonth\n25% of credit sales in the second\nsubsequent month\n5% of credit sales in the third subsequent\nmonth\nThe forecast for both\ncash and credit sales is\nas follows:\nMonth Sales\nJanuary $95,000\nFebruary 65,000\nMarch 70,000\nApril 80,000\nMay 85,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The budgeted cash collections for September are $169,800\n[($120,000 July sales × 36%) + ($211,000 August sales ×\n60%)].","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q406","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) What is the forecasted cash inflow for Pine Hill Wood Products\nfor May?","options":["$70,875","$76,500","$79,375\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$83,650"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The cash inflows for May will come from May cash sales of\n$8,500 ($85,000 × 10%), May credit sales of $30,600\n($85,000 × 90% × 40%), April sales of $21,600 ($80,000 ×\n30% × 90%), March sales of $15,750 ($70,000 × 25% × 90%),\nand February sales of $2,925 ($65,000 × 5% × 90%). The\ntotal is $79,375.","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q407","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) Due to deteriorating economic conditions, Pine Hill Wood\nProducts has now decided that its cash forecast should include\na bad debt adjustment of 2% of credit sales, beginning with\nsales for the month of April. The 5% collection in the fourth\nmonth should be reduced to reflect the bad debt. Because of\nthis policy change, the total expected cash inflow in April\nrelated to sales made in April will","options":["Be unchanged.","Decrease by $1,260.00.","Decrease by $1,440.00.","Decrease by $1,530.00.\nFact Pattern: Birch Corporation has the following historical pattern\non its credit sales:\n 70% collected in month of sale\n 15% collected in the first month after sale\n 10% collected in the second month after sale\n 4% collected in the third month after sale\n 1% uncollectible\nThe sales on open account have been budgeted for the first 6 months\nof the year as follows:\nJanuary $ 70,000\nFebruary 90,000\nMarch 100,000\nApril 120,000\nMay 100,000\nJune 90,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The estimated collections in July related to April credit sales\nwill be reduced by $1,440. Estimated collections in the month\nof the sale will be unchanged.\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q408","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) Birch’s estimated total cash collections during April from\naccounts receivable are","options":["$84,000","$110,800","$118,800","$108,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The estimated April collections are $110,800.\n70% of April sales of $120,000 = $ 84,000\n15% of March sales of $100,000 = 15,000\n10% of February sales of $90,000 = 9,000\n4% of January sales of $70,000 = 2,800\nTotal collections $110,800","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q409","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) Birch’s estimated total cash collections during the second\ncalendar quarter from sales made on open account during the\nsecond calendar quarter are","options":["$262,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$288,800","$306,900","$310,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The second calendar quarter consists of April, May, and June.\nFor April’s sales of $120,000, collections should be 95% (70%\n+ 15% + 10%), or $114,000. For May’s sales of $100,000,\ncollections should be 85% (70% + 15%), or $85,000. For\nJune’s sales of $90,000, collections should be 70%, or\n$63,000. The quarterly total is $262,000 ($114,000 + $85,000\n+ $63,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q410","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) A cash budget is being prepared for the next year. Sales are\nexpected to be $100,000 in January, $200,000 in February,\n$300,000 in March, and $100,000 in April. Approximately half\nof all sales are cash sales, and the other half are on credit.\nExperience indicates that 70% of the credit sales will be\ncollected in the month following the sale, 20% the month after\nthat, and 10% in the third month after the sale. What are the\nbudgeted collections for April?","options":["$130,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$180,000","$260,000","$360,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Collections from April cash sales will be half of total sales, or\n$50,000. From January’s $50,000 of credit sales, collections\nshould be 10%, or $5,000. From February’s $100,000 of credit\nsales, collections should be 20%, or $20,000. From March’s\n$150,000 of credit sales, collections will be 70%, or $105,000.\nThus, total collections will amount to $180,000.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q411","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) A company budgeted sales of $220,000 for June, $200,000 for\nJuly, $280,000 for August, $264,000 for September, $244,000\nfor October, and $300,000 for November. Approximately 75%\nof sales are on credit; the remainder are cash sales. Collection\nexperience indicates that 60% of the budgeted credit sales will\nbe collected the month after the sale, 36% the second month,\nand 4% will be uncollectible. Which month has the highest\nbudgeted cash receipts?","options":["August.","September.\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","October.","November."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Credit sales for June and July are $165,000 ($220,000 × 75%)\nand $150,000 ($200,000 × 75%), respectively. Cash sales for\nAugust are $70,000 [$280,000 × (1.0 – .75)]. The cash\ncollections during August should therefore be $219,400.\nJune credit: $165,000 × .36 = $ 59,400\nJuly credit: 150,000 × .60 = 90,000\nAugust cash: = 70,000\n$219,400\nCredit sales for July and August are $150,000 ($200,000 ×\n75%) and $210,000 ($280,000 × 75%), respectively. Cash\nsales for September are $66,000 [$264,000 × (1.0 – .75)]. The\ncash collections during September should therefore be\n$246,000.\nJuly credit: $150,000 × .36 = $ 54,000\nAugust credit: 210,000 × .60 = 126,000\nSeptember cash: = 66,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n$246,000\nCredit sales for August and September are $210,000\n($280,000 × 75%) and $198,000 ($264,000 × 75%),\nrespectively. Cash sales for October are $61,000 [$244,000 ×\n(1.0 – .75)]. The cash collections during October should\ntherefore be $255,400.\nAugust credit: $210,000 × .36 = $ 75,600\nSeptember credit: 198,000 × .60 = 118,800\nOctober cash: = 61,000\n$255,400\nCredit sales for September and October are $198,000\n($264,000 × 75%) and $183,000 ($244,000 × 75%),\nrespectively. Cash sales for November are $75,000 [$300,000\n× (1.0 – .75)]. The cash collections during November should\ntherefore be $256,080.\nSeptember credit: $198,000 × .36 = $ 71,280\nOctober credit: 183,000 × .60 = 109,800\nNovember cash: = 75,000\n$256,080\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nAmong these four calculations, November yields the highest\ncash collections.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q412","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) A firm budgeted sales on account of $150,000 for July,\n$210,000 for August, and $198,000 for September. Collection\nexperience indicates that 60% of the budgeted sales will be\ncollected the month after the sale, 36% the second month, and\n4% will be uncollectible. The cash receipts from accounts\nreceivable that should be budgeted for September equal","options":["$180,000","$165,600","$194,400","$198,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The budgeted cash collections for September equal $180,000.\nJuly: $150,000 × .36 = $ 54,000\nAugust: 210,000 × .60 = 126,000\n$180,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q413","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) A company budgeted sales of $200,000 for July, $280,000 for\nAugust, $198,000 for September and $200,000 for October.\nApproximately 75% of sales are on credit; the remainder are\ncash sales. Collection experience indicates that 60% of the\nbudgeted credit sales will be collected the month after the sale,\n36% will be collected the second month, and 4% will be\nuncollectible. The cash receipts budgeted for October equal","options":["$164,700","$200,000","$214,700","$244,400"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Credit sales for August and September are $210,000\n($280,000 × 75%) and $148,500 ($198,000 × 75%),\nrespectively. Cash sales for October are $50,000 [$200,000 ×\n(1.0 –.75)]. The cash collections during October should\ntherefore be $214,700.\nAugust: $210,000 × .36 = $ 75,600\nSeptember: 148,500 × .60 = 89,100\nOctober: 50,000 × 1.00 = 50,000\n$214,700\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q414","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) The cash budget for March includes the following information.\nThis information concerns its accounts receivable:\nEstimated credit sales for March $300,000\nActual credit sales for February $250,000\nEstimated collections in March for credit sales in\nMarch\n30%\nEstimated collections in March for credit sales in\nFebruary\n60%\nEstimated collections in March for credit sales prior\nto February\n$15,000\nEstimated write-offs in March for uncollectible credit\nsales\n$7,000\nEstimated provision for bad debts in March for credit\nsales in March $8,000\nDetermine the estimated cash receipts from accounts\nreceivable collections in March.","options":["$240,000","$247,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$248,000","$255,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The estimated cash receipts from accounts receivable\ncollections in March are equal to the estimated amount to be\ncollected on credit sales in March plus the estimated amount\nto be collected in March on credit sales in previous months.\nEstimated credit receipts in March for March\n($300,000 × 30%)\n$ 90,000\nEstimated credit receipts in March for February\n($250,000 × 60%)\n150,000\nEstimated collections for credit sales prior to\nFebruary\n15,000\nEstimated cash to be collected in March $255,000\nThe estimated write-offs in March for uncollectible credit sales\nand the estimated provision for bad debts in March for credit\nsales in March are not used in the calculation because the\npercentages of estimated collections are assumed to be\nbased on the gross credit sales amounts.\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nFact Pattern: Polk Retailers is developing cash and other budget\ninformation for July, August, and September. At June 30, Polk had\ncash of $6,600, accounts receivable of $524,000, inventories of\n$371,280, and accounts payable of $159,666. The budget is to be\nbased on the following assumptions:\nSales\nEach month’s sales are billed on the last day of the month. Customers\nare allowed a 2% discount if payment is made within 10 days after the\nbilling date. Receivables are booked gross. 65% of the billings are\ncollected within the discount period, 20% are collected by the end of\nthe month, 10% are collected by the end of the second month, and\n5% prove uncollectible.\nPurchases\n60% of all purchases of materials and selling, general, and\nadministrative expenses are paid in the month purchased and the\nremainder in the following month. Each month’s ending inventory in\nunits is equal to 120% of the next month’s units of sales. The cost of\neach unit of inventory is $25. Selling, general, and administrative\nexpenses, of which $3,000 is depreciation, are equal to 20% of the\ncurrent month’s sales.\nActual and projected sales are as follows:\nDollars Units\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nMay $424,000 10,600\nJune 436,000 10,900\nJuly 428,000 10,700\nAugust 408,000 10,200\nSeptember 432,000 10,800\nOctober 440,000 11,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q415","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) Polk’s budgeted cash collections during July are","options":["$407,332","$413,000","$417,675","$422,338\nFact Pattern:\nSuper Drive, a computer disk storage and back-up company, uses\naccrual accounting. The company’s Statement of Financial Position\nfor the year ended November 30 is as follows:\nSuper Drive\nStatement of Financial Position\nas of November 30\nAssets\nCash $ 52,00\n0\nAccounts receivable, net 150,000\nInventory 315,000\nProperty, plant, and equipm\nent\n1,000,000\nLiabilities and Stockholders’\nEquity\nAccounts payable $ 175,\n000\nCommon stock 900,000\nRetained earnings 442,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nTotal assets $1,517,00\n0\nTotal liabilities and\nstockholders’\nequity\n$1,517,\n000\nAdditional information regarding Super Drive’s operations include\nthe following:\n Sales are budgeted at $520,000 for December and\n$500,000 for January of the next year.\n Collections are expected to be 60% in the month of\nsale and 40% in the month following the sale.\n Eighty percent of the disk drive components are\npurchased in the month prior to the month of sale, and 20%\nare purchased in the month of sale. Purchased components\nare 40% of the cost of goods sold.\n Payment for the components is made in the month\nfollowing the purchase.\n Cost of goods sold is 80% of sales."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Each month’s cash collections contains three elements: (1)\n65% of the billings are collected with a 2% discount, (2) 20%\nare collected at the end of the month, and (3) 10% are\ncollected by the end of the second month.\nFor the month of July, items 1 and 2 are sales from June. Item\n3 equals sales from May.\n$436,000 (June) × .65 × .98 $277,732\n$436,000 (June) × .20 87,200\n$424,000 (May) × .10 42,400\nCash collections for July $407,332\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q416","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) Super Drive’s budgeted cash collections for the month of\nDecember are","options":["$208,000","$520,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$402,000","$462,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Collections are expected to be 60% in the month of sale and\n40% in the month following the sale. Thus, collections in\nDecember consist of the $150,000 of receivables at November\n30, plus 60% of December sales. Total collections are\ntherefore $462,000 [$150,000 + ($520,000 × 60%)].\nFact Pattern: Kelly Company is a retail sporting goods store that\nuses accrual accounting for its records. Facts regarding Kelly’s\noperations are as follows:\n Sales are budgeted at $220,000 for December Year 1 and\n$200,000 for January Year 2.\n Collections are expected to be 60% in the month of sale and\n38% in the month following the sale.\n Gross margin is 25% of sales.\n A total of 80% of the merchandise held for resale is\npurchased in the month prior to the month of sale and 20% is\npurchased in the month of sale. Payment for merchandise is\nmade in the month following the purchase.\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n Other expected monthly expenses to be paid in cash are\n$22,600.\n Annual depreciation is $216,000.\nBelow is Kelly Company’s statement of financial position at\nNovember 30, Year 1.\nAssets\nCash $ 22,000\nAccounts receivable (net of $4,000\nallowance for uncollectible accounts) 76,000\nInventory 132,000\nProperty, plant, and equipment (net of\n$680,000 accumulated depreciation) 870,000\nTotal assets $1,100,000\nLiabilities and Stockholders’ Equity\nAccounts payable $ 162,000\nCommon stock 800,000\nRetained earnings 138,000\nTotal liabilities and stockholders’ equity $1,100,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q417","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) Kelly’s budgeted cash collections for December Year 1 are","options":["$208,000","$132,000","$203,600","$212,000\nFact Pattern: Esplanade Company has the following historical\npattern for its credit sales:\n 70% collected in month of sale\n 15% collected in the first month after sale\n 10% collected in the second month after sale\n 4% collected in the third month after sale\n 1% uncollectible\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nThe sales on open account have been budgeted for the last 6 months\nof the year as shown below:\nJuly $ 60,000\nAugust 70,000\nSeptember 80,000\nOctober 90,000\nNovember 100,000\nDecember 85,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Since collections are 60% of the current month’s sales and\n38% of the previous month’s sales, total collections should be\nAccounts receivable $ 76,000\nDecember sales of $220,000 × 60% 132,000\nBudgeted cash collections $208,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q418","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) Esplanade’s estimated total cash collections during October\nfrom accounts receivable are","options":["$63,000","$84,400","$86,700","$21,400"],"answer":"B","explanation":"During October, collections will be received from sales made\nin October, September, August, and July.\nMonth Sales Percentage Collections\nOctober $90,000 × 70% = $63,000\nSeptember 80,000 × 15% = 12,000\nAugust 70,000 × 10% = 7,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nJuly 60,000 × 4% = 2,400\nTotal collections $84,400","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q419","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) Esplanade’s estimated total cash collections during the fourth\ncalendar quarter from sales made on open account during the\nfourth calendar quarter are","options":["$170,500","$275,000","$230,000","$251,400\nFact Pattern: Information pertaining to Noskey Corporation’s sales\nrevenue is presented in the following table:\nNovember December January\nYear 1 Year 1 Year 2\n(Actual) (Budget) (Budget)\nCash sales $ 80,000 $100,000 $ 60,000\nCredit sales 240,000 360,000 180,000\nTotal sales $320,000 $460,000 $240,000\nManagement estimates that 5% of credit sales are uncollectible. Of\nthe credit sales that are collectible, 60% are collected in the month of\nsale and the remainder in the month following the sale. Quantity of\ninventory purchased is equal to next month’s cost of sales, and gross\nprofit margin is 30%. All purchases of inventory are on account; 25%\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nare paid in the month of purchase, and the remainder are paid in the\nmonth following the purchase."],"answer":"C","explanation":"For October sales, collections will be 70% in October, 15% in\nNovember, and 10% in December, a total of 95%. For\nNovember sales, collections will be 70% in November and\n15% in December, a total of 85%. Collections on December\nsales will be 70%.\nMonth Sales Percentage Collections\nOctober $90,000 × 95% = $85,500\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nNovember 100,000 × 85% = 85,000\nDecember 85,000 × 70% = 59,500\nTotal collections $230,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q420","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) Noskey Corporation’s budgeted cash collections in December\nYear 1 from November Year 1 credit sales are","options":["$84,000","$136,800","$228,000","$91,200"],"answer":"D","explanation":"November credit sales were $240,000. Of this amount,\n$12,000 ($240,000 × 5%) will likely be written off. Given that\n60% of the remaining credit sales are collected in the month of\nsale, 40% will be collected in the following month. Thus,\nDecember’s expected cash collections are $91,200 [($240,000\n– $12,000) × 40%].","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q421","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) Noskey Corporation’s budgeted total cash receipts in January\nYear 2 are","options":["$240,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$294,000","$299,400","$239,400"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Collections during January will consist of 40% of December’s\ncollectible credit sales, 60% of January’s collectible credit\nsales, and cash sales for January. The January collections of\nDecember sales are expected to be $136,800 ($360,000 ×\n40% × 95%). The January collections of January credit sales\nare expected to be $102,600 ($180,000 × 60% × 95%). Given\nJanuary cash sales of $60,000, total budgeted cash receipts\nfor January are $299,400 ($136,800 + $102,600 + $60,000).","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q422","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) A company budgeted $148,000 sales on account for June,\n$120,000 for July, $211,000 for August, $198,000 for\nSeptember, and $164,000 for October. Collection experience\nindicates that 60% of the budgeted sales will be collected the\nmonth after the sale, 36% will be collected the second month,\nand 4% will be uncollectible. Which month should have the\nlargest amount of cash receipts from accounts receivable\nbudgeted?\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","options":["August.","September.","October.","November."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The budgeted cash collections for October are $194,760\n[($211,000 August sales × 36%) + ($198,000 September sales\n× 60%)].","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q423","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) A company sells products exclusively on account and has\nexperienced the following collection pattern: 60% in the month\nof sale, 25% in the month after sale, and 15% in the second\nmonth after sale. Uncollectible accounts are negligible.\nCustomers who pay in the month of sale are given a 2%\ndiscount. If sales are $220,000 in January, $200,000 in\nFebruary, $280,000 in March, and $260,000 in April, the\naccounts receivable balance on May 1 will be","options":["$107,120","$143,920","$146,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$204,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sales and collections activity can be calculated as follows:\nCollections\nSales January February March April\nJanuary $220,000 $132,000 $ 55,000 $ 33,000\nFebruary 200,000 120,000 50,000 $ 30,000\nMarch 280,000 168,000 70,000\nApril 260,000 156,000\n$960,000 $132,000 $175,000 $251,000 $256,000\nTotal collections for the period were $814,000 ($132,000 +\n$175,000 + $251,000 + 256,000), leaving a balance in\naccounts receivable on May 1 of $146,000 (960,000 –\n$814,000). This total is composed of $104,000 from April\nsales and $42,000 (15%) of March sales.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q424","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) Estimated monthly sales will be as follows:\nJanuary $100,000\nFebruary 150,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nMarch 180,000\nHistorical trends indicate that 40% of sales are collected during\nthe month of sale, 50% are collected in the month following the\nsale, and 10% are collected two months after the sale. The\naccounts receivable balance as of December 31 totals\n$80,000 ($72,000 from December’s sales and $8,000 from\nNovember’s sales). The amount of cash expected to be\ncollected during the month of January is","options":["$76,800","$84,000","$108,000","$133,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The amount collected in January on January sales will be\n$40,000 ($100,000 × 40%). The $72,000 balance in\nreceivables from December sales represents 60% (50% +\n10%) of that month’s total. Since the total December sales\nwere $120,000 ($72,000 ÷ 60%), the amount expected to be\ncollected in January is $60,000 ($120,000 × 50%). The $8,000\nbalance from November represents all the remaining expected\ncollections from that month’s sales. Thus, total January\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\ncollections on receivables are expected to be $108,000\n($40,000 + $60,000 + $8,000).","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q425","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) A corporation anticipates the following sales during the last 6\nmonths of the year:\nJuly $460,000\nAugust 500,000\nSeptember 525,000\nOctober 500,000\nNovember 480,000\nDecember 450,000\n20% of the corporation’s sales are for cash. The balance is\nsubject to the collection pattern shown below.\nPercentage of balance collected in the month of sale 40%\nPercentage of balance collected in the month\nfollowing sale 30%\nPercentage of balance collected in the second month\nfollowing sale 25%\nPercentage of balance uncollectible 5%\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nWhat is the planned net accounts receivable balance as of\nDecember 31?","options":["$279,300","$294,000","$360,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$367,500"],"answer":"B","explanation":"December 31 receivables will consist of portions of credit\nsales from November ($480,000 × 80% = $384,000) and\nDecember ($450,000 × 80% = $360,000). The collections on\nthese sales can be calculated as follows:\nCollections\nCredit\nSales November December January February\nNovembe\nr\n$384,000 $153,600 $115,200 $ 96,000\nDecembe\nr\n360,000 144,000 108,000 $90,000\n$204,000 $90,000\nThe corporation’s December 31 balance in accounts\nreceivable is therefore $294,000 ($204,000 + $90,000).","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q426","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) Projected monthly sales for January, February, March, and\nApril are as follows:\nJanuary $300,000\nFebruary 340,000\nMarch 370,000\nApril 390,000\n The company bills each month’s sales on the last day\nof the month.\n Receivables are booked gross and credit terms of sale\nare 2/10, n/30.\n 50% of the billings are collected within the discount\nperiod, 30% are collected by the end of the month,\n15% are collected by the end of the second month,\nand 5% become uncollectible.\nBudgeted cash collections during April would be","options":["$343,300","$347,000","$349,300","$353,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"April collections will consist of 15% of February’s gross sales\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n($51,000) and both components of March sales, those\ncollected within the discount period ($370,000 × 98% × 50% =\n$181,300), and those collected after it ($370,000 × 30% =\n$111,000). Total April cash collections are therefore $343,300\n($51,000 + $181,300 + $111,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q427","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) A firm carries a large number of different items in its inventory,\ngiving the firm a competitive advantage in its industry. Below is\npart of the firm’s budget for the first quarter of next year.\nSales $855,000\nCost of goods sold 425,000\nRent and salary expenses 375,000\nHistorically, all of the sales are on account and are made\nevenly over the quarter. 5% of all sales are determined to be\nuncollectible and written off. The balance of the receivables is\ncollected in 50 days. This sales and collection experience is\nexpected to continue in the first quarter. The projected balance\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nsheet for the first day of the quarter includes the following\naccount balances.\nCash $ 10,000\nAccounts receivable (net) 450,000\nInventory 900,000\nAccounts payable 800,000\nHow much cash can the firm anticipate collecting in the first\nquarter (based on a 360-day year)?","options":["$811,000","$830,000","$901,250\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$902,500"],"answer":"A","explanation":"At the end of the quarter, receivables will represent 50 days of\nsales, which is about 5/9 of the quarter. The other 4/9 of the\nquarter’s sales will be collected during the quarter. Since bad\ndebts will be 5% of sales, the firm expects to collect $812,250\n($855,000 × 95%). Multiply $812,250 times 4/9 and the\namount to be collected during the quarter is $361,000. Add in\nthe $450,000 of beginning receivables to get total collections\nof $811,000.","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q428","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) A corporation’s budget for the upcoming accounting period\nreveals total sales of $700,000 in April and $750,000 in May.\nThe sales cash collection pattern is\n1. 20% of each month’s sales are cash sales.\n2. 5% of a month’s credit sales are uncollectible.\n3. 70% of a month’s credit sales are collected in the\nmonth of sale.\n4. 25% of a month’s credit sales are collected in the\nmonth following the sale.\nIf the corporation anticipates the cash sale of a piece of old\nequipment in May for $25,000, May’s total budgeted cash\nreceipts would be","options":["$560,000","$702,500","$735,000","$737,500"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cash collections can be calculated as follows:\nCollections\nTotal Credit\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nSales Sales April May\nApril $700,000 $560,000 $392,000 $140,000\nMay 750,000 600,000 420,000\nCash collections on credit sales $392,000 $560,000\nCash sales 140,000 150,000\nProceeds from sale of equipment 25,000\nTotal cash received $532,000 $735,000","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q429","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) A company sells products on account and experiences the\nfollowing collection schedule:\nIn the month of sale 10%\nIn the month after sale 60%\nIn the second month after sale 30%\nAt December 31, the company reports accounts receivable of\n$211,500. Of that amount, $162,000 is due from December\nsales and $49,500 from November sales. The company is\nbudgeting $170,000 of sales for January. If so, what amount of\ncash should be collected in January?\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","options":["$129,050","$174,500","$211,500","$228,500"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The $49,500 of receivables from November sales that are still\noutstanding at December 31 are the last amounts that will be\nreceived from November. The $162,000 in receivables from\nDecember sales that are still outstanding at December 31\ncontain the collections for the month and the second month\nafter sale. Thus, this amount must constitute 90% of total\nsales for December (60% + 30%), meaning total December\nsales were $180,000 ($162,000 ÷ 90%). The portion of this\nthat will be collected in January is $108,000 ($180,000 ×\n60%). Of January’s sales, 10% will be collected in January\n($170,000 × 10% = $17,000). Therefore, total cash collections\nfor January will be $174,500 ($49,500 + $108,000 + $17,000).","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q430","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) Information gathered from the past year reveals the following\ncash collection trends:\n1. 40% of sales are on credit\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n2. 50% of credit sales are collected in month of sale\n3. 30% of credit sales are collected first month after sale\n4. 15% of credit sales are collected second month after\nsale\n5. 5% of credit sales result in bad debts\nGross sales for the last 5 months were as follows:\nJanuary $220,000\nFebruary 240,000\nMarch 250,000\nApril 230,000\nMay 260,000\nSales for June are projected to be $255,000. Based on this\ninformation, the expected cash receipts for March would be","options":["$230,000","$237,400","$242,000","$243,200"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cash receipts can be calculated as follows:\nCollections\nTotal Credit\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nSales Sales January February March\nJanuary $220,000 $88,000 $ 44,000 $ 26,400 $ 13,200\nFebruary 240,000 96,000 48,000 28,800\nMarch 250,000 100,000 50,000\nCollections on credit sales $ 44,000 $ 74,400 $ 92,000\nCollections on cash sales 132,000 144,000 150,000\nTotal cash collections $176,000 $218,400 $242,000","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q431","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) Data regarding the forecast dollar sales for the last 7 months of\nthe year and the projected collection patterns are as follows:\nForecasted sales\nJune $700,000\nJuly 600,000\nAugust 650,000\nSeptember 800,000\nOctober 850,000\nNovember 900,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nDecember 840,000\nTypes of sales\nCash sales 30%\nCredit sales 70%\nCollection pattern on credit sales\n(5% determined to be uncollectible)\nDuring the month of sale 20%\nDuring the first month following the sale 50%\nDuring the second month following the sale 25%\nBudgeted cash receipts from sales and collections on account\nfor September are","options":["$635,000","$684,500","$807,000","$827,000\nFact Pattern:\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nKarmee Company has been\naccumulating operating data in order\nto prepare an annual profit plan.\nDetails regarding Karmee’s sales for\nthe first 6 months of the coming year\nare as follows:\nEstimated\nMonthly Sales\nJanuary $600,0\n00\nFebruar\ny\n650,00\n0\nMarch 700,00\n0\nApril 625,00\n0\nMay 720,00\n0\nJune 800,00\n0\nType of Monthly\nSale\nCash sales 20%\nCredit sales 80%\nCollection Pattern\nfor Credit Sales\nMonth of sale 30\n%\nOne month\nfollowing sale\n40\n%\nSecond month\nfollowing sale\n25\n%\nThe variable operating\nexpenses (other than cost of\ngoods sold) for Karmee are\n10% of sales and are paid\nfor in the month following the\nsale. The annual fixed\noperating expenses are\npresented below. All of these\nare incurred uniformly\nthroughout the year and paid\nmonthly except for insurance\nand property taxes.\nInsurance is paid quarterly in\nJanuary, April, July, and\nOctober. Property taxes are\npaid twice a year in April and\nOctober.\nAnnual Fixed Operating\nCosts\nAdvertising $ 720,00\n0\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nKarmee’s cost of goods sold averages\n40% of the sales value. Karmee’s\nobjective is to maintain a target\ninventory equal to 30% of the next\nmonth’s sales in units. Purchases of\nmerchandise for resale are paid for in\nthe month following the sale.\nDepreciation 420,000\nInsurance 180,000\nProperty\ntaxes\n240,000\nSalaries 1,080,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cash receipts can be calculated as follows:\nCollections\nTota\nl\nCred\nit\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nSale\ns\nSale\ns\nJune July Augus\nt\nSeptemb\ner\nJune $700,0\n00\n$490,0\n00\n$ 98,00\n0\n$245,00\n0\n$122,50\n0\nJuly 600,00\n0\n420,00\n0\n84,000 210,000 $105,000\nAugust 650,00\n0\n455,00\n0\n91,000 227,500\nSeptemb\ner\n800,00\n0\n560,00\n0\n112,000\nCollections on credit\nsales\n$ 98,00\n0\n$329,00\n0\n$423,50\n0\n$444,500\nCollections on cash sales 210,000 180,000 195,000 240,000\nTotal cash collections $308,00\n0\n$509,00\n0\n$618,50\n0\n$684,500","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q432","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) The amount of cash collected in March for Karmee Company\nfrom the sales made during March will be","options":["$140,000","$308,000","$350,000","$636,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cash sales are 20% of monthly sales, credit sales are 80% of\nmonthly sales, and collections on credit sales are 30% in the\nmonth of sale. Consequently, cash collected during a month\nequals 44% [20% + (30% × 80%)] of sales for that month.\nCash collections in March on March sales were therefore\n$308,000 ($700,000 × 44%).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q433","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":32,"question":"32) Karmee Company’s total cash receipts for the month of April\nwill be","options":["$504,000","$629,000","$653,000","$707,400"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cash collected during a month on sales for that month equals\n44% of total sales. Hence, cash receipts in April on April’s\nsales are $275,000 ($625,000 × 44%). April collections on\nMarch credit sales equal $224,000 ($700,000 × 40% × 80%).\nApril collections on February credit sales equal $130,000\n($650,000 × 25% × 80%). Thus, total cash receipts for April\nwere $629,000 ($275,000 + $224,000 + $130,000).","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q434","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":33,"question":"33) A company is in the process of identifying, evaluating, and\nselecting projects that require a large commitment of funds\nand will generate benefits well into the future. The company\nwill look at the budget over the life of the projects and review\nmany different options. This is an example of\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","options":["A rolling budget since it wants information for multiple periods.","A flexible budget as it can prepare the budget using several\noptions.","A capital budget to help with the evaluation and identification.","An activity-based budget to evaluate all activities for each\nproject."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The capital budget concerns financing of major expenditures\nfor long-term assets. Management can use a capital budget to\nrank projects according to their risk and return characteristics,\nwhich is necessary because every organization has finite\nresources.","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q435","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":34,"question":"34) Which one of the following best describes the capital budget?","options":["It ensures that there are sufficient funds available for the\noperating needs of the company.","It sets the long-range goals of the company, including\nconsideration of all available resources.","It results in the cash requirements during the operating cycle.\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","It assesses the long-term needs of the company for plant and\nequipment purchases."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The capital budget assesses the long-term needs of the\ncompany for plant and equipment purchases to plan financing\nof major expenditures for such plant and equipment.","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q436","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":35,"question":"35) A corporation has the opportunity to purchase land adjacent to\nits existing location for $200,000. If purchased, the company\nwould also spend $20,000 to level the property to make it\nusable for building construction. Management believes there is\nan 80% probability it will have enough cash to purchase the\nland outright and a 20% chance it will need to borrow the\n$200,000. The dollar amount the corporation should show on\nits initial capital budget for the purchase of this land is","options":["$220,000","$200,000","$180,000","$160,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The correct dollar amount that the corporation should show on\nits initial capital budget for the purchase of land is $220,000\n($200,000 + $20,000). Both the amount to purchase the land\nand the amount to level the property need to be put in the\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nbudget for the land (both amounts will be capitalized in the\ncost of the land).","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q437","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":36,"question":"36) A firm prepares cash budgets each month, including a forecast\nof monthly cash collections. Collections are expected to be\n65% in the month of sale, 25% in the first month following the\nsale, and 10% in the second month following the sale. Sales\nfor the first 6 months of the year were as follows.\nJanuary $185,000 April $208,000\nFebruary $196,000 May $210,000\nMarch $216,000 June $232,000\nThe total cash collected during the month of May is","options":["$207,900","$208,800","$210,100\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$224,100"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The total cash collected during the month of May is $210,100\n[(65% × $210,000) + (25% × $208,000) + (10% × $216,000)].","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q438","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":37,"question":"37) Sales during the first 3 months of operations are as follows:\nJanuary February March\nCash Sales $100,000 $110,000 $120,000\nSales on account 320,000 335,000 364,000\nTotal sales $420,000 $445,000 $484,000\nCash is collected from credit customers as follows:\nWithin the first 10 days after the month of sale, at a 2%\nearly pay discount 60%\nIn the month after sale, after the discount period 30%\nIn the second month after sale 10%\nWhat will the cash receipts be for the month of March?","options":["$329,480","$449,480\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections","$466,532","$484,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In order to find cash collections for a given month, it is\nnecessary to determine the timing of cash flows. First, the\n$120,000 of cash sales during March will be collected during\nMarch. Second, credit sales from February are to be collected\nin March. 60% of February credit sales will eligible for a 2%\ndiscount. Therefore, February sales collections from the early\npay period will total $196,980 [60% × (98% × $335,000)].\nAdditionally, a further 30% of February sales will be collected\nin March, or $100,500 (30% × $335,000). Finally, 10% of\nJanuary credit sales will be collected in March, or $32,000\n(10% × $320,000). Adding all these amounts together will give\nthe correct answer of $449,480.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q439","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":38,"question":"38) Historically, a freight company has had no significant bad debt\nexperience with its customers. Cash sales have accounted for\n10% of total sales, and payments for credit sales have been\nreceived as follows:\n 40% of credit sales in the month of the sale\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n 30% of credit sales in the first subsequent month\n 25% of credit sales in the second subsequent month\n 5% of credit sales in the third subsequent month\nThe forecast for both cash and credit sales is as follows.\nMonth Sales\nJanuary $95,000\nFebruary 65,000\nMarch 70,000\nApril 80,000\nMay 85,000\nWhat is the forecasted cash inflow for the freight company for\nthe month of May?","options":["$70,875","$78,750","$79,375","$83,850\nFact Pattern: The following information concerns Montero Corp.\nPurchases Sales\nJanuary $42,000 $72,000\nFebruary 48,000 66,000\nMarch 36,000 60,000\nApril 54,000 78,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nCollections from Montero Corp.’s customers are normally 70% in the\nmonth of sale, and 20% and 9%, respectively, in the 2 months\nfollowing the sale. The balance is uncollectible. Montero takes full\nadvantage of the 2% discount allowed on purchases paid for by the\n10th of the following month. Purchases for May are budgeted at\n$60,000, and sales for May are forecasted at $66,000. Cash\ndisbursements for expenses are expected to be $14,400 for the\nmonth of May. Montero’s cash balance at May 1 was $22,000."],"answer":"C","explanation":"In order to calculate the forecasted cash inflow for May, the\nsales for each month must be separated between cash and\ncredit. The facts state that 10% of total sales each month are\ncash. For May, this is $8,500 ($85,000 × 10%). Then, the\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\npercentage of each month’s credit sales collected in May must\nbe calculated. These two steps are done as follows:\nCash Credit\nAmount collected in May from credit\nsales\nJanuary $9,500 $85,500 $0\nFebruary 6,500 58,500 5% × $58,500 = $2,925\nMarch 7,000 63,000 25% × $63,000 = $15,750\nApril 8,000 72,000 30% × $72,000 = $21,600\nMay 8,500 76,500 40% × $76,500 = $30,600\nThus, the total amount of cash collected in May is equal to\n$79,375 ($2,925 + $15,750 + $21,600 + $30,600 + $8,500).","sectionId":"14.4"},{"id":"v2-su14-q440","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":39,"question":"39) What are Montero’s expected cash collections during May?","options":["$46,200","$61,800","$66,000","$67,200"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The expected cash collections during any month equal 9% of\nthe sales of two months before, 20% of the sales of one month\nbefore, and 70% of the current month’s sales.\nMonth Sales % Expected Collections\nMarch $60,000 9 $ 5,400\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\nApril 78,000 20 15,600\nMay 66,000 70 46,200\nMay collections $67,200","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q441","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":40,"question":"40) Shown below is a forecast of sales for the first 4 months of the\nyear (all amounts are in thousands of dollars).\nJanuary February March April\nCash sales $ 15 $ 24 $18 $14\nSales on credit 100 120 90 70\nOn average, 50% of credit sales are paid for in the month of\nsale, 30% in the month following the sale, and the remainder is\npaid 2 months after the month of sale. Assuming there are no\nbad debts, the expected cash inflow for March is","options":["$138,000","$122,000","$119,000","$108,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\n14: (175) Budgeting -- Calculations and Pro Forma\nFinancial Statements\n5: (34) Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cash inflows for March would consist of 50% of March credit\nsales ($90 × 50% = $45), plus 30% of February credit sales\n\nStatements | Subunit 4: The Capital Budget and Projecting Cash\nCollections\n($120 × 30% = $36), plus 20% of January credit sales ($100 ×\n20% = $20), plus cash sales for March of $18. Consequently,\ntotal collections equal $119,000.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q442","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) Which one of the following items would have to be included for\na company preparing a schedule of cash receipts and\ndisbursements for calendar Year 1?","options":["A purchase order issued in December Year 1 for items to be\ndelivered in February Year 2.","Dividends declared in November Year 1 to be paid in January\nYear 2 to shareholders of record as of December Year 1.","The amount of uncollectible customer accounts for Year 1.","The borrowing of funds from a bank on a note payable taken\nout in June Year 1 with an agreement to pay the principal and\ninterest in June Year 2."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A schedule of cash receipts and disbursements (cash budget)\nshould include all cash inflows and outflows during the period\nwithout regard to the accrual accounting treatment of the\ntransactions. Thus, it should include all checks written and all\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nsources of cash, including borrowings. A borrowing from a\nbank in June Year 1 should appear as a cash receipt for\nYear 1.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q443","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) A company is in the process of projecting its cash position at\nthe end of the second quarter. Shown below is pertinent\ninformation from the company’s records.\nCash balance at end of 1st quarter $ 36,000\nCash collections from customers for 2nd quarter 1,300,000\nAccounts payable at end of 1st quarter 100,000\nAccounts payable at end of 2nd quarter 75,000\nAll 2nd quarter costs and expenses (accrual basis) 1,200,000\nDepreciation (accrued expense included above) 60,000\nPurchases of equipment (for cash) 50,000\nGain on sale of asset (for cash) 5,000\nNet book value of asset sold 35,000\nRepayment of notes payable 66,000\nFrom the data above, determine the company’s projected cash\nbalance at the end of the second quarter.","options":["$0","$25,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","$60,000","$95,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The company’s records show that the costs and expenses are\nrecorded using the accrual basis, not that all the expenses are\naccrued expenses.\nThe change in the company’s cash balance can be calculated\nas follows:\nBeginning cash balance $ 36,000\nAdd: Cash collections 1,300,000\nLess: Net change in accounts payable (25,000)\nLess: Accrual-basis costs and expenses (1,200,000)\nAdd: Depreciation expense (noncash) 60,000\nLess: Payment for equipment (50,000)\nAdd: Net cash received from asset sale 40,000\nLess: Retirement of notes payable (66,000)\nEnding cash balance $ 95,000\nNote that cash is generally paid for expenses even in accrual-\nbasis accounting. The company’s records state that\ndepreciation is included in the $1,200,000 of accrual basis\ncosts and expenses. Thus, it is safe to assume that this is the\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nonly noncash expense. Since it is a noncash expense,\ndepreciation will be added back in when calculating the ending\ncash balance.\nFact Pattern:\nKarmee Company has been\naccumulating operating data in order\nto prepare an annual profit plan.\nDetails regarding Karmee’s sales for\nthe first 6 months of the coming year\nare as follows:\nEstimated\nMonthly Sales\nJanuary $600,0\n00\nFebruar\ny\n650,00\n0\nMarch 700,00\n0\nApril 625,00\n0\nType of Monthly\nSale\nCash sales 20%\nCredit sales 80%\nCollection Pattern\nfor Credit Sales\nMonth of sale 30\n%\nOne month\nfollowing sale\n40\n%\nThe variable operating\nexpenses (other than cost of\ngoods sold) for Karmee are\n10% of sales and are paid\nfor in the month following the\nsale. The annual fixed\noperating expenses are\npresented below. All of these\nare incurred uniformly\nthroughout the year and paid\nmonthly except for insurance\nand property taxes.\nInsurance is paid quarterly in\nJanuary, April, July, and\nOctober. Property taxes are\npaid twice a year in April and\nOctober.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nMay 720,00\n0\nJune 800,00\n0\nSecond month\nfollowing sale\n25\n%\nKarmee’s cost of goods sold averages\n40% of the sales value. Karmee’s\nobjective is to maintain a target\ninventory equal to 30% of the next\nmonth’s sales in units. Purchases of\nmerchandise for resale are paid for in\nthe month following the sale.\nAnnual Fixed Operating\nCosts\nAdvertising $ 720,00\n0\nDepreciation 420,000\nInsurance 180,000\nProperty\ntaxes\n240,000\nSalaries 1,080,000","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q444","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) The total cash disbursements that Karmee Company will make\nfor the operating expenses (expenses other than the cost of\ngoods sold) during the month of April will be","options":["$255,000","$290,000","$385,000","$420,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cash disbursements for variable operating expenses in April\n(excluding cost of goods sold) equal $70,000 ($700,000 March\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nsales × 10%). Cash disbursements for fixed operating\nexpenses (excluding depreciation, a noncash expense)\ninclude advertising ($720,000 ÷ 12 = $60,000), salaries\n($1,080,000 ÷ 12 = $90,000), insurance ($180,000 ÷ 4 =\n$45,000), and property taxes ($240,000 ÷ 2 = $120,000).\nHence, cash payments for April operating expenses are\n$385,000 ($70,000 + $60,000 + $90,000 + $45,000 +\n$120,000).","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q445","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) The purchase of merchandise that Karmee Company will need\nto make during February will be","options":["$254,000","$260,000","$266,000","$338,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Purchases equal cost of goods sold, plus ending inventory,\nminus beginning inventory. Estimated cost of goods sold for\nFebruary equals $260,000 ($650,000 sales × 40%). Ending\ninventory is given as 30% of sales in units. Stated at cost, this\namount equals $84,000 ($700,000 March sales × 30% ×\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\n40%). Furthermore, beginning inventory is $78,000 ($260,000\nCOGS for February × 30%). Thus, purchases equal $266,000\n($260,000 + $84,000 – $78,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q446","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) In preparing its cash budget for April, the following projections\nwere made:\nSales $4,000,000\nGross margin (based on sales) 25%\nDecrease in inventories 160,000\nDecrease in accounts payable\nfor inventories 275,000\nFor April, the estimated cash disbursements for inventories\nwere","options":["$3,275,000","$3,115,000","$2,840,000","$2,565,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The cost of goods sold was $3,000,000 [$4,000,000 sales ×\n(1.0 – 25% gross margin)]. Purchases equal cost of goods\nsold adjusted for the change in inventories. A decrease in\ninventories signifies that purchases were less than cost of\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\ngoods sold. Thus, purchases for April were $2,840,000\n($3,000,000 COGS – $160,000 decrease in inventories). A\ndecrease in payables related to inventories indicates that cash\ndisbursements exceeded purchases. Accordingly, the cash\noutflow for inventories was $3,115,000 ($2,840,000 +\n$275,000 decrease in accounts payable).","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q447","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) A company has just completed its prospective financial\nstatements for the coming year. Relevant information is\nsummarized below:\nProjected net income $100,000\nAnticipated capital expenditures 50,000\nIncrease in working capital 25,000\nDepreciation expense 15,000\nFrom the information provided above, the increase in the cash\naccount for the coming year will be","options":["$25,000","$40,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","$90,000","$160,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The change in cash can be calculated as follows:\nAccrual-basis net income $100,000\nLess: Capital outlays (50,000)\nLess: Increase in working capital (25,000)\nAdd: Depreciation expense (noncash) 15,000\nIncrease in cash $ 40,000","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q448","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) A company has budgeted its operations for August. No change\nin the inventory level during the month is planned. Selected\ndata based on estimated amounts are as follows:\nNet loss $(120,000)\nIncrease in accounts payable 48,000\nDepreciation expense 42,000\nDecrease in gross amounts of trade account\nreceivables 72,000\nPurchase of equipment on 90-day credit terms 18,000\nProvision for estimated warranty liability 12,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nWhat is the expected change in the cash position during\nAugust?","options":["$18,000 decrease.","$30,000 decrease.","$36,000 increase.","$54,000 increase."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The net change in expected cash receipts and disbursements\nis determined by adjusting the net loss. The purchase of\nequipment on credit does not affect cash or the net loss.\nDepreciation expense and the accrual of an estimated\nwarranty liability are noncash expenses that are added back to\nthe net loss. The net loss should also be adjusted for the\ndifference between cost of sales (included in the determination\nof the net loss) and cash paid to suppliers. This adjustment\nhas two parts: (1) The difference between cost of sales and\npurchases equals the change in inventory, and (2) purchases\nmust have exceeded cash paid to suppliers because accounts\npayable increased. Given that inventory is not expected to\nchange, cost of sales exceeds the cash to be paid to suppliers\nby the amount of the increase in accounts payable. The\nincrease must be added back to the net loss. A decrease in\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\naccounts receivable means that cash collections exceeded\nsales. Accordingly, this decrease is also added back to the net\nloss. The expected increase in cash position is $54,000\n[$(120,000) net loss + $42,000 depreciation + $12,000\nwarranty liability + $48,000 increase in accounts payable +\n$72,000 decrease in accounts receivable].","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q449","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) A master budget was prepared based on the following\nprojections:\nSales $2,400,000\nDecrease in inventories 60,000\nDecrease in accounts payable 100,000\nGross margin 40%\nEstimated cash disbursements for inventories are","options":["$920,000","$1,000,000","$1,400,000","$1,480,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Projected cost of sales is 60% of $2,400,000 of sales, or\n$1,440,000. Projected purchases is the $1,440,000 cost of\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nsales less $60,000 projected decrease in inventory which is\n$1,380,000. Projected cash payments is the projected\npurchases of $1,380,000 plus the $100,000 projected\ndecrease in accounts payable, which is $1,480,000.\nFact Pattern: The following information concerns Montero Corp.\nPurchases Sales\nJanuary $42,000 $72,000\nFebruary 48,000 66,000\nMarch 36,000 60,000\nApril 54,000 78,000\nCollections from Montero Corp.’s customers are normally 70% in the\nmonth of sale, and 20% and 9%, respectively, in the 2 months\nfollowing the sale. The balance is uncollectible. Montero takes full\nadvantage of the 2% discount allowed on purchases paid for by the\n10th of the following month. Purchases for May are budgeted at\n$60,000, and sales for May are forecasted at $66,000. Cash\ndisbursements for expenses are expected to be $14,400 for the\nmonth of May. Montero’s cash balance at May 1 was $22,000.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q450","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) What are Montero’s expected cash disbursements for May?\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","options":["$14,400","$52,920","$67,320","$68,400"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The expected cash disbursements for any month equal the\nprevious month’s purchases minus the 2% discount, plus any\ncash disbursements for expenses in the current period.\nApril purchases $54,000\nLess: 2% cash discount (1,080)\nNet purchases $52,920\nCash expenses 14,400\nTotal cash disbursements for May $67,320","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q451","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) What was Montero’s cash balance on April 1, assuming cash\ndisbursements for expenses increased 20% from April to May?","options":["$72,540","$22,000","$(3,260)\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","$(2,540)\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nFact Pattern: Polk Retailers is developing cash and other budget\ninformation for July, August, and September. At June 30, Polk had\ncash of $6,600, accounts receivable of $524,000, inventories of\n$371,280, and accounts payable of $159,666. The budget is to be\nbased on the following assumptions:\nSales\nEach month’s sales are billed on the last day of the month. Customers\nare allowed a 2% discount if payment is made within 10 days after the\nbilling date. Receivables are booked gross. 65% of the billings are\ncollected within the discount period, 20% are collected by the end of\nthe month, 10% are collected by the end of the second month, and\n5% prove uncollectible.\nPurchases\n60% of all purchases of materials and selling, general, and\nadministrative expenses are paid in the month purchased and the\nremainder in the following month. Each month’s ending inventory in\nunits is equal to 120% of the next month’s units of sales. The cost of\neach unit of inventory is $25. Selling, general, and administrative\nexpenses, of which $3,000 is depreciation, are equal to 20% of the\ncurrent month’s sales.\nActual and projected sales are as follows:\nDollars Units\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nMay $424,000 10,600\nJune 436,000 10,900\nJuly 428,000 10,700\nAugust 408,000 10,200\nSeptember 432,000 10,800\nOctober 440,000 11,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The solution is to work backward from the $22,000 cash\nbalance on May 1 by deducting collections and adding\ndisbursements for April. The collections for April were:\nMonth Sales % Expected Collections\nApril $78,000 70 $54,600\nMarch 60,000 20 12,000\nFebruary 66,000 9 5,940\nTotal $72,540\nThe disbursements for April were:\nMarch purchases $36,000\nLess: 2% cash discount (720)\nNet Purchases $35,280\nAdd: Cash expenses ($14,400 ÷ 120%) 12,000\nTotal disbursements for April $47,280\nIf collections exceeded disbursements by $25,260 ($72,540 –\n$47,280), but the ending cash balance was only $22,000, the\nbeginning balance must have been $(3,260) [$22,000 –\n$25,260 net collections].","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q452","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) Polk’s budgeted cash disbursements during August are","options":["$297,306","$272,518","$262,300","$345,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Budgeted cash disbursements during August are affected by\nthe accounts payable remaining at July 31 and by the cash\ndisbursements made in August. The latter include 40% of July\npurchases and expenses and 60% of August purchases and\nexpenses. Depreciation expense of $3,000 is a noncash\nexpenditure and should be deducted from the selling, general\nand administrative expenses (SG&A) for each month. SG&A\nexpenses equal 20% of the current month’s sales. Cash\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\ndisbursements in August for purchases are $264,800\n[($252,500 July purchases × 40%) + ($273,000 × 60%)].\nPurchases are calculated based on the stated needs of Polk.\nIn the fact pattern, it states that ending inventory is 120% of\nthe next month’s sales. Purchases = Required ending\ninventory + Current month’s sales – Beginning inventory. For\nJuly, you would calculate [(120% × 10,200 units sold in\nAugust) + 10,700 units sold in July – 12,840 beginning\ninventory] to arrive at 10,100 units purchased for $252,500\n($25/unit). For August, you would calculate [(120% × 10,800\nunits sold in September) + 10,200 units sold in August –\n12,240 beginning inventory for August] to arrive at 10,920\nunits purchased for $273,000 ($25/unit).\nCash disbursements for other expenses are\nAugust: [($408,000 × 20%) – $3,000] × 60% = $47,160\nJuly: [($428,000 × 20%) – $3,000] × 40% = $33,040\nTherefore, total cash disbursements during August are\n$345,000 ($264,800 + $47,160 + $33,040).\nFact Pattern: Information pertaining to Noskey Corporation’s sales\nrevenue is presented in the following table:\nNovember December January\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nYear 1 Year 1 Year 2\n(Actual) (Budget) (Budget)\nCash sales $ 80,000 $100,000 $ 60,000\nCredit sales 240,000 360,000 180,000\nTotal sales $320,000 $460,000 $240,000\nManagement estimates that 5% of credit sales are uncollectible. Of\nthe credit sales that are collectible, 60% are collected in the month of\nsale and the remainder in the month following the sale. Quantity of\ninventory purchased is equal to next month’s cost of sales, and gross\nprofit margin is 30%. All purchases of inventory are on account; 25%\nare paid in the month of purchase, and the remainder are paid in the\nmonth following the purchase.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q453","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) Noskey Corporation’s budgeted total cash payments in\nDecember Year 1 for inventory purchases are","options":["$405,000","$283,500","$220,500","$168,000\nFact Pattern: Cooper Company’s management team is preparing a\ncash budget for the coming quarter. The following budgeted\ninformation is under review:\nJanuary February March\nRevenue $700,000 $800,000 $500,000\nInventory purchases 350,000 425,000 225,000\nOther expenses 150,000 175,000 175,000\nThe company expects to collect 40% of its monthly sales in the month\nof sale and 60% in the following month. 50% of inventory purchases\nare paid in the month of purchase and the other 50% in the following\nmonth. All payments for other expenses are made in the month\nincurred.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The December inventory payments include 75% of November\npurchases plus 25% of December purchases. Given a gross\nmargin of 30%, cost must be 70% of sales. November\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\npurchases are therefore $322,000 ($460,000 December sales\n× 70%), and the December outlay for November purchases is\n$241,500 ($322,000 × 75%). Purchases during December are\n$168,000 ($240,000 January sales × 70%), and the December\noutlay for December purchases is $42,000 ($168,000 × 25%),\na total cash outlay of $283,500.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q454","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) Cooper forecasts the following account balances at the\nbeginning of the quarter.\nCash $100,000\nAccounts receivable 300,000\nAccounts payable (inventory) 500,000\nGiven the above information, the projected change in cash\nduring the coming quarter will be","options":["$412,500","$300,000","$112,500","$0"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cooper’s collections for the quarter can be calculated as\nfollows:\nCollections\nSales January February March\nJanuary $700,000 $280,000 $420,000\nFebruary 800,000 320,000 $480,000\nMarch 500,000 200,000\n$280,000 $740,000 $680,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nTotal collections are therefore $1,700,000 ($280,000 +\n$740,000 + $680,000). The disbursements for inventory can\nlikewise be calculated as follows:\nDisbursements\nInventory\nPurchases January February March\nJanuary $350,000 $175,000 $175,000\nFebruary 425,000 212,500 $212,500\nMarch 225,000 112,500\n$175,000 $387,500 $325,000\nTotal inventory disbursements are therefore $887,500\n($175,000 + $387,500 + $325,000). Disbursements for other\nexpenses total $500,000 ($150,000 + $175,000 + $175,000).\nThe net change in Cooper’s balance of cash for the quarter\ncan now be calculated:\nCollections on sales $1,700,000\nDisbursements for inventory (887,500)\nDisbursements for other expenses (500,000)\nBeginning accounts receivable 300,000\nBeginning accounts payable (500,000)\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nChange in cash $ 112,500","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q455","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) An entity is preparing a cash forecast based on the following\ninformation:\n Monthly sales: December, $200,000; January,\n$200,000; February, $350,000; March, $400,000.\n All sales are on credit and collected the month\nfollowing the sale.\n Purchases are 60% of next month’s sales and are paid\nfor in the month of purchase.\n Other monthly expenses are $25,000, including\n$5,000 of depreciation.\nIf the January beginning cash balance is $30,000, and the\nentity is required to maintain a minimum cash balance of\n$10,000, how much short-term borrowing will be required at\nthe end of February? Loans are repaid in the following month,\neven though that might require additional borrowing at the end\nof the month.","options":["$60,000","$70,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","$75,000","$80,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Short-term cash requirements at the end of February can be\ncalculated as follows:\nBeginning cash balance $ 30,000\nCollections on December sales (in January) 200,000\nCollections on January sales (in February) 200,000\nDisbursements for inventory (in January) (210,000)\nDisbursements for inventory (in February) (240,000)\nDisbursements for other expenses (in January) (20,000)\nDisbursements for other expenses (in February) (20,000)\nMinimum balance requirement (10,000)\nShortfall $ (70,000)","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q456","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) A company is in its first year of business. The company had a\nbeginning cash balance of $85,000 for the quarter. The\ncompany has a $50,000 short-term line of credit. The\nbudgeted information for the first quarter is shown below.\nJanuary February March\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nSales $60,000 $40,000 $50,000\nPurchases 35,000 40,000 75,000\nOperating costs 25,000 25,000 25,000\nAll sales are made on credit and are collected in the second\nmonth following the sale. Purchases are paid in the month\nfollowing the purchase, while operating costs are paid in the\nmonth that they are incurred. How much will the company\nneed to borrow at the end of the quarter if the company needs\nto maintain a minimum cash balance of $5,000, as required by\na loan covenant agreement?","options":["$0","$5,000","$10,000","$45,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Short-term borrowing needs can be calculated as follows:\nBeginning cash balance $ 85,000\nCollections on January sales (in March) 60,000\nDisbursements for January inventory (in February) (35,000)\nDisbursements for February inventory (in March) (40,000)\nDisbursements for other expenses ($25,000 × 3) (75,000)\nMinimum balance requirement (5,000)\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nShortfall $ (10,000)","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q457","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) Which one of the following best represents a factor that should\nbe considered for medium- and long-term cash forecasting?","options":["Pre-tax cost of capital projects.","Current monthly depreciation.","Impact of stock split.","Non-routine property sales."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Non-routine property sales could result in large fluctuations of\ncash and should be considered for medium- and long-term\nforecasting.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q458","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) A corporation’s current year-end sales totaled $240 million,\nand its ending cash balance was $20 million. The corporation\nanticipates its sales for the upcoming year will be $260 million.\nOn average, 10% of a year’s sales will be collected during the\nfollowing year. Assume the corporation has no uncollectible\naccounts. The corporation also anticipates cash expenses of\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\n$240 million and depreciation of $5 million. During the next\nyear, the corporation intends to spend $30 million cash for\ncapital improvements. If the corporation’s policy is to have a\nminimum of $10 million cash available at the beginning of each\nyear, its budgeted cash flow projections indicate that it will\nneed outside financing of","options":["$0","$2 million","$7 million","$26 million\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget"],"answer":"B","explanation":"During the coming year, the corporation will have a beginning\nbalance of $20,000,000, along with cash inflows from current-\nyear sales of $234,000,000 (90% × $260,000,000) and cash\ninflow from previous-year sales of $24,000,000\n(10% × $240,000,000). There will be total cash outflow of\n$270,000,000 ($240,000,000 expenses + $30,000,000 capital\nimprovements). Therefore, at the end of the year, the cash\nbalance will be $8,000,000, requiring $2,000,000 of cash\nfinancing to stay above $10,000,000.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q459","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) As part of the master budget process, a merchandising\ncompany begins to prepare the cash budget for the same\nperiod. Which of the following additional information will\nbe most useful to management in preparing this budget?","options":["Sales credit policies, purchasing terms, and planned capital\nacquisition.","Projected revenues, projected expenses, and intended\nfinancing activities.","Credit policies, projected expenses, and inventory\nprocurement policies.","Planned direct material purchases, planned direct labor, and\npurchasing terms."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The cash budget is a schedule showing the expected cash\nreceipts and disbursements. Thus, knowing the timing of cash\nreceipts and disbursements is essential in preparing this\nbudget.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q460","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) A company is preparing its cash budget for the coming month.\nAll sales are made on account. Given the following:\nBeginning\nBalances\nBudgeted\nAmounts\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nCash $ 50,000\nAccounts receivable 180,000\nSales $800,000\nCash disbursements 780,000\nDepreciation 25,000\nEnding accounts receivable balance 210,000\nWhat is the expected cash balance of the company at the end\nof the coming month?","options":["$15,000","$40,000","$45,000","$70,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nFact Pattern: Tidwell Corporation sells a single product for $20 per\nunit. All sales are on account, with 60% collected in the month of sale\nand 40% collected in the following month. A partial schedule of cash\ncollections for January through March of the coming year reveals the\nfollowing receipts for the period:\nCash Receipts\nJanuary February March\nDecember receivables $32,000\nFrom January sales 54,000 $36,000\nFrom February sales 66,000 $44,000\nOther information includes the following:\n Inventories are maintained at 30% of the following month’s\nsales.\n Tidwell desires to keep a minimum cash balance of $15,000.\nTotal payments in January are expected to be $106,500,\nwhich excludes $12,000 of depreciation expense. Any\nrequired borrowings are in multiples of $1,000.\n The December 31 balance sheet for the preceding year\nrevealed a cash balance of $24,900."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Collections on account equal beginning accounts receivable of\n$180,000, plus sales on account of $800,000, minus budgeted\nending accounts receivable of $210,000, or $770,000. The\nbeginning cash balance of $50,000, plus cash collections on\naccount of $770,000, minus budgeted cash disbursements of\n$780,000 equals $40,000. Depreciation of $25,000 is\nexcluded because it is a noncash expense.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q461","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) Ignoring income taxes, the financing Tidwell will need in\nJanuary to maintain the firm’s minimum cash balance is\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","options":["$8,000","$10,600","$11,000","$23,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Tidwell’s ending cash balance for January is calculated as\nfollows:\nBeginning balance of cash $ 24,900\nAdd: cash receipts 86,000\nCash available $110,900\nLess: payments (106,500)\nEnding cash before borrowing $ 4,400\nTo reach the minimum acceptable cash balance of $15,000,\nthe company will need to borrow at least $10,600. Since loans\nare in multiples of $1,000, the borrowing must be rounded up\nto $11,000.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q462","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) A firm develops an annual cash budget in order to","options":["Support the preparation of its cash flow statement for the\nannual report.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","Ascertain which capital expenditure projects are feasible and\nwhich capital expenditure projects should be deferred.","Determine the opportunity costs of alternative sales and\nproduction strategies.","Avoid the opportunity costs of noninvested excess cash and\nminimize the cost of interim financing."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The cash budget is perhaps the most important part of a\ncompany’s budget program. A cash budget facilitates planning\nfor loans and other financing. Additionally, a firm should plan\nhow to invest temporary surpluses of cash.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q463","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) A company uses a calendar year and prepares a cash budget\nfor each month of the year. Which one of the following items\nshould be considered when developing July’s cash budget?","options":["Federal income tax and Social Security tax withheld from\nemployees’ June paychecks to be remitted to the Internal\nRevenue Service in July.","Quarterly cash dividends scheduled to be declared on July 15\nand paid on August 6 to shareholders of record as of July 25.","Property taxes levied in the last calendar year scheduled to be\npaid quarterly in the coming year during the last month of each\ncalendar quarter.","Recognition that 0.5% of the July sales on account will be\nuncollectible."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Withholding amounts that must be forwarded to the federal\ngovernment represent cash collections that must be\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\ndisbursed. They would therefore be included in the cash\nbudget for the month of disbursement.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q464","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) A publicly traded company operates a seasonal business with\nhigh production in the month of November for which suppliers\nare paid in December in order to take advantage of a purchase\ndiscount. High sales typically occur in December, with\npayment received by the company in January. The company’s\nabbreviated December cash budget is shown below.\nCash balance, beginning $875,000\nCash receipts 200,000\nCash disbursements\nPayments to suppliers 520,000\nOther operating costs 500,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nDividends 80,000\nCash balance, ending $ (25,000)\nThe company is considering alternatives to provide the\ncompany with the desired ending cash balance of $75,000 in\nDecember. The best action(s) for the company would be to","options":["Eliminate $80,000 of dividends and postpone $20,000 of\npayments to suppliers.","Eliminate $80,000 of dividends and arrange for $20,000 of\nshort-term borrowing.","Postpone $100,000 of payments to suppliers.","Arrange for $100,000 of short-term borrowing."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Since the company’s business is seasonal and requires high\npayments to suppliers in December to take advantage of high\nsales in December for which payment will be received by the\ncompany in January, the best alternative to provide the\ncompany with the desired ending cash balance of $75,000\nwould be to arrange for $100,000 of short-term borrowing.\nThis allows the company to pay dividends to its shareholders\nand take advantage of purchase discounts.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q465","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) A company has a beginning cash balance of $10,000 and\nexpects $40,000 in cash receipts for each of the next 2\nmonths. Typically, disbursements total about $20,000 per\nmonth. The company’s payables policy has been to pay the\nbills upon receipt to maintain good vendor relationships and\ntake advantage of any discounts. In month 1, the company\nalso expects a one-time $40,000 bill for a patent application.\nBased on this information, select the statement below that\nreflects the most appropriate action that the company should\ntake relative to the company’s cash position during the 2-\nmonth period.","options":["The company should arrange a short-term line of credit large\nenough to cover the projected $10,000 shortfall during the first\nmonth.","The company should defer disbursements to maintain a\ndesired level of cash.","The company should finance the $40,000 payment over a\nlonger term, but with a higher interest rate.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","No action is necessary as the company will have sufficient\ncash during the 2-month period."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Taking a short-term line of credit large enough only to cover\nthe projected $10,000 shortfall during the first month would\ncover the shortage while minimizing financing costs.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q466","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) A company’s management team is preparing a cash budget for\nthe coming quarter.\nJanuary February March\nRevenue $700,000 $800,000 $500,000\nInventory\npurchases\n350,000 425,000 225,000\nOther\nexpenses\n150,000 175,000 175,000\nThe company expects to collect 40% of its monthly sales in the\nmonth of sale and 60% in the following month. 50% of\ninventory purchases are paid in the month of purchase, and\n50% in the following month. Payments for all other expenses\nare made in the month incurred.\nThe company forecasts the following account balances at the\nbeginning of the quarter:\nCash $200,000\nAccounts receivable 300,000\nAccounts payable (Inventory) 400,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nGiven the above information, the projected ending cash\nbalance for February will be","options":["$712,500","$500,000","$232,500","$120,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The company’s February cash balance is $232,500 as\ncalculated below.\nJanuary February\nOpening\nbalance\n$200,000 $ 55,000\nAccounts\nreceivable\n300,000 (Dec.) 420,000 (700,000\n× .6)\nAccounts\nreceivable\n280,000 (700,000\n× .4)\n320,000 (800,000\n× .4)\nAccounts\npayable\n400,000 (Dec.) 175,000 (350,000\n× .5)\nAccounts\npayable\n175,000 (350,000\n× .5)\n212,500 (425,000\n× .5)\nOther\nexpense\n150,000 175,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nCash\nbalance\n$ 55,000 $232,500","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q467","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) Which one of the following is in a company’s cash budget?","options":["Depreciation of plant equipment.","Amortization of patent costs.","Conversion of debt for equity.","Disposal of land."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A cash budget details projected cash receipts and\ndisbursements. The cash budget combines the results of the\noperating budget with the cash collection and disbursement\nschedules to produce a comprehensive picture of where the\ncompany’s cash flows are expected to come from and where\nthey are expected to go. Proceeds from disposal of land is\ncash collection, which is included in a cash budget.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q468","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) The cash budget must be prepared before completing the","options":["Capital expenditure budget.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","Sales budget.","Forecasted balance sheet.","Production budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The pro forma balance sheet is the balance sheet for the\nbeginning of the period updated for projected changes in cash,\nreceivables, inventories, payables, etc. Accordingly, it cannot\nbe prepared until after the cash budget is completed because\ncash is a current asset reported on the balance sheet.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q469","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) The cash budget is used to plan all of the following except","options":["The cash collection schedule.","Outside financing activity.","The investing of excess cash.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","The firm’s dividend policy."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The cash budget combines the results of the operating budget\nwith the cash collection and disbursement schedules to\nproduce a comprehensive view of the sources and uses of\ncash. As such, the collection schedule is used to prepare the\ncash budget, but the cash budget is not used to plan the cash\ncollection schedule.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q470","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) Which of the following budgets or schedules is not considered\nwhen creating the cash budget?","options":["Operating budget.","Pro forma statement of cash flows.","Cash collections schedule.","Cash disbursements schedule."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The cash budget combines the results of the operating budget\nwith the cash collection and disbursement schedules to\nproduce a comprehensive view of the sources and uses of\ncash. The pro forma statement of cash flows is prepared after\nthe cash budget, and therefore is not considered in the cash\nbudget’s creation.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q471","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) The most direct way to prepare a cash budget for a\nmanufacturing firm is to include","options":["Projected sales, credit terms, and net income.","Projected net income, depreciation, and goodwill impairment.\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","Projected purchases, percentages of purchases paid, and net\nincome.","Projected sales and purchases, percentages of collections,\nand terms of payments."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The most direct way of preparing a cash budget requires\nincorporation of sales projections and credit terms, collection\npercentages, estimated purchases and payment terms, and\nother cash receipts and disbursements. In other words,\npreparation of the cash budget requires consideration of both\ninflows and outflows.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q472","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) The following information applies to B Company:\nPurchases Sales\nJanuary $160,000 $100,000\nFebruary 160,000 200,000\nMarch 160,000 240,000\nApril 140,000 300,000\nMay 140,000 260,000\nJune 120,000 240,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nA cash payment equal to 40% of purchases is made at the\ntime of purchase, and 30% is paid in each of the next 2\nmonths. Purchases for the previous November and December\nwere $150,000 per month. Payroll is 10% of sales in the month\nit occurs, and operating expenses are 20% of the following\nmonth’s sales (July sales were $220,000). Interest payments\nwere $20,000 paid quarterly in January and April. Cash\ndisbursements for the month of April were","options":["$140,000","$152,000","$200,000","$254,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cash disbursements for the month of April are calculated as\nfollows:\nApril purchases: $140,000 × 40% = $ 56,000\nMarch purchases: $160,000 × 30% = 48,000\nFebruary purchases: $160,000 × 30% = 48,000\nApril payroll: $300,000 × 10% = 30,000\nApril op. expenses: $260,000 × 20% = 52,000\nInterest = 20,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget\nTotal April disbursements = $254,000","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q473","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":32,"question":"32) Purchases Sales\nJanuary $150,000 $100,000\nFebruary 150,000 200,000\nMarch 150,000 250,000\nApril 130,000 250,000\nMay 130,000 300,000\nJune 100,000 230,000\nA cash payment equal to 50% of purchases is made at the\ntime of purchase, and 25% is paid in each of the next 2\nmonths. Purchases for the previous November and\nDecember were $140,000 per month. Payroll for a month\nis 10% of that month’s sales, and other operating\nexpenses are 15% of the following month’s sales (July\nsales were $210,000). Interest payments were $25,000\npaid quarterly in January and April. Cash disbursements\nfor the month of April were","options":["$130,000","$140,000","$210,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","$235,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cash disbursements for the month of April are calculated\nas follows:\nApril purchases: $130,000 × 50% = $ 65,000\nMarch purchases: 150,000 × 25% = 37,500\nFebruary purchases: 150,000 × 25% = 37,500\nApril payroll: 250,000 × 10% = 25,000\nApril op. expenses: 300,000 × 15% = 45,000\nInterest = 25,000\nTotal April disbursements = $235,000","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q474","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":33,"question":"33) Consider the following data for a company for the month just\nended.\nSales $4,000,000\nGross margin (based on sales) 25%\nDecrease in inventories $160,000\nDecrease in accounts payable for inventories $275,000\nThe estimated cash disbursements for inventories were\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","options":["$2,565,000","$2,840,000","$3,115,000","$3,275,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The cash disbursements for inventory during the period can\nbe calculated as follows:\nCost of goods sold + Decrease in accounts payable for\ninventories – Decrease in inventories\n($4,000,000 × 0.75) + $275,000 – $160,000 = $3,115,000","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q475","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":34,"question":"34) A corporation’s annual budget shows expected inventory\npurchases of $55,000,000 from its suppliers. Selected financial\ninformation from the corporation’s pro forma beginning and\nending balance sheets are as shown below.\nJanuary 1 December 31\nInventory $6,000,000 $7,500,000\nAccounts payable $4,000,000 $5,000,000\nWhat is the amount of cash paid to suppliers?","options":["$56,000,000","$55,500,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 5: Cash Disbursements and the Cash Budget","$54,500,000","$54,000,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cash paid to suppliers is calculated as follows: purchases –\nincrease in accounts payable. The corporation will have\n$54,000,000 ($55,000,000 purchases – $1,000,000 increase\nin accounts payable) as cash paid to suppliers.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\n14: (175) Budgeting -- Calculations and Pro Forma\nFinancial Statements\n6: (31) Pro Forma Financial Statements\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nFact Pattern: Kelly Company is a retail sporting goods store that\nuses accrual accounting for its records. Facts regarding Kelly’s\noperations are as follows:\n Sales are budgeted at $220,000 for December Year 1 and\n$200,000 for January Year 2.\n Collections are expected to be 60% in the month of sale and\n38% in the month following the sale.\n Gross margin is 25% of sales.\n A total of 80% of the merchandise held for resale is\npurchased in the month prior to the month of sale and 20% is\npurchased in the month of sale. Payment for merchandise is\nmade in the month following the purchase.\n Other expected monthly expenses to be paid in cash are\n$22,600.\n Annual depreciation is $216,000.\nBelow is Kelly Company’s statement of financial position at\nNovember 30, Year 1.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nAssets\nCash $ 22,000\nAccounts receivable (net of $4,000\nallowance for uncollectible accounts) 76,000\nInventory 132,000\nProperty, plant, and equipment (net of\n$680,000 accumulated depreciation) 870,000\nTotal assets $1,100,000\nLiabilities and Stockholders’ Equity\nAccounts payable $ 162,000\nCommon stock 800,000\nRetained earnings 138,000\nTotal liabilities and stockholders’ equity $1,100,000","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q476","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) Kelly’s pro forma income (loss) before income taxes for\nDecember Year 1 is","options":["$32,400","$28,000","$10,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements","$9,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sales are budgeted at $220,000. Given that cost of goods sold\nis 75% of sales, or $165,000, gross profit is $55,000. Deduct\ncash expenses of $22,600, depreciation of $18,000 ($216,000\n÷ 12), and bad debt expense of $4,400 ($220,000 × .02). This\nleaves an income of $10,000.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q477","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) Kelly’s projected balance in accounts payable on December\n31, Year 1, is","options":["$162,000","$204,000","$153,000","Some amount other than those given.\nFact Pattern:\nSuper Drive, a computer disk storage and back-up company, uses\naccrual accounting. The company’s Statement of Financial Position\nfor the year ended November 30 is as follows:\nSuper Drive\nStatement of Financial Position\nas of November 30\nAssets\nCash $ 52,0\n00\nAccounts receivable, net 150,000\nInventory 315,000\nLiabilities and Stockholders’\nEquity\nAccounts payable $ 175,\n000\nCommon stock 900,000\nRetained earnings 442,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nProperty, plant, and equip\nment\n1,000,00\n0\nTotal assets $1,517,0\n00\nTotal liabilities and\nstockholders’ equity\n$1,517,\n000\nAdditional information regarding Super Drive’s operations include\nthe following:\n Sales are budgeted at $520,000 for December and\n$500,000 for January of the next year.\n Collections are expected to be 60% in the month of\nsale and 40% in the month following the sale.\n Eighty percent of the disk drive components are\npurchased in the month prior to the month of sale, and 20%\nare purchased in the month of sale. Purchased components\nare 40% of the cost of goods sold.\n Payment for the components is made in the month\nfollowing the purchase.\n Cost of goods sold is 80% of sales."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The balance is equal to the purchases made during December\nsince all purchases are paid for in the month following\npurchase. Purchases for December is given as 20% of\nDecember’s sales and 80% of January’s sales. Thus, of the\n$220,000 of merchandise sold during December, 20%, or\n$44,000, would have been purchased during the month.\nJanuary’s sales are expected to be $200,000, so 80% of that\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\namount, or $160,000, would have been purchased during\nDecember. December purchases are thus estimated as\n$204,000 at the company’s selling prices. The merchandise\ncosts only 75% of the marked selling prices, however.\nTherefore, the balance in the purchases account at month-end\nis projected to be $153,000 ($204,000 × 75%).","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q478","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) Super Drive’s projected balance in accounts payable on\nDecember 31 is","options":["$161,280\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements","$326,400","$166,400","$416,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Payments are made in the month following purchase. The\nbalance in accounts payable on November 30 is $175,000;\nthis amount will be paid in December. The account is credited\nfor purchases of a portion of components to be used for sales\nin December (20% of December components) and for sales in\nJanuary (80% of January components). Cost of goods sold is\n80% of sales, and components are 40% of cost of goods sold.\nTherefore, December component needs are $166,400\n($520,000 sales × 80% × 40%), and January component\nneeds are $160,000 ($500,000 sales × 80% × 40%). The\nDecember purchases of December component needs are\n$33,280 ($166,400 × 20%). December purchases of January\ncomponent needs are $128,000 ($160,000 × 80%). Thus, total\nDecember purchases (ending balance in accounts payable) is\n$161,280 ($33,280 + $128,000).","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q479","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) Super Drive’s projected gross profit for the month ending\nDecember 31 is\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements","options":["$416,000","$104,000","$134,000","$536,000\nFact Pattern:\nEasecom manufactures products for\nnetworking video-conferencing\nequipment. Production of specialized\nunits is, to a large extent, performed\nunder contract, with standard units\nmanufactured to marketing projections.\nMaintenance of customer equipment is\nan important area of customer\nsatisfaction. Easecom’s income\nstatement for the fiscal year ended\nOctober 31, Year 1, is presented below.\nEasecom\nEasecom’s management\nconsidered the growing\nvideo-conferencing market\nwhen it proposed the\nfollowing actions for fiscal\nYear 2:\n• Increase equipment sales\nprices by 10%.\n•\nIncrease the cost of each\nunit sold by 3% for\nneeded technology and\nquality improvements,\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nIncome Statement\nFor the Year Ended October 31,\nYear 1\n($000 omitted)\nNet sales:\nEquipment $6,000\nMaintenance contracts 1,800\nTotal net sales 7,800\nExpenses:\nCost of goods sold 4,600\nCustomer maintenance 1,000\nSelling expense 600\nAdministrative expense 900\nInterest expense 150\nTotal expenses 7,250\nIncome before income\ntaxes\n550\nIncome taxes 220\nNet income $ 330\nand increased variable\ncosts.\n•\nIncrease maintenance\ninventory by $250,000 at\nthe beginning of the year\nand add two maintenance\ntechnicians at a total cost\nof $130,000 to cover\nwages and related travel\nexpenses. These\nrevisions are intended to\nimprove customer service\nand response time. The\nincreased inventory will\nbe financed at an annual\ninterest rate of 12%; no\nother borrowings or loan\nreductions are\ncontemplated during\nfiscal Year 2. All other\nassets will be held to\nfiscal Year 1 levels.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\n•\nIncrease selling expenses\nby $250,000 but hold\nadministrative expenses\nat Year 1 levels.\n•\nThe effective rate for Year\n2 federal and state taxes\nis expected to be 40%,\nthe same as Year 1.\nThese actions are\nexpected to increase\nequipment unit sales by\n6%, with a corresponding\n6% growth in maintenance\ncontracts."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Given that cost of goods sold is 80% of sales, gross profit is\n20% of sales. Consequently, pro forma gross profit is\n$104,000 ($520,000 × 20%).","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q480","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) In its pro forma income statement for the fiscal year ending\nOctober 31, Year 2, Easecom estimated that total net sales will\nbe","options":["$8,904,000","$7,800,000","$6,996,000","$6,000,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Equipment unit sales and price are projected to increase by\n6% and 10%, respectively. Hence, equipment sales are\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nprojected to be $6,996,000 ($6,000,000 for the year ended\n10/31/Year 1 × 1.06 × 1.10). Maintenance contracts are\nprojected to be increased by 6%. Hence, maintenance sales\nare projected to be $1,908,000 ($1,800,000 for the year ended\n10/31/Year 1 × 1.06). The pro forma total net sales will\ntherefore be $8,904,000 ($6,996,000 + $1,908,000).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q481","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) In its pro forma income statement for the year ended October\n31, Year 2, Easecom estimated that cost of goods sold will be","options":["$8,082,280","$7,250,000","$5,022,280","$4,600,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The cost and unit sales of equipment are projected to increase\nby 3% and 6%, respectively. Accordingly, pro forma cost of\ngoods sold will be $5,022,280 ($4,600,000 for the year ended\n10/31/Year 1 × 1.03 × 1.06).","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q482","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) In its pro forma income statement for the year ended October\n31, Year 2, estimated net income will be","options":["$493,032","$330,000","$328,688","$220,000\nFact Pattern: Kelly Company is a retail sporting goods store that\nuses accrual accounting for its records. Facts regarding Kelly’s\noperations are as follows:\n Sales are budgeted at $220,000 for December Year 1 and\n$200,000 for January Year 2.\n Collections are expected to be 60% in the month of sale and\n38% in the month following the sale.\n Gross margin is 25% of sales.\n A total of 80% of the merchandise held for resale is\npurchased in the month prior to the month of sale and 20% is\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\npurchased in the month of sale. Payment for merchandise is\nmade in the month following the purchase.\n Other expected monthly expenses to be paid in cash are\n$22,600.\n Annual depreciation is $216,000.\nBelow is Kelly Company’s statement of financial position at\nNovember 30, Year 1.\nAssets\nCash $ 22,000\nAccounts receivable (net of $4,000\nallowance for uncollectible accounts) 76,000\nInventory 132,000\nProperty, plant, and equipment (net of\n$680,000 accumulated depreciation) 870,000\nTotal assets $1,100,000\nLiabilities and Stockholders’ Equity\nAccounts payable $ 162,000\nCommon stock 800,000\nRetained earnings 138,000\nTotal liabilities and stockholders’ equity $1,100,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Equipment unit sales and price are projected to increase by\n6% and 10%, respectively. Hence, equipment sales are\nprojected to be $6,996,000 ($6,000,000 for the year ended\n10/31/Year 1 × 1.06 × 1.10). Maintenance contracts are\nprojected to be increased by 6%. Hence, maintenance sales\nare projected to be $1,908,000 ($1,800,000 for the year ended\n10/31/Year 1 × 1.06). The pro forma total net sales will\ntherefore be $8,904,000 ($6,996,000 + $1,908,000). Next, the\ncost and unit sales of equipment are projected to increase by\n3% and 6%, respectively. Accordingly, pro forma cost of\ngoods sold will be $5,022,280 ($4,600,000 for the year ended\n10/31/Year 1 × 1.03 × 1.06). Customer maintenance expense\nis projected to increase to $1,130,000 ($1,000,000 for the year\nended 10/31/Year 1 + $130,000 wages and travel expenses\nincrease for Year 2), and selling expenses are projected to\nincrease to $850,000 ($600,000 for the year ended 10/31/Year\n1 + $250,000 increase for Year 2). Administrative expense is\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nunchanged at $900,000, but interest is projected to increase\nfrom $150,000 to $180,000 [$150,000 + ($250,000 × 12%\ninventory financing rate)]. Consequently, total pro forma\nexpenses are $8,082,280 ($5,022,280 + $1,130,000 +\n$850,000 + $900,000 + $180,000). Pro forma pretax income is\ntherefore $821,720 ($8,904,000 total net sales – $8,082,280\ntotal expenses), and pro forma net income is $493,032\n[$821,720 × (1.0 – .40 tax rate)].","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q483","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) Kelly’s projected balance in inventory on December 31, Year\n1, is","options":["$160,000","$120,000","$153,000","$150,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The inventory is expected to be 80% of January’s needs.\nProjected January sales of $200,000 × 80% = $160,000.\nThus, the ending inventory would be goods that the company\ncould sell for $160,000. Given a gross margin of 25%, cost\nwould only be 75% of sales, and ending inventory would be\n$120,000 ($160,000 × 75%).","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q484","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) In November, a company finalized its budget for the upcoming\ncalendar year. In December, the decision was made to acquire\nnew equipment in January by trading in old equipment and\nfinancing the amount due by a loan with principal and interest\ndue at the end of 3 years. Out-of-pocket costs to operate the\nmachinery would not change. This decision would change\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nwhich of the company’s budgeted financial statements for the\nupcoming year?","options":["The budgeted balance sheet only.","Both the budgeted balance sheet and the income statement.","The budgeted balance sheet, the income statement, and the\nstatement of cash flows.","Both the budgeted income statement and the statement of\ncash flows."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The budgeted balance sheet, the income statement, and the\nstatement of cash flows would all change.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q485","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) A company owns several retail stores. After all initial budget\nrequests were received for the upcoming year, the company’s\nabbreviated pro forma income statement is as follows:\nSales $46,000,000\nCost of goods sold 20,700,000\nSelling and administrative costs 19,800,000\nOperating income 5,500,000\nThe cost of goods sold and a 5% sales commission are the\nonly variable costs. The company’s upper management\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nbelieves that the sales manager underestimated projected\nsales units and wants the sales budget increased such that the\ncompany can achieve its goal of a 15% return on sales. The\namount by which sales must increase to achieve this goal is","options":["$4,000,000","$3,500,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements","$1,750,000","$1,400,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Return on sales is equal to operating income divided by sales.\nFor every dollar added to sales, 45% will be the cost of goods\nsold ($20,700,000 ÷ $46,000,000), and 5% will be sales\ncommissions. Therefore, for every dollar that is added to\nsales, $.50 [(100% – 45% – 5%) × $1] is added to operating\nincome. To find how much the sales budget must be\nincreased by to raise the return on sales to 15%, algebra is\nthe most appropriate tool, as follows:\n[($5,500,000 + .5X) ÷ ($46,000,000 +\nX)]\n= .15\n($5,500,000 + .5X) = .15 × ($46,000,000 +\nX)\n$5,500,000 + .5X = $6,900,000 + .15X\n.35X = $1,400,000\nX = $4,000,000","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q486","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) Pro forma financial statements are part of the budgeting\nprocess. Normally, the last pro forma statement prepared is\nthe","options":["Capital expenditure plan.","Income statement.","Statement of cost of goods sold.","Statement of cash flows."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The statement of cash flows is usually the last of the listed\nitems prepared. All other elements of the budget process must\nbe completed before it can be developed.\nFact Pattern:\nKarmee Company has been\naccumulating operating data in order to\nprepare an annual profit plan. Details\nregarding Karmee’s sales for the first 6\nThe variable operating\nexpenses (other than cost\nof goods sold) for Karmee\nare 10% of sales and are\npaid for in the month\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nmonths of the coming year are as\nfollows:\nEstimated\nMonthly Sales\nJanuary $600,0\n00\nFebruar\ny\n650,00\n0\nMarch 700,00\n0\nApril 625,00\n0\nMay 720,00\n0\nJune 800,00\n0\nType of Monthly\nSale\nCash sales 20%\nCredit sales 80%\nCollection Pattern for\nCredit Sales\nMonth of sale 30\n%\nOne month\nfollowing sale\n40\n%\nSecond month\nfollowing sale\n25\n%\nKarmee’s cost of goods sold averages\n40% of the sales value. Karmee’s\nobjective is to maintain a target\ninventory equal to 30% of the next\nmonth’s sales in units. Purchases of\nfollowing the sale. The\nannual fixed operating\nexpenses are presented\nbelow. All of these are\nincurred uniformly\nthroughout the year and\npaid monthly except for\ninsurance and property\ntaxes. Insurance is paid\nquarterly in January, April,\nJuly, and October. Property\ntaxes are paid twice a year\nin April and October.\nAnnual Fixed\nOperating Costs\nAdvertising $ 720,00\n0\nDepreciation 420,000\nInsurance 180,000\nProperty\ntaxes\n240,000\nSalaries 1,080,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nmerchandise for resale are paid for in\nthe month following the sale.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q487","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) The amount for cost of goods sold that will appear on Karmee\nCompany’s pro forma income statement for the month of\nFebruary will be","options":["$195,000","$254,000","$260,000","$272,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is expected to be 40% of sales. Thus, cost\nof goods sold is $260,000 ($650,000 February sales × 40%).","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q488","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) The cash budget must be prepared before the company can\ncomplete the","options":["Capital expenditure budget.","Forecasted income statement.","Production budget.","Forecasted balance sheet.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The cash amount on the forecasted balance sheet is taken\ndirectly from the ending cash balance on the cash budget.\nThus, the cash budget must be prepared before the\nforecasted balance sheet.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q489","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) One of the final steps in completing a master budget is the\npreparation of a pro forma cash flow statement. This statement\nis intended to help users of financial statements","options":["Evaluate a firm’s economic resources and obligations.","Evaluate a firm’s liquidity, solvency, and financial flexibility.","Determine a firm’s components of income from operations.","Determine whether or not accounts receivable are collectible.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The pro forma statement of cash flows classifies cash receipts\nand disbursements depending on whether they are from\noperating, investing, or financing activities. Thus, it will help\nusers evaluate a firm’s liquidity, solvency, and financial\nflexibility by analyzing the different cash disbursements and\nreceipts.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q490","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) A company has used the following data to prepare a pro forma\nincome statement for the first quarter of next year. The\ncompany’s effective income tax rate is 40%. The company’s\ntargeted gross margin percentage is 50%.\nSales $4,678,500\nBeginning finished goods inventory 12,600\nEnding finished goods inventory 18,900\nSelling and administrative expenses 1,250,760\nCost of goods manufactured 2,445,790\nWhich one of the following is the best course of action?","options":["Since the pro forma gross margin percentage is lower than\n50%, management should plan to lower the company’s tax\nrate to improve next quarter’s results.","Since the pro forma gross margin percentage is higher than\n50%, management should plan to follow the master budget to\nachieve the targeted results.","Since the pro forma gross margin percentage is lower than\n50%, management should plan to decrease manufacturing\ncosts next quarter.","Since the pro forma gross margin percentage is higher than\n50%, management should plan to increase the sales price next\nquarter."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of Goods Sold = Costs of Goods Manufactured +\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nBeginning finished goods inventory – Ending finished goods\ninventory\nCost of Goods Sold = $2,445,790 + $12,600 – $18,900\nCost of Goods Sold = $2,439,490\nThe gross margin percentage is 47.86% [($4,678,500 –\n$2,439,490) ÷ $4,678,500]. This is lower than the targeted\ngross margin percentage of 50%. If management decreases\nmanufacturing costs, the gross margin percentage will\nincrease.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q491","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) A corporation manufactures goods that are sold by\nindependent sales agents who receive a 20% payment based\non sales value. The corporation’s pro forma income statement\nfor the upcoming year is below.\nSales $15,000,000\nCost of goods sold (all variable) 6,000,000\nPayment to sales agents 3,000,000\nOther expenses (all fixed) 2,000,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nOperating income $ 4,000,000\nAfter the budget was created, the corporation became aware\nthat its primary competitors would each be raising their selling\nprices by 5%. If the corporation also increased its selling price\nby 5%, the company’s revised operating income would be","options":["$4,750,000","$4,600,000","$4,300,000","$4,200,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Raising the selling prices by 5% would increase the\ncorporation’s revenue by $750,000 ($15,000,000 × 0.05).\nHowever, payments to sale agents increase by 5% as well,\nwhich results in an expense of $150,000 ($3,000,000 × 0.05).\nThe net increase in operating income is therefore $600,000\n($750,000 – $150,000), which makes the revised operating\nincome $4,600,000.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q492","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) A company is creating its pro forma balance sheet for next\nyear. The company anticipates that 50% of sales will be\ncollected during the month of sale, 40% will be collected in the\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nmonth following the sale, and 10% will be collected 2 months\nafter the sale. If the company’s budgeted sales for the months\nof October, November, and December of the upcoming year\nare $200,000, $350,000, and $450,000, respectively, the\ncompany’s budgeted year-end accounts receivable balance is","options":["$260,000","$285,000","$299,000","$385,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The entire October sales account will be collected by the end\nof the year; however, 10% of November sales will be collected\nin January in the following year, and a total of 50% of\nDecember sales will be collected in the following year (40% in\nJanuary and 10% in February). The budgeted year-end\naccounts receivable balance is $260,000 [($350,000 × 10%) +\n($450,000 × 40%) + ($450,000 × 10%)].","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q493","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) A budgeted income statement based upon sales of 100,000\nunits is shown below:\nSales €10,000,000\nCost of sales (all variable) 6,000,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nSelling and general expenses (55% fixed) 3,000,000\nInterest expense 50,000\nIncome tax expense 285,000\nIncome € 665,000\nIf the sales estimate increased to 120,000 units, the\ncompany’s projected operating income would be","options":["€1,530,000","€1,480,000","€1,465,000","€1,200,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Operating income includes sales, cost of sales, and selling\nand general expenses. Interest and taxes are not included in\noperating income. The selling price per units is €100\n(€10,000,000 ÷ 100,000 units). The cost of sales per unit is\n€60 (€6,000,000 ÷ 100,000 units). The fixed portion of selling\nand general expenses is €1,650,000 (€3,000,000 × 55%). The\nvariable selling and general expense per unit is €13.50\n[(€3,000,000 – €1,650,000) ÷ 100,000 units]. Thus, the\noperating income is calculated as follows:\nSales (€100 × 120,000 units) €12,000,000\nCost of sales (€60 × 120,000 units) 7,200,000\nSelling and general expenses [€1,650,000 +\n(€13.50 × 120,000 units)] 3,270,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nOperating income € 1,530,000","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q494","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) A company makes one product that it sells for €125 per unit.\nThe product has a contribution margin of 35% of sales. Direct\nmaterials account for 10% of sales. Variable manufacturing\noverhead is 5% of sales. Fixed costs are €200,000 per year.\nThe controller wants to create a pro forma income statement\nwhere the sales increase from 10,000 units to 12,000 units.\nThe average income tax rate is 25.71%. What is the change in\noperating income as a result of the increase in unit sales?","options":["€50,000","€65,000","€75,000","€87,500"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Since there is no increase in fixed costs, the change in income\nis equal to the increase in contribution margin. The increase in\nsales is €250,000 (2,000 extra units at €125 each). Multiplying\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nthe 35% contribution margin by the €250,000 of incremental\nsales results in an €87,500 increase in contribution and an\nincrease in operating income of the same amount.","sectionId":"14.2"},{"id":"v2-su14-q495","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) The pro forma income statement for a manufacturing company\nis built on projections of all of the following except","options":["Marketing costs.","Production overhead.","Cash balances.","Ending inventory."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cash is an asset, which appears on the balance sheet. Thus,\ncash does not appear on the income statement.","sectionId":"14.5"},{"id":"v2-su14-q496","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) A company expects the following results for Year 1.\nSales $2,000,000\nCost of goods sold 1,200,000\nIndirect costs 400,000\nEarnings before interest and taxes $ 400,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nThe controller is preparing a forecast for Year 2 using the\nfollowing assumptions.\nUnit sales growth: 5% per year\nIncrease in unit selling price: 3% per year\nIncrease in direct cost per unit: 2% per year\nIncrease in indirect costs: 4% per year\nThe forecast of earnings before interest and taxes for Year 2\nusing the above assumptions (rounded to the nearest\nthousand) would be","options":["$523,000","$461,800","$460,000","$423,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Current sales are $2,000,000; therefore, with a 5% increase in\nthe unit sales growth, the forecasted sales are expected to be\n$2,100,000 [$2,000,000 × (1 + 5%)]. The 3% increase in unit\nselling price would increase total sales to $2,163,000\n($2,100,000 × 1.03). Direct costs would increase by the 5%\nincrease in units and the 2% increase in unit costs, resulting in\ntotal costs of $1,285,200 ($1,200,000 × 1.05 × 1.02). Indirect\ncosts would increase by 4% to $416,000 ($400,000 × 1.04).\nThus, the Year 2 income statement would appear as follows:\nSales $2,163,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nCost of goods sold 1,285,200\nIndirect costs 416,000\nEarnings before interest and taxes $ 461,800\n$461,800 rounded to the nearest thousand is $462,000.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q497","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) The pro forma statement of employee benefit costs, a budget\nschedule that is prepared as part of an organization’s annual\nprofit plan, would include costs related to","options":["Employees’ gross wages and salaries and the related company-\npaid benefits.","Employees’ net wages and salaries and the related company-\npaid benefits.","All payroll-related deductions withheld from employees and\ncompany-paid benefits.","Company-paid benefits and company-paid payroll taxes."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The pro forma statement of employee benefit costs reports\nthose personnel-related costs that are not included in direct\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nlabor, i.e., company-paid benefits and company-paid payroll\ntaxes.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q498","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) All of the following would appear on a projected schedule of\ncost of goods manufactured except for","options":["Ending work-in-process inventory.","Beginning finished goods inventory.","The cost of raw materials used.","Applied manufacturing overhead."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Beginning finished goods inventory is a component of cost of\ngoods sold, not cost of goods manufactured.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q499","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) The information contained in a cost of goods manufactured\nbudget most directly relates to the","options":["Materials used, direct labor, overhead applied, and ending\nwork-in-process budgets.","Materials used, direct labor, overhead applied, and work-in-\nprocess inventories budgets.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements","Materials used, direct labor, overhead applied, work-in-process\ninventories, and finished goods inventories budgets.","Materials used, direct labor, overhead applied, and finished\ngoods inventories budgets."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured equals all manufacturing costs\nincurred during the period, plus beginning work-in-process\ninventory, minus ending work-in-process. The cost of goods\nmanufactured schedule therefore includes direct materials,\ndirect labor, factory overhead, and changes in work-in-process\ninventories.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q500","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) When preparing the annual budget, which one of the following\nis an acceptable method of allocating production line workers’\nfringe benefits?","options":["100% allocation to manufacturing overhead.","100% allocation to administrative expenses.","Pro rata allocation between manufacturing overhead and\nadministrative expenses.","Pro rata allocation between nonoperating expenses and\nadministrative expenses."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The cost of fringe benefits must be derived once the cost of\nwages has been determined. The fringe benefits are then\nincluded in direct labor or allocated as manufacturing\noverhead, and the effect on cost of goods sold is the same.\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nThus, out of all the answer choices, 100% allocation to\nmanufacturing overhead is the most accurate.","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q501","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) In general, which of the following pro forma financial statements\nis prepared first?","options":["Pro forma income statement.","Pro forma balance sheet.","Pro forma statement of cash flows.","Pro forma statement of retained earnings."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The first financial statement forecasted is generally the income\nstatement.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q502","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) Which of the following is not a reason that a bank would be\ninterested in reviewing a firm’s pro forma financial statements?\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements","options":["To determine a borrower’s expected future profitability.","To determine whether a debt covenant has been violated.","To assess a current borrower’s projected solvency.","To evaluate a potential borrower’s projected capital structure."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Although a bank may use pro forma financial statements to\nassess whether the company anticipates satisfying the\nrequirement of debt companies, the pro forma statements are\nbased on projected information and therefore cannot\nnecessarily determine that a debt covenant has been violated.\nIn order to determine whether the covenant has already been\nviolated, the bank should consult the borrower’s most recent\nfinancial statements that are based on actual activity.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q503","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) Which of the following parties is least likely to be interested in\nan entity’s pro forma financial statements?","options":["Potential lenders.","Current creditors.","Governmental taxing authorities.","Investment analysts."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Governmental taxing authorities generally are not concerned\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nwith projected results of an entity’s operations; they are more\ninterested in actual income as opposed to pro forma income.\nAs such, they are least likely to be interested in an entity’s pro\nforma financial statements.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q504","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) Calculate the pro forma after-tax profit for next year based on\nthe data below.\nResults for\nlast year\nProjections\nfor next year\nSales $100,000 10% increase\nVariable cost $60,000 10% increase\nSalaries $15,000 5% increase\nOther expenses $5,000 20% increase\nIncome tax rate 30% No change","options":["$15,000","$15,575","$16,100","$18,200"],"answer":"B","explanation":"To find the after-tax profit for next year, the amounts given must\nbe adjusted based on the projections for next year:\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nSales = $110,000 ($100,000 × 1.1)\nVariable cost = $66,000 ($60,000 × 1.1)\nSalaries = $15,750 ($15,000 × 1.05)\nOther expenses = $6,000 ($5,000 × 1.2)\nThe sales projection minus the other projected amounts\nmultiplied by 1 minus the tax rate equals an after-tax profit of\n$15,575 [($110,000 – $66,000 – $15,750 – $6,000) × (1 – 0.3)].","sectionId":"14.1"},{"id":"v2-su14-q505","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) StoniBrook Industries produces decorative hardware used by\nhome construction firms and sold in do-it-yourself retail outlets.\nThe business is highly competitive with little opportunity for\nraising prices. StoniBrook’s master budget for the next fiscal\nyear is shown below.\nSales $8,400,000\nCost of goods sold* 6,000,000\nAdvertising 500,000\nSales salaries 400,000\nSales commissions 168,000\nAdministrative expenses 350,000\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nOperating income $ 982,000\n*50% direct material, 30% direct labor, 20% fixed overhead\nThe following events could impact the budget.\n Renegotiation of a union contract resulting in a 3.5%\nwage increase for factory workers.\n A 5% increase in the cost of 20% of its material.\n Elimination of sales commissions with an equal\nincrease in sales salaries.\nIf all of these events occur, the incremental change in\nStoniBrook’s operating income will be","options":["$123,000 decrease.","$93,000 decrease.","$45,000 increase.","$75,000 increase."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The incremental effects of all three of the potential events must\nbe considered.\n Of the $6,000,000 cost of goods sold, 30% is direct\nlabor. Thus, direct labor costs are currently $1,800,000.\nAn increase by 3.5% will result in direct labor costs of\n$1,863,000 ($1,800,000 × 1.035).\n Fifty percent of cost of goods sold relates to direct\nmaterials. Thus, direct materials costs are currently\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\n$3,000,000. If 20% of those costs increases by 5%,\ntotal direct materials cost will be $3,030,000\n[$3,000,000 × (0.8 + 0.2 × 1.05)].\n The elimination of sales commissions with an equal\nincrease of sales salaries will have no change on\noperating income because it will lead to the same\namount of money paid to salespeople.\nWith these new values for direct materials and direct labor, the\nincremental change in cost of goods sold and thus the\nincremental change in operating income can be calculated.\nDirect materials costs increased by $30,000 ($3,030,000 –\n$3,000,000), and direct labor costs increased by $63,000\n($1,863,000 – $1,800,000). The total increase in cost of goods\nsold is $93,000. An increase in expenses causes a decrease in\noperating income. Thus, operating income will decrease by\n$93,000 if the events listed occur.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"v2-su14-q506","volume":2,"unit":14,"unitTitle":"Budgeting — Calculations and Pro Forma Financial Statements","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) The controller of a store has asked a staff accountant to\nprepare detailed reports that summarize the firm’s cash flows\nfor the upcoming accounting period and cash position at the\n\nStatements | Subunit 6: Pro Forma Financial Statements\nend of the period. Accordingly, the controller has requested\npreparation of a cash budget, a pro forma statement of cash\nflows, a detailed listing of cash collections from customers, and\na detailed listing of cash payments for merchandise purchases.\nWhich one of the following correctly identifies the first and\nlast document to be prepared by the accountant?\nFirst Document Last Document","options":["Listing of cash collections Pro forma statement of cash flows","Listing of cash collections Cash budget","Cash\nbudget\nEither the listing of cash collections or listing of cash\npayments, the order of which is unimportant","Listing of\ncash\npayments\nEither the pro forma statement of cash flows or\nthe cash budget, the order of which is\nunimportant\n\nAnalysis Overview\n15: (264) Cost and Variance Measures\n1: (16) Variance Analysis Overview\n2: (39) Static and Flexible Budget Variances\n3: (46) Direct Materials Variances\n4: (35) Direct Labor Variances\n5: (12) Mix and Yield Variances\n6: (67) Overhead Variances\n7: (18) Comprehensive Example\n8: (31) Sales Variances\n15: (264) Cost and Variance Measures\n1: (16) Variance Analysis Overview\nAnalysis Overview"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The listing of cash collections must be prepared first, since\ncash must be collected before it can be spent. The pro forma\nstatement of cash flows is the culmination of the entire\nbudgeting process.","sectionId":"14.6"},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.005_1805","question":"Techgear Inc. manufactures specialty suits for triathletes. For the month of January, it projects the following selling and administrative expenses: sales commissions, 4% of sales; shipping expense, $4,500; advertising and marketing costs payable next month, $12,000; electric and water utilities, $750; corporate office depreciation for computers, $8,800; bad debt expense, $8,000. Sales for the month of January equal $400,000. What is the total of cash costs that will be reported on the cash budget?","options":["$33,250","$41,250","$21,250","$50,050"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A cash budget is based on expected cash flows for a specific period of time, not accrual accounting expenses. Non-cash expenses such as depreciation expense and allowance for bad debts and expenses not to be paid in the current period are not included. In this example the cash outflows for January are $16,000 for sales commissions ($400,000 × 4%), $4,500 for shipping expense, and $750 for electric and water utilities. This is a total of $21,500. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.001_1805","question":"The Eccleston Company has the following budgeted sales: January $40,000, February $60,000, and March $50,000. 40% of the sales are for cash and 60% are on credit. For the credit sales, 50% are collected in the month of sale, and 50% the next month. What are the total expected cash receipts during March?","options":["$53,000","$54,000","$52,000","$50,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cash collections in March will come from three sources: cash sales in March, credit sales in March, and credit sales in February. The collections from March cash sales is $20,000 (40% × $50,000), the collections from March credit sales is $15,000 (60% × $50,000 × 50%), and the collections from February credit sales is $18,000 (60% × $60,000 × 50%). The total is $53,000. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.032_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Increasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 30 days to 45 days would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 30 days to 45 days would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Increasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 30 days to 45 days would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 30 days to 45 days would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash disbursements are mainly from cash payments for expenses and the payment of purchases made on credit. Increasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases would decrease expected cash disbursements as cash payments would be made more slowly, resulting in lower cash disbursements.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.027_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the use of a cash budget?","options":["The cash budget can be used to evaluate whether sufficient inventory will be on hand.","The cash budget can be used to evaluate whether sales targets will be met.","The cash budget can be used to predict the amount of current liabilities to be on hand at a particular point in time.","The cash budget can be used to determine the feasibility of increasing R&D expenditures during a year."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. This information can be used to determine the budgeted ending cash balance. Having an estimate of cash on hand can help a company determine the feasibility of increasing R&D expenditures during a year.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.011_1805","question":"Sophia Inc. is preparing its cash budget for the fourth quarter. The inventory manager advises that materials purchases will be $40,000 in October and will increase by 25% for each month thereafter because of the holiday season. Sophia's purchases in September were $32,000. Sophia pays for purchases 50% in the month after sale and 50% in the second month after the sale. What are Sophia's budgeted cash payments for purchases in November?","options":["$36,000","$45,000","$61,000","$40,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cash will be disbursed in November for two reasons: To pay 50% of October material purchases and to pay 50% of September material purchases. In November $20,000 will be expected to be paid for October purchases ($40,000 × 50%) and $16,000 for September purchases ($32,000 × 50%). These two add up to $36,000; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.014_1809","question":"Which of the following items would most likely be included in a company's capital expenditure budget?","options":["Spending for training.","Spending for travel.","Spending for purchasing equipment.","Spending for legal fees."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A capital expenditure budget focuses on capital projects. Capital projects are projects that are expected to provide benefits in the future from spending in the current year. Because of that, the evaluation of capital projects involves discounted cash flow analysis. Spending for purchasing equipment is a capital project as it involves cash flows for multiple years. The initial purchase would be an outflow in one year and the sale of product made on the equipment would be inflows in subsequent years. As a result, it would be a part of a company's capital expenditure budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.007_1802","question":"The following information is available for Water Bottle Company, a producer of durable reusable water bottles:","options":["$228,200 for January, $223,300 for February, and $242,900 for March","$285,250 for January, $279,125 for February, and $303,625 for March","$287,200 for January, $280,350 for February, and $298,725 for March","$280,000 for January, 273,000 for February, and 297,500 for March."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This solution requires first determining how much inventory needs to be purchased to support the budgeted sales volume. The budgeted purchases are then evaluated with respect to the payment policy for Water Bottle Company to determine budgeted purchase payments.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.028_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Increasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Increasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash receipts are mainly from cash sales and the collection of credit sales. Since cash sales result in cash receipts immediately, increasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.005_1802","question":"The sales revenue budget and direct material purchases budget for Want Best Corp are below. As Want Best Corp closes out its current year, management is planning a $40,000 ending balance in accounts receivable (net of uncollectible debt). Want Best Corp's budget policy assumes that 70% of sales will be collected in the current quarter, 25% will be collected in the subsequent quarter, and 5% will not be collected. Want Best Corp's direct material suppliers expect 80% of purchases to be paid in the current quarter, with 20% to be paid the subsequent quarter. Want Best Corp will close out its current year with a $36,000 ending balance in accounts payable.","options":["There will be no change in net cash because of the new budget policy.","$64,150","$13,800","$77,950"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The following is Want Best Corp's new sales budget and purchases budget under the new budget policy:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.013_1809","question":"Which of the following items would most likely be included in a company's capital expenditure budget?","options":["The purchase of food and beverages for an office party.","The purchase of a business.","The purchase of raw materials.","The purchase of copier machine paper."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A capital expenditure budget focuses on capital projects. Capital projects are projects that are expected to provide benefits in the future from spending in the current year. Because of that, the evaluation of capital projects involves discounted cash flow analysis. The purchase of a business is a capital project as it involves cash flows for multiple years. The initial purchase would be an outflow in one year and the net inflows from selling products would be inflows in subsequent years. As a result, it would be a part of a company's capital expenditure budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.002_1802","question":"Which of the following statements describes the relationship between the capital expenditure budget, the cash budget, and the pro forma financial statements?","options":["The pro forma balance sheet affects the cash budget, which affects the capital expenditure budget.","The cash budget affects the pro forma balance sheet, which affects the capital expenditure budget.","The capital expenditure budget affects the cash budget, which affects the pro forma balance sheet.","The cash budget affects the capital expenditure budget, which affects the pro forma balance sheet."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This statement correctly describes the relationship between the capital expenditure budget, the cash budget, and the pro forma financial statements.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.003_1802","question":"Which of the following budgets do not affect the cash budget?","options":["Direct materials and direct labor budgets","Sales and capital expenditures budgets","Manufacturing overhead and selling and admin budgets","All of the budgets listed above affect the cash budget."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The direct materials, direct labor, manufacturing overhead, selling and admin, sales, and capital expenditures budgets all affect the cash budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.021_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly explains the relationship between the cash budget, the pro forma income statement, and pro forma balance sheet?","options":["Information from the pro forma income statement and pro forma balance sheet are used to create the cash budget.","Information from the pro forma balance sheet and cash budget are used to create the pro forma income statement.","Information from the cash budget is used to create the pro forma income statement and pro forma balance sheet.","Information from the cash budget and pro forma income statement are used to create the pro forma balance sheet."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma income statement provides information on expected revenues, expenses, and income for a given period of time. The pro forma balance sheet provides information on expected balances for assets, liabilities, and equity as of the end of the period. A cash budget provides a summary of expected cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a period of time. The cash budget and pro forma income statement are used to create the pro forma balance sheet as these two statements provide the information needed to determine expected balances for assets, liabilities, and equity. For example, expected credit sales on the pro forma income statement can be combined with expected cash collections on the cash budget to determine the change in accounts receivable for a period.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.020_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly explains the relationship between the cash budget, the capital expenditure budget, and the pro forma statement of cash flows?","options":["All of the cash flows used to prepare the pro forma statement of cash flows are used to prepare the capital expenditure budget.","Some of the cash flows used to prepare the pro forma statement of cash flows are used to prepare the capital expenditure budget and some are used to prepare the cash budget.","All of the cash flows used to prepare the pro forma statement of cash flows are used to prepare the cash budget.","The cash flows used to prepare the pro forma statement of cash flows are different from the cash flows used to prepare the cash budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of expected cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a period of time. The pro forma statement of cash flows classifies all expected cash inflows and outflows from operating, investing, and financing activities for a period. All of the cash flows used to prepare the pro forma statement of cash flows are used to prepare the cash budget. The cash budget reconfigures all of the cash flows into expected receipts, disbursements, and financings.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.019_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly explains the relationship between the cash budget and capital expenditure budget?","options":["All of the cash outflows from the cash budget are included as outflows on the capital expenditure budget.","All of the cash outflows from the capital expenditure budget are included as outflows on the cash budget.","All of the cash inflows from the cash budget are included as outflows on the capital expenditure budget.","All of the cash inflows from the capital expenditure budget are included as outflows on the cash budget."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A capital expenditure budget focuses on capital projects. Capital projects are projects that are expected to provide benefits in the future from spending in the current year. A cash budget provides a summary of expected cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a period of time. All of the outflows from a capital expenditure budget are included on the cash budget as the cash budget includes expected cash outflows from all sources.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.035_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Decreasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Decreasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Decreasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Decreasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash disbursements are mainly from cash payments for expenses and the payment of purchases made on credit. Decreasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would increase expected cash disbursements as cash payments would be made more quickly if less inventory is purchased on credit, resulting in higher cash disbursements.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.016_1809","question":"All of the following items would most likely be included in a company's capital expenditure budgetexcept:","options":["Spending for utilities.","Spending to purchase a business.","Spending for adding a new product line.","Spending for purchasing equipment."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. A capital expenditure budget focuses on capital projects. Capital projects are projects that are expected to provide benefits in the future from spending in the current year. Because of that, the evaluation of capital projects involves discounted cash flow analysis. Spending for utilities is not a capital project as utilities would typically only involve cash flows for a single year. This means it would be a part of an operating budget, not a capital expenditure budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.025_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["The cash budget is prepared using only information from the direct materials budget, direct labor budget, and manufacturing overhead budget.","The cash budget is prepared using only information from the sales budget.","The cash budget is prepared using only information from the capital expenditures budget.","The cash budget is prepared using information from all parts of the master budget."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Since cash is needed in all parts of the organization, the cash budget is prepared using information from all parts of the master budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.004_1802","question":"Practical Pianos Company is analyzing their cash budget for the year. It appears that there won't be sufficient cash in the organization at a key point in the next year. Which of the following is not an approach that Practical Pianos Company can use to solve the cash need?","options":["Increase the amount of cash generated by operations or reduce the amount of cash used in operations.","Establish a less strict credit policy that will accelerate cash collections.","Take out a short-term loan.","Pressure suppliers to accept payments on a slower basis."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A less strict credit policy will actually slow down cash collections, which will increase the cash need. On the other hand, a stricter credit policy will accelerate cash collections, but perhaps at the expense of losing some sales. Hence, Practical Pianos will need to balance the impact of its credit policy on faster cash collections versus high volumes of sales. Establishing a less strict credit policy could result in a few additional sales; however, overall, cash collections will not accelerate.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.022_1809","question":"Which of the following pairs of financial statements are most similar?","options":["The pro forma income statement and pro forma statement of cash flows.","The pro forma statement of cash flows and cash budget.","The pro forma balance sheet and cash budget.","The pro forma income statement and cash budget."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma statement of cash flows classifies all expected cash inflows and outflows from operating, investing, and financing activities for a given period of time. A cash budget provides a summary of expected cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. The only difference between the two is how cash flows are classified. They are prepared using the same information. This means they are quite similar to each other.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.001_1802","question":"In order to prepare a capital expenditure budget, Ekin Shoes needs to determine the cash needed for production. Based on the following information, compute the cash budget for production for each of the next four quarters.","options":["$111,000,000 for the first quarter, $79,000,000 for the second quarter, $70,000,000 for the third quarter, and $53,400,000 for the fourth quarter","$111,000,000 for the first quarter, $79,000,000 for the second quarter, $70,000,000 for the third quarter, and $47,000,000 for the fourth quarter","$143,000,000 for the first quarter, $143,000,000 for the second quarter, $143,000,000 for the third quarter, and $143,000,000 for the fourth quarter","$88,000,000 for the first quarter, 79,000,000 for the second quarter, $47,000,000 for the third quarter, and $53,400,000 for the fourth quarter"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The following is Ekin's capital expenditure schedule for the year:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.030_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Reducing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 45 days to 30 days would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Reducing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 45 days to 30 days would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Reducing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 45 days to 30 days would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Reducing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 45 days to 30 days would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash receipts are mainly from cash sales and the collection of credit sales. Reducing the time for customers to pay for purchases would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget as credit sales would be collected more quickly, resulting in higher cash receipts.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.029_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Decreasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Decreasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Decreasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Decreasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash receipts are mainly from cash sales and the collection of credit sales. Since cash sales result in cash receipts immediately, decreasing the percentage of cash sales relative to credit sales would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.002_1805","question":"The Brenneman Company's direct materials budget shows total cost of direct materials purchases for January $125,000, February $150,000 and March $175,000. Cash payments are 60% in the month of purchase and 40% in the following month. What are the budgeted cash payments for March?","options":["$160,000","$150,000","$130,000","$165,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cash collections in March will come from two sources: March credit sales collected in March and February credit sales collected in March. The collections from March credit sales is $105,000 (60% × $175,000) and the collections from February credit sales is $60,000 (40% × $150,000). The total is $165,000. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.ce.cb.006_1802","question":"Bright Lights Company uses the following budget policy to plan for cash receipts on sales:","options":["$56,950 cash collections during May. $70,000 June 30 balance in accounts receivable.","$81,300 cash collections during May. $38,950 June 30 balance in accounts receivable.","$82,800 cash collections during May. $45,150 June 30 balance in accounts receivable.","$85,000 cash collections during May. $109,350 June 30 balance in accounts receivable."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Solving for total cash collections in May requires working backwards to identify previous months’ sales that are subsequently collected in May. Solving for the June 30 accounts receivable balance requires evaluating how much sales revenue remains from the previous three months of the year that can still be collected. Note, for example, that 67% of June sales are collected in June. However, that doesn't mean that 33% of June sales exist in the net balance of accounts receivable because 2% of June sales is not expected to be collected.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.026_1809","question":"All of the following statements are correct concerning the use of a cash budget except:","options":["The cash budget can be used to help determine whether a company will be compliant with bond covenants.","The cash budget can be used to help determine whether a company will need external financing during a time period.","The cash budget can be used to determine whether a company will have satisfactory profitability.","The cash budget can be used to determine if cash will be available at the end of the period to pay back a loan."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. The pro forma income statement, not the cash budget, can be used to determine whether a company will have satisfactory profitability.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.034_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Increasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Increasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash disbursements are mainly from cash payments for expenses and the payment of purchases made on credit. Increasing the percentage of inventory purchased on credit relative to inventory purchased for cash would decrease expected cash disbursements as cash payments would be made more slowly if more inventory is purchased on credit, resulting in lower cash disbursements.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.015_1809","question":"Which of the following items would most likely be included in a company's capital expenditure budget?","options":["Spending for utilities.","Spending for labor.","Spending for adding a new product line.","Spending for administrative fees."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A capital expenditure budget focuses on capital projects. Capital projects are projects that are expected to provide benefits in the future from spending in the current year. Because of that, the evaluation of capital projects involves discounted cash flow analysis. Spending for adding a new product line is a capital project as it involves cash flows for multiple years. The initial spending would be an outflow in one year and the sale of product would be inflows in subsequent years. As a result, it would be a part of a company's capital expenditure budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.031_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Increasing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 30 days to 45 days would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 30 days to 45 days would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Increasing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 30 days to 45 days would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Increasing the time for customers to pay for purchases from 30 days to 45 days would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash receipts are mainly from cash sales and the collection of credit sales. Increasing the time for customers to pay for purchases would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget as credit sales would be collected more slowly, resulting in lower cash receipts.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.033_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning the cash budget?","options":["Decreasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 45 days to 35 days would increase expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Decreasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 45 days to 35 days would increase expected cash disbursements in a cash budget.","Decreasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 45 days to 35 days would decrease expected cash receipts in a cash budget.","Decreasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases from 45 days to 35 days would decrease expected cash disbursements in a cash budget."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. Cash disbursements are mainly from cash payments for expenses and the payment of purchases made on credit. Decreasing the time a company takes to pay for credit purchases would increase expected cash disbursements as cash payments would be made more quickly, resulting in higher cash disbursements.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.024_1809","question":"All of the following are a part of a cash budget except:","options":["Budgeted cash inflows.","Budgeted cash outflows.","Budgeted credit sales.","Budgeted ending cash balance."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. This information can be used to determine whether it will need to obtain additional cash during the time period and its budgeted ending cash balance. While budgeted credit sales are used to determine budgeted cash inflows, they are not an actual part of the cash budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.023_1809","question":"All of the following are a reason for a company to prepare a cash budget except:","options":["To determine whether a company will need external financing during a time period.","To determine the expected cash balance at the end of a period.","To determine whether a company will need to liquidate investments during a time period.","To determine what the targeted ending cash balance should be."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A cash budget provides a summary of budgeted cash inflows and outflows from all sources for a given period of time. While a company can use a cash budget to determine its expected ending cash balance, management determines what the targeted ending cash balance should be. If the expected balance before additional cash is less than the targeted balance, additional cash will be needed. If it is higher, excess cash can be invested or used for other purposes.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.ce.cb.006_1805","question":"Home Health manufactures medical supplies. For the month of April, it projects the following selling and administrative expenses: sales commissions, 8% of sales; shipping expense, $12,800; advertising and marketing costs payable next month, $4,200; electric and water utilities, $1,900; equipment depreciation, $18,700; bad debt expense, $3,000. Sales for the month of April equal $694,000. What is the total of cash costs that will be reported on the cash budget?","options":["$597,880","$96,120","$623,780","$70,220"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A cash budget is based on expected cash flows for a specific period of time, not accrual accounting expenses. Non-cash expenses such as depreciation expense and allowance for bad debts and expenses not to be paid in the current period are not included. In this example the cash outflows for April are $55,520 for sales commissions ($694,000 × 8%), $12,800 for shipping expense, and $1,900 for electric and water utilities. This is a total of $70,220. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.4","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.007_1802","question":"Which of the following best describes the mathematical relationship between sales volume and production when inventory is present in the budget?","options":["Production volume = Sales volume + Ending inventory – Beginning inventory","Production volume – Beginning inventory = Sales volume + Ending inventory","Production volume + Ending inventory = Sales volume – Beginning inventory","Production volume = Sales volume × Ending inventory – Beginning inventory"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer describes the relationship between sales volume and production when inventory is present in the budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.005_1805","question":"Why is a company's sales budget developed before the production budget?","options":["The company needs to find ways to sell as many units as it can maximally produce.","The company's sales managers have more control over the company's finances than the production managers.","The sales budget is more important than the production budget, so it receives a larger portion of the company's financial reserves.","The company only wants to produce as many units as it expects to sell."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The sales budget is used to determine expected revenue. It is based on the sales forecast (expected units to be sold) and expected selling prices. By developing the production budget after the sales budget, a company can produce only as many units as it expects to sell (before any changes in inventory). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.025_1809","question":"All of the following statements concerning the relationship between the sales budget and the annual profit plan are correct except:","options":["The sales budget is an input for preparing the selling expense budget.","Without an accurate sales budget a company is less likely to have an accurate annual profit plan.","It does not matter when the sales budget is developed while preparing the annual profit plan as it only impacts budgeted sales revenue.","The sales budget is an input for preparing the production budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A sales budget is an estimate of expected unit sales and expected selling prices. The two factors are combined to determine budgeted sales revenue. In addition, the expected unit sales figure is used to determine the production budget as well as the selling expense budget. Therefore, it needs to be prepared at the beginning of the annual profit plan development process.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.022_1805","question":"In early February, Heavenly Swimwear is preparing the summer season swimsuits budget. Budgeted sales are 47,600 suits for May, 58,200 suits for June, and 43,500 suits for July. Each suit requires 1.3 yards of fabric. Heavenly Swimwear requires ending Finished Goods inventory equal to 30% of the following month's budgeted sales. What is Heavenly Swimwear's budgeted production for May?","options":["47,600 units","65,060 units","50,780 units","44,420 units"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed for Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory – Beginning Inventory.” Based on expected June sales, target ending inventory for May is 17,460 units (30% × 58,200). Based on expected May sales, beginning inventory for May is 14,280 units (30% × 47,600). Putting this all together yields planned production of 50,780 units in May (47,600 + 17,460 – 14,280). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.002_1805","question":"The ________ is derived from the _________.","options":["sales budget, sales forecast","sales forecast, sales budget","production budget, budgeted income statement","production budget, direct labor budget"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sales forecast is the starting point for the master budget process. It is the basis for the sales budget since the sales budget is based on the sales forecast and expected selling prices. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.027_1809","question":"Each of following factors is likely to be considered when preparing a sales budget except:","options":["Expected advertising and marketing expenditures.","Historical sales trends.","Expected competitor actions.","Desired ending inventory level."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A sales budget is an estimate of expected unit sales and expected selling prices. Desired ending inventory level impacts the production budget, not the sales budget. It is not likely to impact expected sales and expected selling prices.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.002_1802","question":"Organizations invest significant time and resources researching the market industry, economic environment, and competitive landscape to reduce uncertainty around the sales forecast. Which of the following is not one of the common tools employed in this process?","options":["Leading economic indicators","Cross-functional research teams","Sophisticated analysis of small, limited data sets","External consultants"],"answer":"C","explanation":"While analysis of small data sets can be helpful, a better insight comes from sophisticated analysis of large data sets—both internal data sets and external data sets. This is sometimes called big data analytics.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.005_1802","question":"Card & Co. (CC), a creator of popular card games such as “Tres” and “Go Hunt,” is preparing a quarterly sales budget. CC predicts sales of 40,000 games in Q1 at $5.00 per game. CC predicts that game sales will grow by 4% each quarter and that game price will increase by 5% each quarter. Each game costs $3.00 to produce in Q1; however, costs will grow by 3% each quarter. Using a quarterly sales budget, calculate sales revenue for the year.","options":["$800,000","$917,306","$169,859","$383,598"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The following is CC's quarterly sales budget for the year:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.024_1809","question":"Which statement is most correct concerning the role of the sales budget in the development of an accurate annual profit plan?","options":["Having an accurate sales budget ensures a company of having an accurate annual profit plan.","Without an accurate sales budget a company is less likely to have an accurate annual profit plan.","It is more likely for a company to have an accurate annual profit plan with an inaccurate sales budget.","The accuracy of a company's annual profit plan is unrelated to the accuracy of its sales forecast."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A sales budget is an estimate of expected unit sales and expected selling prices. The two factors are combined to determine budgeted sales revenue. The expected unit sales figure is used to determine the production budget as well as the selling expense budget. If the expected unit sales figure is not accurate, these other budgets are also less likely to be accurate. This makes an accurate annual profit plan less likely.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.007_1805","question":"What effect does an increase in the production budget have on the sales budget?","options":["An increase in the production budget will increase the sales budget by the same amount.","An increase in the production budget has no effect on the sales budget.","An increase in the production budget will decrease the sales budget by the same amount.","An increase in the production budget will increase the sales budget by double the amount."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The production budget is based on the sales budget (the number of units expected to be sold), expected beginning inventory, and targeted ending inventory. As a result, an increase in the production budget has no effect on the sales budget. An increase in the sales budget results in an increase in the production budget. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.023_1805","question":"The Crawford Company has 3,000 units in beginning finished goods. The sales budget shows expected sales to be 12,000 units. If the production budget shows that 14,000 units are required for production, what was the desired ending finished goods?","options":["1,000","3,000","23,000","5,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Units to be produced is calculated as “Expected Sales + Target Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” Rearranging the formula results in Targeted Ending Inventory calculated as “Production + Beginning Inventory − Expected Sales.” In this example targeted ending inventory is 5,000 units (14,000 + 3,000 − 12,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.004_1802","question":"Card & Co. (CC), a creator of popular card games such as “Tres” and “Go Hunt,” is preparing a quarterly sales budget. CC predicts sales of 40,000 games in Q1, 45,000 in Q2, 45,000 in Q3, and 90,000 in Q4, CC plans to hold the price of each game constant at $5.00 for the whole year. Each game costs $3.00 to produce. Using a quarterly sales budget, calculate sales revenue for the year.","options":["$220,000","$440,000","$200,000","$1,100,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The following is CC's quarterly sales budget for the year:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.006_1805","question":"If the sales budget increases by 10%, the production budget:","options":["Is likely to increase by the same number of units as the sales budget","Is not likely to increase by the same number of units as the sales budget","Is not likely to be affected","Is likely to decrease by the same number of units as the sales budget"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The production budget is based on the sales budget (the number of units expected to be sold), expected beginning inventory, and targeted ending inventory. As the number of units expected to be sold increases, the number of units expected to be produced increases by the same number of units (assuming expected beginning inventory and targeted ending inventory remain the same). This ensures production will satisfy expected sales. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.021_1805","question":"In early March, Percy's Pickled Snacks is preparing the upcoming quarter's budget for pickled beets. Budgeted sales are 12,000 jars for April, 16,000 jars for May, and 19,000 jars for June. Each jar requires 1.2 pounds of beets and sells for $15. Percy requires ending finished goods inventory equal to 25% of the following month's budgeted sales. What is Percy's budgeted production for April?","options":["12,000 units","13,000 units","16,000 units","11,000 units"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed for Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory – Beginning Inventory.” Based on expected May sales, target ending inventory for April is 4,000 units (25% × 16,000). Based on expected April sales, beginning inventory for April is 3,000 units (25% × 12,000). Putting this all together yields planned production of 13,000 units in April (12,000 + 4,000 – 3,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.008_1805","question":"The Burlington Company has 12,000 units in beginning finished goods. If sales are expected to be 60,000 units for the year and Burlington desires ending finished goods of 15,000 units, how many units must Burlington produce?","options":["57,000","60,000","63,000","75,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Units to be produced is calculated as “Expected Sales + Target Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” In this example 63,000 units are to be produced (60,000 + 15,000 − 12,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.011_1805","question":"Spartan Bicycles forecasts sales of 12,000 bikes for June and 14,000 bikes for July. Its inventory at the end of May stands at 3,000 bikes. If Spartan plans to keep an inventory of 25% of July sales at the end of June, how many bikes should it manufacture for the month of June?","options":["12,000","11,500","13,000","12,500"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed for Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” In this example the targeted ending inventory for June is 25% of expected July sales or 3,500 units (25% × 14,000). The ending inventory for May is the same as the beginning inventory for June. Putting it all together yields planned production of 12,500 units (12,000 + 3,500 − 3,000) for June; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.034_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the impact of finished goods inventory on the production budget?","options":["A decrease in beginning finished goods inventory decreases the units needed to be produced.","An increase in desired ending inventory of finished goods inventory does not impact the units needed to be produced.","A decrease in desired ending inventory of finished goods inventory decreases the units needed to be produced.","An increase in beginning finished goods inventory does not impact the units needed to be produced."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A production budget is an estimate of expected production for a period. It is based on expected unit sales, beginning finished goods inventory, and desired ending inventory of finished goods. Desired ending inventory of finished goods is one component of the total finished goods units needed in a period (expected sales is the other). As desired ending inventory of finished goods inventory decreases, the units needed to be produced also decreases.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.009_1805","question":"Sam Montana manufactures bookcases for colleges and universities to use in dorm rooms. Each bookcase sells for $65. Due to the cyclical nature of the business, the company budgets two months ahead. In early May, it is planning for July, its busiest month. The company plans to sell 114,500 bookcases in July. At the end of June, it expects to have 26,000 assembled bookcases in inventory, and it would like to have 3,000 bookcases in inventory at the end of July. What is the company's budgeted sales revenue for July?","options":["$5,947,500","$7,442,500","$9,132,500","$1,690,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Each bookcase is expected to sell for $65 and a total of 114,500 bookcases are expected to be sold. This results in budgeted sales revenue of $7,442,500 (114,500 × $65). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.026_1809","question":"Which statement is correct concerning preparing a sales budget?","options":["Sales forecasts should only be based on statistical analysis techniques such as regression analysis and time series analysis.","Sales forecasts should only be based on qualitative analysis techniques such as sales managers’ knowledge about the market and customer needs.","Sales forecasts should be based on both statistical analysis and qualitative analysis techniques.","Last year's sales should be used as the sales forecast for the current year."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A sales budget is an estimate of expected unit sales and expected selling prices. One way to determine expected unit sales is to use statistical analysis techniques such as regression analysis and time series analysis. These techniques have the advantage of being able to incorporate a large amount of data in an objective way. In addition, qualitative techniques that rely on sales managers’ judgment and experience are also useful as these can provide information that statistical techniques cannot. Using both of these types of techniques results in better forecasts than relying on only one type of technique.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.031_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the relationship between the sales budget and the production budget?","options":["The sales budget is prepared after the production budget.","The production budget is prepared after the sales budget.","The production budget is used to prepare the sales budget.","The production budget and the sales budget are the same things."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A sales budget is an estimate of expected sales revenue for a period. It is based on expected unit sales and expected selling prices. The expected units to be sold is used to prepare the production budget as it is one component of production needs. The other two components are beginning inventory and desired ending inventory. This means the production budget is prepared after the sales budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.015_1805","question":"Boots Unlimited has budgeted for cowboy boots sales of $2,783,104 in October. It wants to start every month with inventory equal to 15 days’ worth of the next month's sales (based on a 30-day month). September sales are projected at $2,550,016. The sales price is $128 per pair. Inventory at the beginning of September is estimated at 9,960 pairs of cowboy boots. How many pairs of cowboy boots should Boots Unlimited manufacture in September?","options":["30,794 pairs","10,872 pairs","19,010 pairs","20,834 pairs"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed tor Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” A total of 19,922 pairs are needed to satisfy September sales ($2,550,016 ÷ $128). Based on expected October sales of 21,743 pairs ($2,783,104 ÷ $128), target ending inventory for September is 10,872 pairs (15 days of October sales). Putting this all together yields planned production of 20,834 pairs in September (19,922 + 10,872 − 9,960). This is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.009_1802","question":"Card & Co. (CC), a creator of popular card games such as “Tres” and “Go Hunt,” is preparing a quarterly production budget. CC predicts sales of 45,000 games in Q1 and predicts that game sales will grow 6% every quarter indefinitely. The company constantly follows a budget policy of maintaining an ending inventory of 20% of the next quarter's sales. Using a quarterly production budget, calculate production volume for the year.","options":["199,220","208,220","196,858","238,591"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The following is CC's quarterly production budget for the year. Q1 Planned Ending Inventory is calculated as 47,700 Q2 Sales Volume × 20% = 9,540. Q2 Sales Volume is calculated as 45,000 Q1 Sales Volume × 1.06 = 47,700. Q1 Beginning Inventory is the ending inventory from last year's Q4. Even though this number is not provided, it can be calculated because CC constantly follows the budget policy of maintaining an ending inventory of 20% of the next quarter's sales. Q1 Beginning Inventory is calculated as 45,000 × 20% = 9,000. Follow this same pattern for all quarters. Note: Sales volume in Q1 of the following year, necessary for computing ending inventory in Q4, will be 53,596 units × 1.06 = 56,812 units, following the same assumption of 6% growth in game sales.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.006_1802","question":"Which of the following best describes the relationship between the sales budget and the production budget?","options":["The sales budget is dependent on the production budget.","The sales budget and production budget in turn affect each other. A change in one will change the other.","The production budget is dependent on the sales budget.","There is no relationship between the sales budget and the production budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This statement describes the relationship between the sales budget and the production budget. The production budget is often relatively simple to assemble once the sales budget is complete. Whatever is needed for the budgeted sales volume is then scheduled to be produced.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.014_1805","question":"Quality Foods has budgeted $1,500,000 in sales of ginger chicken for May. It wants to start every month with inventory equal to 10 days’ worth of the next month's sales (based on a 30-day month). April sales are projected at $1,400,000. The sales price is $5 per box. Inventory at the beginning of April is estimated at 93,400 boxes of ginger chicken. How many boxes of ginger chicken should Quality Foods manufacture in April?","options":["280,000 boxes","273,400 boxes","286,600 boxes","300,000 boxes"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed for Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” A total of 280,000 boxes are needed to satisfy April sales ($1,400,000 ÷ $5). Based on expected May sales of 300,000 boxes ($1,500,000 ÷ $5), target ending inventory for April is 100,000 boxes (10 days of May sales). Putting it all together yields planned production of 286,600 boxes in April (280,000 + 100,000 − 93,400). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.033_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the impact of finished goods inventory on the production budget?","options":["An increase in beginning finished goods inventory increases the units needed to be produced.","An increase in desired ending inventory of finished goods inventory decreases the units needed to be produced.","An increase in desired ending inventory of finished goods inventory increases the units needed to be produced.","A decrease in beginning finished goods inventory decreases the units needed to be produced."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A production budget is an estimate of expected production for a period. It is based on expected unit sales, beginning finished goods inventory, and desired ending inventory of finished goods. Desired ending inventory of finished goods is one component of the total finished goods units needed in a period (expected sales is the other). As desired ending inventory of finished goods inventory increases, the units needed to be produced also increases.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.010_1805","question":"Sewing Masters produces curtains and sells each curtain for $40. Sewing Masters often sees a spike in sales in March and April due to spring cleaning. The company plans to sell 257,000 curtains in the month of April. At the end of March, it expects to have 52,000 curtains in inventory, and it would like to have 15,000 curtains in inventory at the end of April. What is the company's budgeted sales revenue for April?","options":["$8,800,000","$11,760,000","$10,280,000","$9,680,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Budgeted revenue is calculated as “Budgeted Unit Sales × Budgeted Selling Price.” Each curtain is expected to sell for $40 and a total of 257,000 curtains are expected to be sold. This results in budgeted sales revenue of $10,280,000 (257,000 × $40). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.003_1802","question":"Sales volumes and prices that make up revenue need to be carefully forecasted; however, they can be impacted significantly by many external factors. Which of the following is not one of those external factors?","options":["Market opportunities for the organization","Improper sales budget planning","Legal factors","Supplier power"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Improper sales budget planning could impact sales volumes and prices; however, this is an internal factor, not an external factor.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.013_1805","question":"Gulfcoast Shrimp Packaging supplies frozen shrimp to grocery chains. It expects sales of 34 tons of shrimp in the next quarter. It currently has an inventory of 15 tons of shrimp. If production of shrimp adds up to 26 tons in the upcoming quarter, what will be the company's inventory at the end of the quarter?","options":["8 tons.","7 tons.","0 tons.","An indeterminate level."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed for Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” Rearranging these figures results in target ending inventory being calculated as “Planned Production − Units Needed for Sales + Beginning Inventory.” In this example the targeted ending inventory is 7 tons (26 − 34 + 15). This is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.018_1805","question":"Sam Montana Bookcases (SMB) manufactures bookcases for sale to colleges and universities for use in dorm rooms. Each bookcase sells for $65. Due to the cyclical nature of the business, the company budgets two months ahead. In early May, company managers are planning for July, the company's busiest month. SMB plans to sell 114,500 bookcases in July. The company expects to have 26,000 assembled bookcases in its inventory at the end of June. It also plans to have 560,000 board feet of lumber (cost $6,720,000), 75,000 quarts of stain (cost $300,000), and 160,000 board feet of trim (cost $2,400,000) in inventory. The company would like to have 3,000 assembled bookcases, 120,000 board feet of lumber, 4,500 quarts of stain, and 12,000 board feet of trim on hand at the end of July. What is SMB's budgeted production for July?","options":["143,500","88,500","91,500","85,500"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Planned production is calculated as “Units Needed for Sales + Targeted Ending Inventory − Beginning Inventory.” In July 114,500 bookcases are needed to support expected sales. Targeted ending inventory is 3,000 bookcases and beginning inventory is 26,000 bookcases. Putting this all together yields planned production of 91,500 bookcases (114,500 + 3,000 − 26,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.001_1802","question":"Which of the following specific budgets is the key driving force of the overall operational budget?","options":["The sales budget","The production budget","The capital projects budget","The pro forma income statement"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sales budget is the key driving force of the operational budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.001_1805","question":"Hailey has been tasked with overseeing the development of the company's master budget. This is the first time Hailey has had this role, and she's not sure where to start. What advice would you give her? Why?","options":["She should help develop the cash budget before the production budget because understanding cash flows will help determine the level of production.","She should help develop the direct material and direct labor budgets before the production budget because the production budget is just the sum of the direct material and direct labor budgets.","She should help develop the selling and administrative budget before developing the manufacturing overhead budget because selling and administrative expenses are part of manufacturing overhead.","She should help develop the sales budget before the production budget because production will depend on the number of sales."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The master budget is the overall budget for an organization. It comprises an operating budget (expected day-to-day activities) and a financial budget (expected sources and uses of cash). Two important sub-budgets are the sales budget and the production budget. The production budget is based on expected sales, expected beginning inventory, and targeted ending inventory; therefore, the sales budget must be developed before the production budget can be developed. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.032_1809","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the impact of finished goods inventory on the production budget?","options":["Beginning finished goods inventory increases budgeted production while desired ending inventory of finished goods decreases budgeted production.","Beginning finished goods inventory and desired ending inventory of finished goods both increase budgeted production.","Finished goods inventory levels do not impact the production budget.","Beginning finished goods inventory decreases budgeted production while desired ending inventory of finished goods increases budgeted production."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. A production budget is an estimate of expected production for a period. It is based on expected unit sales, beginning finished goods inventory, and desired ending inventory of finished goods. The amount of beginning finished goods inventory decreases budgeted production as these units can be used to satisfy finished goods needs. In addition, desired ending inventory of finished goods increases budgeted production as this increases the need for finished goods.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.sp.bud.008_1802","question":"Card & Co. (CC), a creator of popular card games such as “Tres” and “Go Hunt,” is preparing a quarterly production budget. The sales forecast for game sales is provided below:","options":["202,000","208,000","232,300","200,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The following is CC's quarterly production budget for the year.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.sp.bud.028_1809","question":"Which of the following correctly lists the components of a sales budget?","options":["Expected unit sales and expected selling prices.","Expected unit sales, beginning inventory, and desired ending inventory.","Expected production needs, beginning inventory, and desired ending inventory.","Expected selling prices and desired ending inventory."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. A sales budget is an estimate of expected sales revenue for a period. It is based on expected unit sales and expected selling prices. These are multiplied together to determine expected sales revenue.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.021_1805","question":"For fiscal year 20x6, Nancy calculated the following costs for Choco-rama's manufacturing process:","options":["$10,013,419","$10,405,419","$9,998,145","$9,589,895"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured = Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period − Ending Work in Process Inventory. Manufacturing costs incurred during the period = Direct materials + Direct labor + Manufacturing overhead. Direct materials used during 20x6 = Beginning Raw Materials + Raw Materials Purchased – Ending Raw Materials or $42,385 + $387,521 − $44,299 = $385,607 Direct labor for 20x6 = 388,400 hours × $23.60 per hour = $9,166,240 Manufacturing overhead consists of manufacturing costs other than direct materials and direct labor. For Choco-rama manufacturing overhead in 20x6 = factory oversight of $408,250 + factory utilities of $18,500 + factory depreciation of $9,700 + factory repairs of $15,400 = $451,850 The total manufacturing costs for 20x6 are $385,607 + $9,166,240 + $451,850 = $10,003,697 Putting this into the cost of goods manufactured formula results in cost of goods manufactured of $9,998,145 ($22,655 + $10,003,697 − $28,207). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.043_1809","question":"The WEQ Company currently has a contribution margin of $50 per unit. It is considering decreasing shipping costs from $9 per unit to $5 per unit and increasing fixed administrative expenses from $7 per unit to $12 per unit. If both of these changes are implemented, what will be WEQ's new contribution margin per unit?","options":["$49 per unit.","$54 per unit.","$45 per unit.","$50 per unit."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. If WEQ decreases shipping costs by $4 per unit then its contribution margin will increase by $4 per unit. The increase in administrative expenses will not change the contribution margin per unit as fixed costs (like administrative expenses) are not a part of the contribution margin. This means these two actions will increase the contribution margin to $54 per unit ($50 + $4).","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.024_1805","question":"Major Industries manufactures iPad covers. For 20x7 it had the following amounts related to its most popular product, Hercules: beginning finished goods inventory $24,000, cost of goods manufactured $96,000, and cost of goods sold $80,000. What was the amount of ending finished goods inventory?","options":["$8,000","$40,000","$24,000","$16,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is defined as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured − Ending Finished Goods Inventory.” Rearranging the figures results in ending finished goods inventory being calculated as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured − Cost of Goods Sold.” Using the amounts for Major results in ending finished goods inventory of $40,000 ($24,000 + $96,000 − $80,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.005_1805","question":"How do the direct materials budget, direct labor budget, and manufacturing overhead budget relate to the budgeted income statement?","options":["They are incorporated into the selling price that is used to calculate the sales of the income statement.","They are incorporated into the machine hours that are used to calculate the selling and administrative expenses of the income statement.","They are incorporated into the unit cost that is used to calculate the cost of goods sold of the income statement.","They are incorporated into the variable expenses that are used to calculate the expenses of the income statement."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The direct materials, direct labor, and manufacturing overhead budgets all track manufacturing costs as inventory moves through the production process and is eventually sold. These items eventually flow to the cost of goods sold budget, which then gets incorporated into the budgeted income statement. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.036_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning total contribution margin?","options":["Total contribution margin represents the selling price from an individual unit left over after paying the variable costs related to producing and selling that individual unit.","Total contribution margin represents the total revenue left over after paying all the variable costs incurred in a period.","Total contribution margin represents the selling price from an individual unit left over after paying the costs related to producing that individual unit.","Total contribution margin represents the total revenue left over after paying all the production costs of goods sold during a period."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. One type of cost is a variable cost. Examples include direct materials, direct labor, and sales commissions. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after variable costs have been paid. The total revenue left over after paying all variable costs incurred in a period is the total contribution margin.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.002_1802","question":"Ledd Computer Company is launching a new line of laptops. Production and sales begin in January. The sales and production budgets for the first two months are provided below, along with the standard cost sheets for each month.","options":["$321,275","$245,875","$240,500","$315,900"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The following is the February COGS budget for Ledd Computer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.012_1805","question":"Sewing Masters produces curtains. Each curtain sells for $40. The company plans to sell 257,000 curtains in the month of April. Sewing Masters’ salespeople earn a 6% commission on all sales and shipping costs are $2.59 per curtain. Administrative salaries total $118,000 annually, and advertising is $48,000 annually. What are the company's budgeted selling and administrative expenses for April?","options":["$1,282,430","$679,463","$1,448,430","$1,296,263"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Selling and administrative expenses include a company's non-manufacturing costs. They can be variable, fixed, or mixed costs. In this example variable selling and administrative expenses per curtain are budgeted to be $4.99 ($2.59 + commissions of $2.40, which is 6% of $40). The variable selling and administrative expenses for 257,000 curtains would be budgeted as $1,282,430 (257,000 × $4.99 per curtain). Annual total fixed selling and administrative expenses would be budgeted as $166,000 ($118,000 + $48,000). On a monthly basis that would be $13,833 ($166,000 ÷ 12). The sum of the budgeted variable and fixed selling and administrative expenses would be $1,296,263 for the month of April ($1,282,430 + $13,833). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.053_1809","question":"Which of the following statements about contribution margin is correct?","options":["A decrease in variable administrative expenses per unit will not impact the contribution margin per unit, while a decrease in variable manufacturing expenses per unit will increase the contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in variable administrative expenses per unit will increase the contribution margin per unit, while a decrease in variable manufacturing expenses per unit will not impact the contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in variable administrative expenses per unit and a decrease in variable manufacturing expenses per unit will both have no impact on the contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in variable administrative expenses per unit and a decrease in variable manufacturing expenses per unit will both increase the contribution margin per unit."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid, regardless of what type of activity the costs relate to. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit, while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. A decrease in any variable expense will increase the contribution margin per unit as a smaller per-unit amount will be subtracted from the sales price per unit when calculating contribution margin per unit.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.009_1802","question":"Given the following information for Every Tool's line of wrenches, create the budgeted operating statement and the budgeted contribution margin statement. Then reconcile the difference between the two statements. What is the reconciled difference? Note that Every Tool sells each wrench for $15.00.","options":["There is no difference between the two statements.","$1,000","$500","$1,640"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The following is Every Tool's budgeted operating statement, budgeted contribution margin statement, and reconciliation of the difference. *Fixed production overhead is 37,500 × $2.00 = $75,000. Add this amount to fixed selling & admin overhead of $48,000 to get $123,000.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.003_1805","question":"Edward is trying to calculate the cost of goods manufactured. He knows the total manufacturing costs and the ending work in process inventory. What value is he missing?","options":["The beginning work in process inventory","The raw materials inventory","The raw materials purchased","The finished goods inventory"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured is defined as the manufacturing cost of goods finished during an accounting period. It is also the amount transferred out of work-in-process inventory during an accounting period. It is defined as “Beginning Work-In-Process + Total Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period − Ending Work-In-Process.” Since Edward knows total manufacturing costs and ending work-in-process inventory, he still needs to know the beginning work-in-process inventory; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.020_1805","question":"Cyclone Industries reported the following information: beginning work in process inventory, $128,000; cost of goods manufactured, $1,200,000; beginning finished good inventory, $80,000; ending work in process inventory, $64,000; and ending finished goods inventory, $64,000. What is Cyclone's cost of goods sold for the year?","options":["$1,264,000","$1,216,000","$1,280,000","$1,184,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is defined as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured – Ending Finished Goods Inventory.” Using the numbers for Cyclone results in cost of goods sold of $1,216,000 ($80,000 + $1,200,000 − $64,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.008_1802","question":"Ironing Board Builders (IBB) is a small manufacturer of premium end ironing boards. IBB wants to create a budgeted operating statement to calculate operating income. IBB sells each ironing board for $20 and production costs are the same this year as they were last year. The following information is available:","options":["$180,000","$150,000","$62,000","$92,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The following is IBB's budgeted operating statement.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.034_1805","question":"Which of the following items is correctly paired with its location?","options":["Indirect labor – Direct labor budget","Indirect materials – Selling and administrative expense budget","Units to be produced – Manufacturing overhead budget","Budgeted sales in units – Selling and administrative expense budget"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The selling and administrative budget details expected spending on non-manufacturing items. Some of these expenses are variable, some are fixed, and some are mixed. The variable and mixed ones are at least partially based on sales. This means budgeted sales in units must be a part of the selling and administrative budget. Without it, the variable and mixed costs could not be budgeted. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.051_1809","question":"Which of the following statements about operating budgets is correct?","options":["Components of an operating budget include the sales budget, cost of goods sold budget, and cash budget.","Components of an operating budget include the sales budget, capital budget, and selling and administrative expense budget.","Components of an operating budget include the sales budget, cost of goods sold budget, and selling and administrative expense budget.","Components of an operating budget include the cash budget, capital budget, and sales budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. An operating budget is one part of the master budget. It is a plan for all of the company's day-to-day operations. It covers budgeted revenues and expenses. It includes the sales budget, the cost of goods sold budget, and the selling and administrative expense budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.047_1809","question":"Which of the following statements about contribution margin is correct?","options":["A decrease in variable administrative expenses per unit will decrease contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in fixed administrative expenses per unit will not impact contribution margin per unit.","An increase in variable administrative expenses per unit will increase contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in variable administrative expenses per unit will not impact contribution margin per unit."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. Since the choice involves a change in fixed administrative expenses, there will be no impact on the contribution margin per unit.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.005_1802","question":"Pretty Paints Products Company mixes and cans paints for supermarkets across the country. Pretty Paints’ sales representatives get a commission of $100 per pallet (20 one-gallon paint cans) that is sold at a price of $1,200. The variable costs to produce each pallet of paint is $410 (direct materials, direct labor, and variable overhead). In order to transport pallets to supermarkets, Pretty Paints must pay $60 for each pallet. The salaries of the administrative staff and base salaries of sales representatives total $374,525. The salaries of the production line managers total $135,650. Depreciation of the administration building is $10,500 and depreciation of the manufacturing facility is $8,650. Pretty Paints actively advertises their paints for $10,000 per year. Pretty Paints plans to sell 935 pallets this year.","options":["$544,625","$553,275","$1,072,275","$534,125"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The following is the selling and administrative budget for Pretty Paints.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.044_1809","question":"The CVF Company currently has a contribution margin of $80 per unit. It is considering decreasing shipping costs from $9 per unit to $5 per unit and increasing sales commissions from $10 per unit to $16 per unit. If both of these changes are implemented, what will be CVF's new contribution margin per unit?","options":["$82 per unit.","$80 per unit.","$78 per unit.","$84 per unit."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. If CVF decreases shipping costs by $4 per unit then its contribution margin will increase by $4 per unit. Likewise, the increase in sales commissions of $6 per unit will cause the contribution margin per unit to decrease $6 per unit. This means these two actions will decrease the contribution margin to $78 per unit ($80 + $4 − $6).","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.027_1805","question":"Versaille Company has cost of goods manufactured $325,000, beginning finished goods inventory $150,000, and ending finished goods inventory $175,000, for the year. What is the cost of goods sold?","options":["$300,000","$475,000","$325,000","$350,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is defined as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured − Ending Finished Goods Inventory.” Using the numbers for Versaille results in cost of goods sold of $300,000 ($150,000 + $325,000 − $175,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.006_1802","question":"Textbook Manufacturing (TM) is the leading textbook printing and binding company in the region. TM is starting a new line of Managerial Accounting textbooks this year and would like to calculate the budgeted total contribution margin for this line for 20X1. TM plans to sell every textbook they manufacture this year. The following information is available:","options":["$74.00","$128.00","$806,400","$466,200"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The following is TM's contribution margin statement.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.009_1805","question":"Which of the following items is correctly paired with its location?","options":["Sales salaries – Direct labor budget","Supervisory salaries – Selling and administrative expense budget","Freight-out – Selling and administrative expense budget","Budgeted sales in units – Manufacturing overhead budget"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The selling and administrative budget details expected spending on non-manufacturing items. Freight-out is classified as a selling expense as it is incurred to facilitate sales. As such, it is included in the selling and administrative budget. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.018_1805","question":"On April 1, 2016, Jensen's Auto has $542,194 in partially completed cars and trucks in their inventory. During the month of April, they incur the following costs:","options":["$2,373,063","$1,830,869","$1,288,675","$1,984,421"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured is defined as “Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period – Ending Work in Process Inventory.” The manufacturing costs incurred during the period consists of costs incurred to finished goods started in a prior period, costs incurred to start and finish goods, and costs incurred to start goods that are unfinished at the end of the period. Using the numbers for Jensen's Auto results in costs incurred in April of $1,830,869 ($167,588 + $1,274,639 + $388,642). Putting this into the cost of goods manufactured formula results in cost of goods manufactured of $1,984,421 ($542,194 + $1,830,869 − $388,642). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.023_1805","question":"Cupid Co. manufactures dog toys. For 20x7 it had the following amounts related to its most popular toy, Bacon Ben: beginning finished goods inventory, $30,000, cost of goods manufactured $120,000, and cost of goods sold $100,000. What was the amount of ending finished goods inventory?","options":["$50,000","$10,000","$30,000","$20,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is defined as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured − Ending Finished Goods Inventory.” Rearranging the figures results in ending finished goods inventory being calculated as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured − Cost of Goods Sold.” Using the amounts for Cupid results in ending finished goods inventory of $50,000 ($30,000 + $120,000 − $100,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.001_1802","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the cost of goods sold (COGS) formula?","options":["The traditional COGS formula begins with direct materials purchases, which is adjusted by subtracting beginning and adding ending direct materials inventory to compute the cost of direct materials used in production. This cost is combined with direct labor and manufacturing overhead costs used in production. The total production costs (i.e., total manufacturing costs) are adjusted by subtracting beginning and adding ending finished goods inventory to determine the COGS computation.","The traditional COGS formula begins with direct materials purchases, which is adjusted by adding beginning and adding ending direct materials inventory to compute the cost of direct materials used in production. This cost is combined with direct labor and manufacturing overhead costs used in production. The total production costs (i.e., total manufacturing costs) are adjusted by adding beginning and adding ending finished goods inventory to determine the COGS computation.","The traditional COGS formula begins with direct materials purchases, which is adjusted by subtracting beginning and subtracting ending direct materials inventory to compute the cost of direct materials used in production. This cost is combined with direct labor and manufacturing overhead costs used in production. The total production costs (i.e., total manufacturing costs) are adjusted by subtracting beginning and subtracting ending finished goods inventory to determine the COGS computation.","The traditional COGS formula begins with direct materials purchases, which is adjusted by adding beginning and subtracting ending direct materials inventory to compute the cost of direct materials used in production. This cost is combined with direct labor and manufacturing overhead costs used in production. The total production costs (i.e., total manufacturing costs) are adjusted by adding beginning and subtracting ending finished goods inventory to determine the COGS computation."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This answer correctly describes the COGS formula.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.wrap.ob.004_1802","question":"Which of the following is not a component of the selling and administrative expense budget?","options":["The costs of advertising products","The costs of delivering purchased products to customers","The production line manager","The depreciation of the administration building"],"answer":"C","explanation":"This is a manufacturing overhead expense, not a selling and administrative expense.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.033_1805","question":"A merchandising company has computed that its required merchandise purchases are $513,600. The company's budgeted cost of goods sold is $835,200, and its desired ending inventory is budgeted at $98,880. What is the company's budgeted beginning inventory?","options":["$321,600","$414,720","$420,480","$222,720"],"answer":"C","explanation":"For a merchandising company, cost of goods sold is defined as “Budgeted Beginning Inventory + Budgeted Merchandise Purchases − Desired Ending Inventory.” Rearranging the figures results in budgeted beginning inventory being calculated as “Cost of Goods Sold − Budgeted Merchandise Purchases + Desired Ending Inventory.” Using the numbers in this example results in budgeted beginning inventory of $420,480 ($835,200 − $513,600 + $98,880). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.010_1805","question":"Dobbins Resources pays sales commissions of $2 per unit and shipping costs of $0.75 per unit. If Dobbins expects to sell 12,000 units in the third quarter, what will their total variable expenses be?","options":["$24,000","$33,000","$9,000","$42,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Variable expenses are expenses that vary (in total) as sales vary. In this example variable costs per unit total $2.75 per unit ($2 + $0.75). If sales are expected to be 12,000 units, total variable expenses are expected to be $33,000 (12,000 × $2.75 per unit). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.025_1805","question":"Chris Manufacturing has beginning work in process inventory of $276,000, ending work in process inventory of $270,000, and cost of goods manufactured of $960,000. What was the amount of Chris Manufacturing's total manufacturing costs for the period?","options":["$966,000","$546,000","$954,000","$414,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured is defined as “Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period − Ending Work in Process Inventory.” Rearranging the figures results in total manufacturing costs being calculated as “Cost of Goods Manufactured − Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Ending Work in Process Inventory.” Using the amounts for Chris results in total manufacturing costs of $954,000 ($960,000 − $276,000 + $270,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.002_1805","question":"When they made their master budget, Vann Enterprises had direct material per unit costs of $12.43, direct labor per unit costs of $8.46, and manufacturing overhead per unit costs of $14.29. They planned to sell 16,000 units in the first quarter. However, in the first week of the first quarter, the direct material per unit costs rose to $16.12, which increased the selling price of the finished product. Therefore, Vann Enterprises sold only 15,500 units. What would the difference in cost of goods sold be between the budgeted income statement and the actual income statement?","options":["$39,605 decrease.","$59,040 increase.","$59,040 decrease.","$39,605 increase."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The budgeted cost of goods sold is $35.18 per unit ($12.43 + $8.46 + $14.29) and a total of $562,880 ($35.18 × 16,000). The actual cost of goods sold is $38.87 per unit ($16.12 + $8.46 + $14.29) and a total of $602,485 ($38.87 × 15,500). The actual cost of goods sold increased by $39,605 over budgeted cost of goods sold. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.011_1805","question":"Sam Montana Bookcases (SMB) manufactures bookcases for colleges and universities to use in dorm rooms. Each bookcase sells for $65. The company plans to sell 114,500 bookcases in July. Sam's salespeople earn a 5% commission on all sales and shipping costs are $8.50 per bookcase. Administrative salaries are $62,400 annually, and advertising is $132,000 annually. What are the company's budgeted selling and administrative expenses for July?","options":["$1,345,375","$388,325","$1,361,575","$1,539,775"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Selling and administrative expenses include a company's non-manufacturing costs. They can be variable, fixed, or mixed costs. In this example variable selling and administrative expenses per unit are budgeted to be $11.75 (shipping costs of $8.50 + commissions of $3.25 which is 5% of $65). The total variable selling and administrative expenses for 114,500 bookcases would be budgeted to be $1,345,375 (114,500 × $11.75 per unit). Annual total fixed selling and administrative expenses would be $194,400 ($62,400 + $132,000), or $16,200 on a monthly basis ($194,400 ÷ 12). The sum of the budgeted variable and fixed selling and administrative expenses would be $1,361,575 for the month of July ($1,345,375 + $16,200). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.008_1805","question":"________ is/are shown on the _____________.","options":["Sales commissions; direct labor budget","Maintenance expense; manufacturing overhead budget","Direct labor cost per hour; manufacturing overhead budget","Indirect labor; selling and administrative expense budget"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The manufacturing overhead budget details expected spending for fixed and variable manufacturing overhead for a period. Examples include indirect materials, indirect labor, and rent for manufacturing equipment. Maintenance expense is a part of the manufacturing overhead budget as it is a part of the manufacturing process. It is classified as overhead since it cannot be traced directly to a product; rather, it is incurred to benefit multiple products. This is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.016_1805","question":"On April 1, 20x6, Jensen's Auto has $542,194 in partially completed cars and trucks in their inventory. During the month of April, they incur another $167,588 to finish the vehicles, $1,274,639 to complete another 75 vehicles, and $388,642 to start 40 more vehicles. What is the total cost of work in process for the month of April?","options":["$1,984,421","$2,373,063","$1,830,869","$2,205,475"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The total cost of work in process for the month of April consists of the beginning balance in work in process as of April 1 and the manufacturing costs incurred during the month of April. The total manufacturing costs incurred during April is $1,830,869 ($167,588 + $1,274,639 + $388,642). Adding this to the beginning work in process of $542,194 gives total work in process of $2,373,063 ($542,194 + $1,830,869). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.037_1809","question":"All of the following statements concerning breakeven analysis are correct except:","options":["Contribution margin per unit is used to determine the number of units that need to be sold to break even.","Gross margin per unit is used to determine the number of units that need to be sold to break even.","The breakeven point is defined as the level of activity where total revenues are equal to total operating costs.","The breakeven point is defined as the level of activity where operating income is zero."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. The breakeven point is defined as the level of activity where operating income is equal to zero. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after variable costs have been paid. The number of units that need to be sold to break even can be calculated by dividing fixed costs by contribution margin per unit, not gross margin per unit.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.022_1805","question":"Jacques has been asked to calculate the cost of goods manufactured for Sully Enterprises for fiscal year 20x7. He has accumulated the following values.","options":["$8,777,538","$8,282,538","$7,651,249","$8,253,889"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured = Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period − Ending Work in Process Inventory.” Manufacturing costs incurred during the period = Direct materials + Direct labor + Manufacturing overhead. Direct materials used during 20x7 = Beginning Raw Materials + Raw Materials Purchased − Ending Raw Materials or $94,782 + $1,487,237 − $85,377 = $1,496,642. Direct labor for 20x7 = 282,100 hours × $21.50 per hour = $6,065,150. Manufacturing overhead consists of manufacturing costs other than direct materials and direct labor. For Jacques manufacturing overhead in 20x7 = factory oversight of $602,640 + factory utilities of $22,300 + factory depreciation of $12,400 + factory repairs of $64,450 = $701,790. The total manufacturing costs for 20x7 are $1,496,642 + $6,065,150 + $701,790 = $8,263,582. Putting this into the cost of goods manufactured formula results in cost of goods manufactured of $8,253,889 ($45,681 + $8,263,582 − $55,374). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.046_1809","question":"Which of the following statements about contribution margin is correct?","options":["An increase in variable selling expenses per unit will increase contribution margin per unit.","An increase in fixed selling expenses per unit will decrease contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in variable selling expenses per unit will increase contribution margin per unit.","A decrease in variable selling expenses per unit will not impact contribution margin per unit."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. A decrease in variable selling expenses per unit will increase contribution margin per unit as a smaller per-unit amount will be subtracted from the sales price per unit when calculating contribution margin per unit.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.030_1805","question":"A merchandising company has computed that its required merchandise purchases are $342,720. The company's beginning inventory is budgeted at $100,800, and its desired ending inventory is budgeted at $107,520. What is the company's budgeted cost of goods sold?","options":["$551,040","$349,440","$208,320","$336,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"For a merchandising company, cost of goods sold is defined as “Budgeted Beginning Inventory + Budgeted Merchandise Purchases − Desired Ending Inventory.” Using the numbers in this example results in cost of goods sold of $336,000 ($100,800 + $342,720 − $107,520). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.045_1809","question":"The TJP Company currently has a contribution margin of $95 per unit. It is considering increasing sales salaries from $13 per unit to $17 per unit and decreasing administrative expenses from $15 per unit to $12 per unit. If both of these changes are implemented, what will be TJP's new contribution margin per unit?","options":["$95 per unit.","$94 per unit.","$91 per unit.","$98 per unit."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. Sales salaries and administrative expenses are both fixed costs. This means that these actions will have no impact on TJP's contribution margin per unit.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.032_1805","question":"A merchandising company has computed that its required merchandise purchases are $428,000. The company's budgeted cost of goods sold is $696,000, and its desired ending inventory is budgeted at $82,400. What is the company's budgeted beginning inventory?","options":["$268,000","$350,400","$345,600","$185,600"],"answer":"B","explanation":"For a merchandising company, cost of goods sold is defined as “Budgeted Beginning Inventory + Budgeted Merchandise Purchases − Desired Ending Inventory.” Rearranging the figures results in budgeted beginning inventory being calculated as “Cost of Goods Sold − Budgeted Merchandise Purchases + Desired Ending Inventory.” Using the numbers in this example results in budgeted beginning inventory of $350,400 ($696,000 − $428,000 + $82,400). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.026_1805","question":"Emig Co. has beginning work in process inventory of $552,000, ending work in process inventory of $540,000, and cost of goods manufactured of $1,920,000. What was the amount of Emig's total manufacturing costs for the period?","options":["$1,932,000","$1,092,000","$828,000","$1,908,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured is defined as “Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period − Ending Work in Process Inventory.” Rearranging the figures results in total manufacturing costs being calculated as “Cost of Goods Manufactured − Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Ending Work in Process Inventory.” Using the amounts for Emig results in total manufacturing costs of $1,908,000 ($1,920,000 − $552,000 + $540,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.035_1809","question":"Which of the following statements is correct concerning contribution margin per unit?","options":["Contribution margin per unit represents the selling price from an individual unit left over after paying the variable costs related to producing and selling that individual unit.","Contribution margin per unit represents the total revenue left over after paying all the variable costs incurred in a period.","Contribution margin per unit represents the selling price from an individual unit left over after paying the costs related to producing that individual unit.","Contribution margin per unit represents the total revenue left over after paying all the production costs of goods sold during a period."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. One type of cost is a variable cost. Examples include direct materials, direct labor, and sales commissions. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.015_1805","question":"On January 1, 20x6, Windows Incorporated has $14,218 in partially completed windows in their inventory. During the month of January, they incur another $5,247 to finish the windows, $37,922 to complete another 120 windows, and $9,732 to start 50 more windows. What is the total cost of work in process for the month of January?","options":["$67,119","$57,387","$19,465","$52,901"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The total cost of work in process for the month of January consists of the beginning balance in work in process as of January 1 and the manufacturing costs incurred during the month of January. The total manufacturing costs incurred during January is $52,901 ($5,247 + $37,922 + $9,732). Adding this to the beginning work in process of $14,218 gives total work in process of $67,119 ($14,218 + $52,901). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.042_1809","question":"The LPG Company currently has a contribution margin of $25 per unit. It is considering increasing sales commissions from $4 per unit to $7 per unit and reducing sales salaries from $10 per unit to $8 per unit. If both of these changes are implemented, what will be LPG's new contribution margin per unit?","options":["$24 per unit.","$27 per unit.","$22 per unit.","$25 per unit."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. Contribution margin represents the amount of revenue left over after all variable costs have been paid. Contribution margin per unit is the selling price per unit less variable costs per unit. Changes in variable costs per unit impact the contribution margin per unit while changes in fixed costs per unit do not. If LPG increases sales commissions by $3 per unit then its contribution margin will decrease by $3 per unit. The reduction in sales salaries will not change the contribution margin per unit as fixed costs (like sales salaries) are not a part of the contribution margin. This means these two actions will reduce the contribution margin to $22 per unit ($25 − $3).","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.007_1805","question":"________ is/are shown on the _____________.","options":["Advertising expense; selling and administrative expense budget","Office salaries; direct labor budget","Indirect labor; selling and administrative expense budget","Office salaries; manufacturing overhead budget"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The selling and administrative budget details expected spending on non-manufacturing items. One type of selling and administrative expense is advertising expense. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.019_1805","question":"Rachel Inc. reported the following information: beginning work in process inventory, $160,000; cost of goods manufactured, $1,500,000; beginning finished good inventory, $100,000; ending work in process inventory, $80,000; and ending finished goods inventory, $80,000. What is Rachel's cost of goods sold for the year?","options":["$1,520,000","$1,580,000","$1,600,000","$1,480,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is defined as “Beginning Finished Goods Inventory + Cost of Goods Manufactured – Ending Finished Goods Inventory.” Using the numbers for Rachel Inc. results in cost of goods sold of $1,520,000 ($100,000 + $1,500,000 − $80,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.001_1805","question":"How is the beginning work in process inventory related to the cost of goods manufactured?","options":["The beginning work in process inventory is calculated by subtracting the cost of goods manufactured from the ending work in process inventory.","The beginning work in process inventory is calculated by adding the total manufacturing costs to the cost of goods manufactured and subtracting the ending work in process inventory.","The cost of goods manufactured is calculated by adding the beginning work in process inventory to the total manufacturing costs and subtracting the ending work in process inventory.","The cost of goods manufactured is calculated by adding the ending work in process inventory to the total manufacturing costs."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured is defined as the manufacturing cost of goods finished during an accounting period. It is also the amount transferred out of work-in-process inventory during an accounting period. It is defined as “Beginning Work-In-Process + Total Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period − Ending Work-In-Process.” Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.004_1805","question":"If the cost of goods sold is more than the cost of goods manufactured, then which of the following is true?","options":["Finished Goods Inventory has increased.","Finished Goods Inventory has decreased.","Work in Process Inventory has increased.","Work in Process Inventory has decreased."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cost of goods sold is defined as the manufacturing cost of goods sold during an accounting period. It is also the amount transferred out of finished goods inventory during an accounting period. It is defined as “Beginning Finished Goods + Cost of Goods Manufactured − Ending Finished Goods.” If cost of goods sold is higher than cost of goods manufactured, it means that beginning finished goods is higher than ending finished goods; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.017_1805","question":"On January 1, 2016, Windows Incorporated has $14,218 in partially completed windows in their inventory. During the month of January, they incur the following costs:","options":["$67,119","$48,415","$57,387","$52,901"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods manufactured is defined as “Beginning Work in Process Inventory + Manufacturing Costs Incurred During the Period – Ending Work in Process Inventory.” The manufacturing costs incurred during the period consists of costs incurred to finished goods started in a prior period, costs incurred to start and finish goods, and costs incurred to start goods that are unfinished at the end of the period. Using the numbers for Windows results in costs incurred this period of $52,901 ($5,247 + $37,922 + $9,732). Putting this into the cost of goods manufactured formula results in cost of goods manufactured of $57,387 ($14,218 + $52,901 − $9,732). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.048_1809","question":"What is the typical end product of the operating budget process?","options":["A pro forma balance sheet.","A pro forma statement of cash flows.","A capital budget.","A pro forma income statement."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. An operating budget is one part of the master budget. It is a plan for all of the company's day-to-day operations. It covers budgeted revenues and expenses. With this information, it is possible to prepare a pro forma income statement. That is the typical end product of the operating budget process.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.wrap.ob.031_1805","question":"A merchandising company has computed that its required merchandise purchases are $685,440. The company's beginning inventory is budgeted at $201,600, and its desired ending inventory is budgeted at $215,040. What is the company's budgeted cost of goods sold?","options":["$672,000","$1,102,080","$698,880","$416,640"],"answer":"A","explanation":"For a merchandising company, cost of goods sold is defined as “Budgeted Beginning Inventory + Budgeted Merchandise Purchases − Desired Ending Inventory.” Using the numbers in this example results in cost of goods sold of $672,000 ($201,600 + $685,440 − $215,040). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.3","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.005_1802","question":"Which of the following expresses the relationship of changes in balance sheet accounts with changes in the cash account?","options":["∆ Cash = ∆ Debt + ∆ Equity − ∆ Assets","∆ Cash = ∆ Debt − ∆ Equity − ∆ Assets","∆ Cash = ∆ Debt + ∆ Equity + ∆ Assets","∆ Cash = ∆ Debt − ∆ Assets"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer correctly expresses the relationship of changes in balance sheet accounts with changes in the cash account.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.005_1805","question":"Home Health manufactures medical supplies. For the month of April, it projects the following selling and administrative expenses: sales commissions, 8% of sales; shipping expense, $12,800; advertising and marketing costs payable next month, $4,200; electric and water utilities, $1,900; equipment depreciation, $18,700; and bad debt expense, $3,000. Sales for the month of April equal $694,000. What are the total expenses that will be reported on the budgeted income statement?","options":["$74,420","$597,880","$619,580","$96,120"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Total expenses on a budgeted income statement are based on accrual accounting, not cash accounting. Cash accounting would be used to budget cash expenditures. In this example budgeted expenses for April are $55,520 for sales commissions ($694,000 × 8%), $12,800 for shipping, $4,200 for advertising and marketing, $1,900 for electric and water utilities, $18,700 for depreciation, and $3,000 for bad debt expense. These total $96,120; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.024_1809","question":"The CFO of CSB Company wants to evaluate whether expected cash flow from operating activities is expected to increase over the previous year. What report or statement should be used for this purpose?","options":["The pro forma income statement.","The pro forma balance sheet.","The pro forma statement of cash flows.","The capital expenditure budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma statement of cash flows classifies all expected cash inflows and outflows from operating, investing, and financing activities for a given period of time. As a result, it can be used to evaluate whether expected cash flows from operating activities is expected to increase over the previous year.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.001_1802","question":"What is the relationship between the pro forma financial statements and the operational budget and the capital project budgets?","options":["Pro forma financial statements are determined by the operational budget and the capital project budgets.","Pro forma financial statements determine the operational budget and the capital project budgets.","Pro forma financial statements can be either created as a result of the operational budget and the capital project budgets, or built in advance and used to determine the operational budget and the capital project budgets.","Pro forma financial statements are created based on the organization's strategic plan and have no relationship with the operational budget and the capital project budgets."],"answer":"C","explanation":"If managers use the strategic plan to create an operational budget and capital project budgets, once these budgets are complete, the information can be used to create the pro forma financial statements. However, if managers create pro forma financial statements directly based on the organization's strategic plan, the pro forma financial statements will feed forward to help guide the creation of the operational budget and capital project budgets.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.019_1809","question":"Which phrase best describes a use of a pro forma income statement?","options":["Evaluating whether profitability targets will be met.","Evaluating whether cash flow targets will be met.","Evaluating whether liquidity ratio targets will be met.","Evaluating whether debt will increase too much."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma income statement provides information on expected revenues, expenses, and income for a given period of time. As a result, it can be used to evaluate whether profitability targets will be met.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.008_1802","question":"Shovel Makers Company (SMC) has already created a pro forma income statement and pro forma balance sheet and needs to make a pro forma statement of cash flows. Depreciation for the year was $750,000 and new equipment costing $2,475,000 was purchased. Dividends of $1,500,000 were paid on the 100,000 shares outstanding. Below are SMC's current balance sheet, pro forma income statement, and pro forma balance sheet:","options":["$725,000 cash from operating activities; ($2,725,000) cash spent on investing activities; $0 cash spent on financing activities","$2,225,000 cash from operating activities; ($2,725,000) cash spent on investing activities; ($1,500,000) cash spent on financing activities","$1,475,000 cash from operating activities; ($1,975,000) cash spent on investing activities; ($1,500,000) cash spent on financing activities","$1,225,000 cash from operating activities; ($2,725,000) cash spent on investing activities; ($500,000) cash spent on financing activities"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Below is the pro forma statement of cash flows for SMC:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.020_1809","question":"Which phrase best describes the use of a pro forma balance sheet?","options":["Evaluating whether profitability targets will be met.","Evaluating whether cash flow targets will be met.","Evaluating whether the debt-to-equity ratio is expected to increase over last year.","Evaluating whether gross profit as a percentage of sales is expected to increase over last year."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma balance sheet provides information on expected assets, liabilities, and equity. This information can be used to calculate expected values for ratios such as debt-to-equity to see if they will increase over last year.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.018_1805","question":"On January 1, Scarlett Company has a beginning cash balance of $21,000. During the year, the company expects cash disbursements of $170,000 and cash receipts of $145,000. If Scarlett requires an ending cash balance of $20,000, how much must the Scarlett Company borrow?","options":["$24,000","$16,000","$25,000","$26,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In cash budgeting the sources of cash must be equal to the uses of cash. The sources of cash are “Beginning Cash Balance + Expected Cash Receipts + Required Financing” and the uses of cash are “Expected Cash Disbursements + Desired Ending Balance.” Rearranging these formulas results in required financing being calculated as “Expected Cash Disbursements + Desired Ending Balance − Beginning Cash Balance − Expected Cash Receipts.” In this example required financing is $24,000 ($170,000 + $20,000 − $21,000 − $145,000); therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.004_1802","question":"Microwave Manufacturer (MM), a manufacturer of high-end microwaves, is putting together a pro forma income statement as an end goal to help guide its master budgeting process. Based on the strategy plan, the MM management team is projecting sales next year at $30,000,000. The following expectations have been established","options":["$2,785,145","$2,725,895","$3,450,500","$2,962,895"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Below is MM's pro forma income statement:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.009_1802","question":"Shovel Makers Company (SMC) has created a pro forma income statement and pro forma balance sheet. Depreciation for the year was $750,000 and new equipment costing $2,475,000 was purchased. Dividends of $1,500,000 were paid to the 100,000 shares outstanding. Below is SMC's pro forma income statement and pro forma balance sheet:","options":["Return on assets, return on equity","Quick ratio, current ratio","Quick ratio, current ratio, and debt ratio","Gross margin, operating profit margin, net income margin"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Below is the calculation of SMC's ratios:","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.004_1805","question":"Techgear Inc. manufactures specialty suits for triathletes. For the month of January, it projects the following selling and administrative expenses: sales commissions, 4% of sales; shipping expense, $4,500; advertising and marketing costs payable next month, $12,000; electric and water utilities, $750; corporate office depreciation for computers, $8,800; and bad debt expense, $8,000. Sales for the month of January equal $400,000. What are total expenses that will be reported on the budgeted income statement?","options":["$33,250","$41,250","$50,050","$21,250"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Total expenses on a budgeted income statement are based on accrual accounting, not cash accounting. In this example budgeted expenses for January are $16,000 for sales commissions ($400,000 × 4%), $4,500 for shipping, $12,000 for advertising and marketing, $750 for electric and water utilities, $8,800 for depreciation, and $8,000 for bad debt expense. These total $50,050; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.021_1809","question":"Which phrase best describes the use of a pro forma statement of cash flows?","options":["Evaluating whether inventory as a percentage of assets will increase over last year.","Evaluating whether cash flow targets will be met.","Evaluating whether the debt-to-equity ratio is expected to increase over last year.","Evaluating whether gross profit as a percentage of sales is expected to increase over last year."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma statement of cash flows classifies all expected cash inflows and outflows from operating, investing, and financing activities for a given period of time. This information can be used to evaluate whether cash flow targets will be met.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.048_1809","question":"Which of the following actions is least likely to impact the cash needs of a company?","options":["A company decides to sell on credit rather than selling for cash only.","A company decides to offer customers a discount for paying payables within 10 days.","A company decides to increase production capacity by 15%.","A company decides to recognize an impairment loss on a piece of equipment."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. Cash needs are based on expected cash disbursements and expected cash receipts. Since impairment losses on equipment are non-cash losses, the decision to recognize them will not impact the cash needs of a company.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.047_1809","question":"Which of the following factors is least likely to impact the cash needs of a company?","options":["Collection patterns for accounts receivable.","Payment patterns for accounts payable and other accruals.","Capital expenditure plans.","Write-offs for obsolete inventory."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. Cash needs are based on expected cash disbursements and expected cash receipts. Since write-offs for obsolete inventory are non-cash losses, they do not impact the cash needs of a company.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.006_1805","question":"The Wombert Company has completed its budget for all cash receipts and disbursements for the first six months of 20X4. The company's bank requires a monthly minimum balance of $30,000 and offers the company short-term financing when it cannot meet the requirement. For April, the company has cash receipts of $18,000 and cash disbursements of $62,000. If beginning cash balance in April is forecasted at $38,000, how much will Wombert need to borrow from the bank to meet the minimum cash balance requirement of $30,000?","options":["$36,000","$6,000","$24,000","$44,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In this example, Wombert's sources of cash total $56,000 ($18,000 + $38,000). It needs $92,000 to cover expenditures of $62,000 plus a targeted ending cash balance of $30,000. Based on this, it will need to borrow $36,000 to cover the shortfall ($56,000 − $92,000); therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.023_1809","question":"The CFO of DE Incorporated wants to evaluate whether accounts receivable as a percentage of total assets is expected to increase over the previous year. What report or statement should be used for this purpose?","options":["The pro forma income statement.","The pro forma balance sheet.","The pro forma statement of cash flows.","The capital expenditure budget."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma balance sheet provides information on expected assets, liabilities, and equity. As a result, it can be used to evaluate whether accounts receivable as a percentage of total assets is expected to increase.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.002_1802","question":"Which of the following does not describe how pro forma financial statements benefit an organization?","options":["Pro forma financial statements can help the organization's leadership team and budget committee to “begin with the end in mind.”","Pro forma financial statements help organizations determine whether they will meet goals, lender requirements, and investor expectations.","Pro forma financial statements provide a view of the organization's future financial performance based on current financial statements and anticipated future actions.","Pro forma financial statements are not adjusted once created, which saves time and resources for the organization."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Pro forma financial statements are adjusted as the organization changes its capital project plans and operational objectives.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.010_1805","question":"E&D Clothing manufactures and sells 100% organic clothing. The company is preparing the budget for the third quarter and estimates that cash sales will be $124,000, $140,000, and $138,000 for July, August, and September, respectively. Credit sales during this time are expected to be $204,000, $210,000, and $234,000, respectively. The company collects its credit sales in the following pattern: 30% in the month of sale, 60% in the month following the sale, and 10% in the second month after the sale. What amount will be reported for accounts receivable in the September balance sheet?","options":["$184,800","$295,400","$354,600","$110,600"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The accounts receivable balance shown on a balance sheet represents the amount of credit sales that have not yet been collected. As of September 30, 10% of August budgeted credit sales ($210,000 × 10%) and 70% of September budgeted credit sales ($234,000 × 70%) would be uncollected. These two add up to $184,800 ($21,000 + $163,800); therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.012_1805","question":"Bee Colonies produces organic honey, which it sells to health food stores. The company's cash payments budget for 20x5 indicates that all of Bee's direct materials are purchased on account. Bee's payment history indicates that it pays for 70% of its purchases in the quarter of the purchase and 30% in the following quarter. Purchases for the fourth quarter of 20x5 were $50,000. Determine the balance of accounts payable on Bee's budgeted balance sheet at December 31, 20x5.","options":["$35,000","$50,000","$15,000","$25,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The accounts payable balance shown on a balance sheet represents the amount of credit purchases that have not yet been paid. As of December 31, 30% of 4th quarter budgeted credit purchases are expected to be unpaid. This translates to an accounts payable balance of $15,000 ($50,000 × 30%). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.003_1802","question":"How do the pro forma financial statements link back to the strategic plan?","options":["Pro forma financial statements never link back to the strategic plan. Pro forma financial statements link back to the operational budget as a feedback evaluation loop.","Pro forma financial statements link back to the strategic plan by determining the strategic plan of the organization for the next year.","Pro forma financial statements never link back to the strategic plan—they are determined by the strategic plan.","Pro forma financial statements link back to the strategic plan as a feedback evaluation loop."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The comparison of pro forma financial statements with results reported in actual financial statements are a key measure of the success of the organization's strategic work, which is how the pro forma financial statements link back to the strategic plan—as a feedback evaluation loop.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.008_1805","question":"Aspen Firs had an accounts receivable balance of $21,500 on its December 31, 20X4, balance sheet. Cash receipts for 20X5 show uncollected credit sales of $25,000 at the end of 20X5. Aspen is also budgeting $1,000,000 of credit sales in 20X5. What was the accounts receivable balance on the December 31, 20X5, budgeted balance sheet?","options":["$46,500","$21,500","$25,000","$3,500"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The accounts receivable balance shown on a balance sheet represents the amount of credit sales that have not yet been collected. It comprises the beginning balance for the period, the credit sales for the period, and the amount collected on credit sales for the period. If cash receipts show that $25,000 in credit sales have not yet been collected at the end of 20X5, the accounts receivable balance as of 12/31/X5 is $25,000. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.015_1805","question":"Paradise Inc. has budgeted sales of $2,480,000 in January, $2,320,000 in February, and $2,880,000 in March. All sales are on credit and accounts are collected 50% in the month of sale and 50% in the month after sale. What amount will Paradise report on its budgeted balance sheet for accounts receivable at the end of the quarter?","options":["$3,840,000","$1,440,000","$2,400,000","$1,160,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The accounts receivable balance shown on a balance sheet represents the amount of credit sales that have not yet been collected. As of the end of the quarter, 50% of March budgeted credit sales ($1,440,000) would be uncollected. All other credit sales for the quarter will be collected; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.013_1805","question":"Sally Automotive is preparing the budget for the first quarter and estimates that budgeted cash purchases will be $106,000, $98,400, and $103,600 for January, February, and March, respectively. The company also purchases on credit and historically pays 40% of its credit purchases in the month of purchase, 50% in the month following purchase, and 10% in the second month after the purchase. Credit purchases during this time are expected to be $186,000, $174,800, and $202,000 for January, February, and March, respectively. What amount will be reported for Accounts Payable in the March balance sheet?","options":["$290,400","$72,000","$210,680","$138,680"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The accounts payable balance shown on a budgeted balance sheet represents the amount of credit purchases that are expected not to be paid yet. As of March 31, $17,480 of February credit purchases ($174,800 × 10%) and $121,200 of March credit purchases ($202,000 × 60%) are expected to be unpaid. This translates to an expected accounts payable balance of $138,680 as of March 31. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.009_1805","question":"Jericho's Mediterranean Restaurant is preparing its budgeted balance sheet. The cash receipts budget indicates that net sales in the fourth quarter of 20X5 were $36,000. Jericho expects 25% of these sales to be cash. Of the credit sales, it expects 80% to be collected in the quarter of the sale and 20% to be collected in the following quarter. Calculate the amount of accounts receivable that Jericho would report on the budgeted balance sheet at December 31, 20X5.","options":["$9,000","$1,800","$7,200","$5,400"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The accounts receivable balance shown on a balance sheet represents the amount of credit sales that have not yet been collected. In the fourth quarter there will be cash sales of $9,000 ($36,000 × 25%) and credit sales of $27,000 ($36,000 × 75%). If 20% of the credit sales are not collected until the following quarter, the budgeted accounts receivable balance as of 12/31/X5 would be $5,400 ($27,000 × 20%). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.007_1802","question":"Plastic Containers (PC) is the leading producer of plastic storage containers in the region. PC has already prepared a pro forma income statement, but would like to prepare a pro forma balance sheet. The following expectations have been established:","options":["$693,000","$7,440,000","Even with the maximum loan available, PC does not have enough resources for a dividend","$1,893,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The following is PC's pro forma balance sheet: Beginning retained earnings + Income – Ending retained earnings = Dividends $6,000,000 + $2,133,000 − $7,440,000 = $693,000","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pf.fs.006_1802","question":"Stove Suppliers (SS) manufactures and sells kitchen stove and oven combinations. SS has already prepared a pro forma income statement, but would like to prepare a pro forma balance sheet. The following expectations have been established:","options":["$9,725,000","$15,725,000","$21,650,000","$5,725,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The following is SS's pro forma balance sheet: $15,725,000 total bank loan needed − $10,000,000 current bank loan = $5,725,000 new loan.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.017_1805","question":"Cheddar's Café is preparing its cash budget for August. At the beginning of the month, the café expects to have $17,500 in total available cash. Over the course of the month, it expects to make $12,250 in cash disbursements and obtain $3,750 in financing. What is Cheddar's anticipated ending cash balance for August?","options":["$1,500","$26,000","$5,250","$9,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The ending cash balance is calculated as “Available Cash − Expected Cash Disbursements + Required Financing.” In this example the ending cash balance is $9,000 ($17,500 − $12,250 + $3,750). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.016_1805","question":"In July, Dora Inc. had $150,000 in total available cash. Financing needs total $12,000, and cash disbursements were $50,000. What is Dora's ending cash balance for July?","options":["$100,000","$138,000","$112,000","$150,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The ending cash balance is calculated as “Available Cash − Expected Cash Disbursements + Required Financing.” In this example the ending cash balance is $112,000 ($150,000 − $50,000 + $12,000); therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.007_1805","question":"Solar Manufacturing is preparing its cash budget for the fourth quarter. It has $42,400 in cash on September 30. Sales are expected to be $104,300, $127,800, and $133,200 in October, November, and December. All sales are cash sales. Solar's monthly operating expenses are expected to be $92,500, $89,400, and $94,300 in October, November, and December. Monthly expenses include depreciation of $22,600 per month. Cash expenses are paid in the month incurred. Cash payments for merchandise inventory are expected to be $212,700 for the quarter. The desired cash balance on December 31 is $40,000. How much financing will Solar Manufacturing need during the quarter?","options":["$121,200","$53,400","$13,400","$95,800"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Solar's sources of cash are the $42,400 beginning cash balance and $365,300 in cash collections from sales for a total of $407,700. While total operating expenses are $276,200, depreciation of $67,800 (3 × $22,600) needs to be subtracted since depreciation is a non-cash expense. This leaves cash needs for operating expenses of $208,400. Total cash needs for the quarter are $461,100 ($208,400 + $212,700 + $40,000). Based on this, it will need to borrow $53,400 to cover the shortfall ($407,700 − $461,100). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.022_1809","question":"The CFO of the POI Company wants to evaluate whether operating income for the upcoming year is likely to meet expectations. What report or statement should be used for this purpose?","options":["The pro forma income statement.","The pro forma balance sheet.","The pro forma statement of cash flows.","The sales budget."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. The pro forma income statement provides information on expected revenues, expenses, and income for a given period of time. As a result, it can be used to evaluate whether operating income for the upcoming year is likely to meet expectations.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pf.fs.014_1805","question":"Paradise Inc. has budgeted sales of $2,480,000 in January, $2,320,000 in February, and $2,880,000 in March. All sales are on credit and accounts are collected 50% in the month of sale and 50% in the month after sale. How much cash will Paradise receive in February?","options":["$2,400,000","$2,600,000","$2,320,000","$1,160,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paradise will receive cash from two sources in February: from credit sales made in February and from credit sales made in January. It will receive $1,160,000 from February credit sales ($2,320,000 × 50%) and $1,240,000 from January credit sales ($2,480,000 × 50%). These two add up to $2,400,000; therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.6","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.003_1805","question":"How are a company's direct materials budget and direct labor budget related to each other?","options":["They are both calculated based on the number of units to be sold.","They are both calculated based on machine hours.","They are both calculated based on the number of units to be produced.","They are both calculated based on overhead costs."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The direct materials budget includes the number of units to be produced, the expected materials required per unit, and the expected purchase price of materials. The direct labor budget includes the number of units to be produced, the expected labor hours required per unit, and the expected hourly wage rate. This means the number of units to be produced is used in the direct materials budget and the direct labor budget. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.039_1805","question":"Sports Enthusiasts manufactures skateboards. During May, June, and July, Sports Enthusiasts budgets for sales of 12,300 skateboards, 10,800 skateboards, and 9,700 skateboards, respectively. Each skateboard requires 4 wheels. The company requires an ending inventory of wheels equal to 20% of the following month's production needs. How many wheels does Sports Enthusiasts need to purchase in June?","options":["50,960 wheels","42,320 wheels","34,560 wheels","43,200 wheels"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The total budgeted direct materials to be purchased is calculated as “Material Needed for Budgeted Production + Desired Ending DM Inventory − Budgeted Beginning DM Inventory.” Materials needed for budgeted production is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Materials Needed per Unit.” To produce 10,800 skateboards in June, 43,200 wheels will be needed (10,800 × 4). The budgeted beginning DM inventory for June would be 8,640 wheels (43,200 × 20%) and the desired ending DM inventory for June would be 7,760 wheels (9,700 × 4 × 20%). Based on these figures, Sports needs to budget to purchase 42,320 wheels in June (43,200 + 7,760 − 8,640). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.004_1805","question":"Jackie and Carolyn are working together to develop the production budgets. If Jackie is working on the direct materials budget and Carolyn is working on the direct labor budget, what common information will they both need to complete their budgets?","options":["The number of units to be sold each period.","The selling price of each unit.","The machine hours used to produce each unit.","The number of units to be produced each period."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The direct materials budget is based on the number of units expected to be produced, the expected amount of materials to be used in each finished unit, and the expected price to pay for materials. The direct labor budget is based on the number of units expected to be produced, the expected amount of labor hours required to complete each finished unit, and the expected hourly wage to be paid. Both require the number of units expected to be produced each period. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.050_1809","question":"All of the following statements concerning a direct materials purchase budget are correct except:","options":["As budgeted production increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases.","As desired ending direct materials inventory increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases.","As beginning direct materials inventory increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases.","As the amount of direct materials required per unit increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A direct materials purchases budget is an estimate of the quantity and cost of direct materials to be purchased in a period. The quantity to be purchased is based on materials needed for production, beginning direct materials inventory, and desired ending direct materials inventory. Materials needed during a period come from beginning inventory of direct materials and material purchases. As beginning direct materials inventory increases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases, not increases.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.007_1802","question":"What is the relationship between the manufacturing overhead budget and the production budget?","options":["The manufacturing overhead budget has no relation with the production budget.","The production budget depends on, and is a function of, the manufacturing overhead budget.","The manufacturing overhead budget depends on, and is a function of, the production budget.","The manufacturing overhead budget affects the sales budget, which then affects the production budget."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This answer correctly describes the relationship between the manufacturing overhead budget and the production budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.011_1805","question":"B&R Tax Services is preparing its direct labor budget for the first quarter of the year. The total direct labor budget for the first quarter is $55,500. The firm expects to prepare 300 small returns which take 1 hour each, 200 medium returns which take 1.75 hours each, and 100 complicated returns which take 2.75 each. What is B&R's direct labor cost per hour?","options":["$92.50","$52.86","$60.00","$33.64"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The total direct labor budget is calculated as “Expected Hours Needed × Direct Labor Cost Per Hour.” Rearranging this results in direct labor per hour being calculated as “Total Direct Labor Budget ÷ Expected Hours Needed.” In this example 300 hours are needed for small returns (300 × 1 hour), 350 hours for medium returns (200 × 1.75), and 275 hours for complicated returns (100 × 2.75). This sums to 925 total hours. Since the total direct labor budget is $55,500, the direct labor cost per hour is $60.00 ($55,500 ÷ 925). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.007_1805","question":"Philip is a manager who oversees raw materials ordering, inventory levels, employee efficiency and productivity, and assembly line maintenance. Which of the following budgets would Philip most likely help develop?","options":["Selling and administrative expense budget","Sales budget","Manufacturing overhead budget","Capital expenditure budget"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The manufacturing overhead budget details the expected amount to be paid for manufacturing overhead expenses in a period. It is based on expected usage of the allocation base and expected variable and fixed manufacturing overhead expenses. Raw materials ordering, inventory levels, employee efficiency and productivity, and assembly line maintenance are all related to the production and manufacturing processes. As such, Philip is likely to be involved in the development of budgets related to the manufacturing process. These include the production budget, the direct materials budget, the direct labor budget, and the manufacturing overhead budget. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.008_1805","question":"Ellen is a manager who helps develop sales promotions, targets customers for upselling, and searches for potential new customers. Which of the following budgets would Ellen most likely help develop?","options":["Selling and administrative expense budget","Manufacturing overhead budget","Cash budget","Sales budget"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The sales budget details the expected amount of revenue to be generated in a period. It is based on the expected units to be sold and the expected selling price per unit sold. Since sales promotions, upselling, and acquiring new customers are all likely to impact the expected units to be sold and the expected selling price received per unit, Ellen would most likely help develop the sales budget. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.001_1805","question":"Which of the following items is correctly paired with its location?","options":["Units to be produced – Direct materials budget","Unit selling price – Production budget","Total required units – Sales budget","Total sales – Direct materials budget"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The units to be produced appears in the production budget, direct materials budget, and direct labor budget. The direct materials budget includes the units to be produced, the materials required per unit, and the purchase price of materials. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.037_1805","question":"Cupid Manufacturing is preparing its direct materials budget. The company has budgeted to purchase 88,480 pounds of direct materials. The desired ending inventory of direct materials is 67,200 pounds and the total materials required is 96,600 pounds. What is the amount of total direct materials needed for production?","options":["8,120 pounds","21,280 pounds","185,080 pounds","29,400 pounds"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The total materials needed for a period is calculated as “Materials for Production + Desired Ending Inventory.” Rearranging these figures results in materials for production being calculated as “Total Materials Needed for a Period – Desired Ending Inventory.” If a total of 96,600 pounds are needed and 67,200 yards are needed for ending inventory, the other 29,400 yards must be for production (96,600 – 67,200). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.017_1805","question":"Sarah is trying to prepare the manufacturing budgets. She believes that the manufacturing overhead budget will be the most complicated, so she decides to tackle it first before the direct materials and direct labor budgets. Do you think this is the best way to prepare the budgets? Why or why not?","options":["No, because the manufacturing overhead budget depends on the direct labor budget, so the direct labor budget needs to be prepared first.","No, because the manufacturing overhead budget depends on the direct materials budget, so the direct materials budget needs to be prepared first.","Yes, because the manufacturing overhead budget only depends on the sales budget, which should already be complete.","Yes, because the manufacturing overhead budget does not depend on any other budget, so it can be prepared at any time."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The manufacturing overhead budget details expected spending for manufacturing overhead. The variable component of the budget is often based on direct labor hours as direct labor is used to allocate variable manufacturing overhead; consequently, the direct labor budget needs to be prepared prior to the manufacturing overhead budget. This is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.038_1805","question":"Norma's Tack produces saddles for horseback riders. During October, November, and December, Norma budgets for sales of 6,400 saddles, 8,200 saddles, and 10,300 saddles, respectively. Each saddle requires 1.80 yards of leather (including .25 yard for waste). The company requires an ending inventory of leather equal to 30% of the following month's production needs. How many yards of leather does Norma need to purchase in November?","options":["15,894 yards","13,626 yards","8,830 yards","20,322 yards"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The total budgeted direct materials to be purchased is calculated as “Material Needed for Budgeted Production + Desired Ending DM Inventory – Budgeted Beginning DM Inventory.” Materials needed for budgeted production is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Materials Needed per Unit.” To produce 8,200 saddles in November, 14,760 yards of leather will be needed (8,200 × 1.8). The budgeted beginning DM inventory for November would be 4,428 yards (14,760 × 30%) and the desired ending DM inventory for November would be 5,562 yards (10,300 × 1.8 × 30%). Based on these figures, Norma needs to budget to purchase 15,894 yards of leather in November (14,760 + 5,562 − 4,428). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.047_1805","question":"Determine the correct cost for Gabby's Gifts using the following information: materials, $4,200; labor cost, $1,600; depreciation, $800; rent, $700; and other fixed costs, $500. The company produced 400 ceramic pots in May.","options":["The fixed cost per unit is $3.75.","The variable cost per unit is $14.50.","The fixed cost per unit is $19.50.","The total cost per unit is $14.50."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Budgeted variable cost per unit is calculated as “Total Budgeted Variable Cost ÷ Budgeted Units.” Materials and labor are variable costs as they will change in direct proportion to changes in output. The two add up to $5,800. The other costs are fixed costs as they do not change when output changes. Dividing the $5,800 by 400 pots gives variable cost per unit of $14.50 per unit ($5,800 ÷ 400). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.036_1805","question":"LDM Co. is preparing its direct materials budget. The company has budgeted to purchase 126,400 yards of direct materials. The desired ending inventory of direct materials is 96,000 yards and the total materials required is 138,000 yards. Assuming there is no beginning inventory, what is the amount of total direct materials needed for production?","options":["11,600 yards","30,400 yards","42,000 yards","264,400 yards"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The total materials needed for a period is calculated as “Materials for Production + Desired Ending Inventory.” Rearranging these figures results in materials for production being calculated as “Total Materials Needed for a Period – Desired Ending Inventory.” If a total of 138,000 yards are needed and 96,000 yards are needed for ending inventory, the other 42,000 yards must be for production (138,000 – 96,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.054_1809","question":"Which of the following statements concerning the relationship between the manufacturing overhead budget and production budget is correct?","options":["As budgeted production increases, the total budgeted fixed manufacturing overhead increases.","As budgeted production decreases, the budgeted fixed manufacturing overhead per unit decreases.","As budgeted production increases, the budgeted total variable manufacturing overhead increases.","As budgeted production increases, the budgeted variable manufacturing overhead per unit increases."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A manufacturing overhead budget is an estimate of the total expected manufacturing overhead costs to be incurred in a period. It is typically separated into variable and fixed components. The total amount of variable manufacturing overhead increases as production increases as the definition of a variable cost is a cost that changes in direct proportion to changes in activity.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.041_1805","question":"Boyd Industries had a beginning balance of $59,600 in its Raw Materials Inventory account. At the end of the period, the ending balance in this account was $61,300. During the period, Boyd purchased $232,700 of materials. What was the cost of raw materials used during the period?","options":["$353,600","$111,800","$232,700","$231,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The total budgeted direct materials to be purchased is calculated as “Material Needed for Budgeted Production + Desired Ending DM Inventory − Budgeted Beginning DM Inventory.” Rearranging these figures results in materials used during the period being calculated as “Materials Purchased − Desired Ending DM Inventory + Budgeted Beginning DM Inventory.” In this example, Boyd used $231,000 for production this period ($232,700 − $61,300 + $59,600). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.012_1805","question":"Casteel Lawn Service is preparing its direct labor budget for the first quarter of the year. The firm provides a variety of mowing, clean-up, and planting services. The total direct labor budget for the first quarter is $22,200. The firm expects 300 small jobs which take 1 hour each, 200 medium jobs which take 1.75 hours each, and 100 large jobs which take 2.75 each. What is Casteel's direct labor cost per hour?","options":["$37.00","$21.15","$13.46","$24.00"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The total direct labor budget is calculated as “Expected Hours Needed × Direct Labor Cost Per Hour.” Rearranging this results in direct labor per hour being calculated as “Total Direct Labor Budget ÷ Expected Hours Needed.” In this example 300 hours are needed for small jobs (300 × 1 hour), 350 hours for medium jobs (200 × 1.75), and 275 hours for large jobs (100 × 2.75). This sums to 925 total hours. Since the total direct labor budget is $22,200, the direct labor cost per hour is $24.00 ($22,200 ÷ 925). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.022_1805","question":"Major Brands Inc. a clothing manufacturer, is planning to produce 8,700 jackets and sell 9,000 jackets during February. Each jacket requires 4.4 yards of fabric at $4.00 per yard and 0.25 direct labor hours at $12 per hour. Manufacturing overhead is applied at 110% of direct labor costs. Major Brands has 39,000 yards of fabric in beginning inventory and wants 45,000 yards in ending inventory. What is the total amount of budgeted manufacturing overhead for February?","options":["$114,840","$29,700","$505,296","$28,710"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Manufacturing overhead refers to manufacturing costs that are not directly traceable to products. In this example manufacturing overhead is allocated as 110% of direct labor dollars. This means that overhead will be $1.10 for every $1.00 in direct labor. To produce 8,700 jackets, 2,175 hours of direct labor will be required. At a wage rate of $12 per hour, budgeted total direct labor dollars would be $26,100 (2,175 × $12). This results in budgeted manufacturing overhead of $28,710 ($26,100 × 110%). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.010_1805","question":"Cara's Wedding Designs makes and sells bridal dresses, bridesmaids’ dresses, tuxedos, and suits. The company plans to produce 60 dresses during the next month and will require 390 yards of fabric for production. The total fabric required for the month is 429 yards. What are the direct materials per dress?","options":["0.15 yards.","6.5 yards.","7.15 yards.","1.1 yards."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The total materials required to be purchased for the quarter is calculated as “Materials needed for Production + Desired Ending Inventory − Expected Beginning Inventory.” Materials needed for production is calculated as “Expected Production × Direct Materials per Unit.” Rearranging this results in direct materials per unit being calculated as “Materials Needed for Production ÷ Expected Production.” In this example the direct materials per unit is 6.5 years (390 ÷ 60). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.049_1809","question":"Which of the following statements concerning a direct materials purchase budget is most correct?","options":["The direct materials purchases budget is based on materials needed for production, beginning finished goods inventory, and desired ending finished goods inventory.","The direct materials purchases budget is based on materials needed for production, beginning direct materials inventory, and desired ending direct materials inventory.","The direct materials purchases budget is based on budgeted production, beginning finished goods inventory, and desired ending finished goods inventory.","The direct materials purchases budget is based on budgeted production, beginning direct materials inventory, and desired ending direct materials inventory."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A direct materials purchases budget is an estimate of the quantity and cost of direct materials to be purchased in a period. The quantity to be purchased is based on materials needed for production, beginning direct materials inventory, and desired ending direct materials inventory.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.015_1805","question":"At the Progressa Company, the required June production is 44,000 units. To make one unit of finished product, three pounds of direct material Z are required. Actual beginning and desired ending inventories of direct material Z are 100,000 and 110,000 pounds, respectively. How many pounds of direct material Z must be purchased?","options":["122,000","132,000","142,000","54,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The total materials required to be purchased for a period is calculated as “Materials needed for Production + Desired Ending Inventory − Expected Beginning Inventory.” Materials needed for production is calculated as “Expected Production × Direct Materials per Unit.” In this example 132,000 pounds are needed for production (44,000 × 3 pounds per unit). This means that the company needs to purchase 142,000 pounds of materials (132,000 + 110,000 − 100,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.024_1805","question":"Gulfcoast Shrimp Packaging supplies frozen shrimp to grocery chains. It expects production of 380 tons and sales of 400 tons of shrimp in the next quarter. Each ton requires 50 direct labor hours and employees are paid $15 per hour. Manufacturing overhead is applied at 120% of direct labor costs. What is the total amount of budgeted manufacturing overhead for the quarter?","options":["$360,000","$342,000","$6,840","$702,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Manufacturing overhead refers to manufacturing costs that are not directly traceable to products. In this example, overhead will be $1.20 for every $1.00 in direct labor. To produce 380 tons, 19,000 hours of direct labor will be required. At a wage rate of $15 per hour, budgeted total direct labor dollars would be $285,000. This results in budgeted manufacturing overhead of $342,000 ($285,000 × 120%). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.042_1805","question":"Tim's Toys, a maker of high-end collectible teddy bears, had a beginning balance of $71,200 in its Raw Materials Inventory account. At the end of the period, the ending balance in this account was $75,000. During the period, Tim purchased $150,700 of materials. What was the cost of raw materials used during the period?","options":["$146,900","$225,700","$75,700","$221,900"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The total budgeted direct materials to be purchased is calculated as “Material Needed for Budgeted Production + Desired Ending DM Inventory − Budgeted Beginning DM Inventory.” Rearranging these figures results in materials used during the period being calculated as “Materials Purchased − Desired Ending DM Inventory + Budgeted Beginning DM Inventory.” In this example, Tim used $146,900 for production this period ($150,700 − $75,000 + $71,200). This is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.048_1805","question":"Nathan is working on the operating budgets for his company. He has already done the sales and production budgets, and he is now working on the direct materials, direct labor, and manufacturing overhead budgets. He decides to do the direct labor budget first, then the manufacturing overhead budget, then the direct materials budget. Do you think this is an appropriate way to prepare the budgets? Why or why not?","options":["Yes, because the direct materials budget depends on the direct labor and manufacturing overhead budgets, so it should always be prepared last.","Yes, because the manufacturing overhead budget depends on the direct labor budget, but none of the other budgets depend on the direct materials budget.","No, because the direct labor budget depends on the direct materials budget, so the direct materials budget should be prepared first.","No, because the manufacturing overhead budget depends on the direct materials budget, so the direct materials budget should be prepared second."],"answer":"B","explanation":"All three budgets are based on the production budget. The variable component of the manufacturing overhead budget is often based on direct labor hours as direct labor is used to allocate variable manufacturing overhead; consequently, the direct labor budget needs to be prepared prior to the manufacturing overhead budget. The direct labor and manufacturing overhead budgets do not depend on the direct materials budget, so the direct materials budget can be prepared independent of the other two. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.009_1802","question":"Plush Pillows Production (3P) manufactures premium feather pillows for a chain of mattress stores. The company plans to manufacture and sell 80,000 pillows during the first quarter. Overhead costs are expected to include:","options":["$3.00 per pillow and $84,000 total for the quarter","$4.05 per pillow and $324,000 total for the quarter","$1.05 per pillow and $84,000 total for the quarter","$3.00 per pillow and $240,000 total for the quarter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The following is 3P's manufacturing overhead budget for the first quarter.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.023_1805","question":"Natural Beauty, a cosmetic manufacturer, is planning to produce 60,900 lipsticks and sell 63,000 lipsticks during November. Each lipstick requires 4.4 ounces of raw material at $0.28 per ounce and 0.25 direct labor hours at $12 per hour. Manufacturing overhead is applied at 110% of direct labor costs. Natural Beauty has 273,000 ounces of raw material in beginning inventory and wants 315,000 ounces in ending inventory. What is the total amount of budgeted manufacturing overhead for November?","options":["$200,970","$803,880","$207,900","$3,537,072"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In this example manufacturing overhead is allocated as 110% of direct labor dollars. This means that overhead will be $1.10 for every $1.00 in direct labor. To produce 60,900 lipsticks, 15,225 hours of direct labor will be required. At a wage rate of $12 per hour, budgeted total direct labor dollars would be $182,700 (15,225 × $12). This results in budgeted manufacturing overhead of $200,970 ($15,225 × 110%). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.052_1809","question":"All of the following statements concerning a direct materials purchase budget are correct except:","options":["As budgeted production decreases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases.","As desired ending direct materials inventory deceases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases.","As beginning direct materials inventory decreases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases.","As the amount of direct materials required per unit decreases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Correct. A direct materials purchases budget is an estimate of the quantity and cost of direct materials to be purchased in a period. The quantity to be purchased is based on materials needed for production, beginning direct materials inventory, and desired ending direct materials inventory. Materials needed during a period come from beginning inventory of direct materials and material purchases. As beginning direct materials inventory decreases, the amount of materials to purchase increases, not decreases.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.040_1805","question":"Bake-Fine is planning its material purchase budget. During the month, it expects to sell 50,000 units, each of which requires 1.5 pounds of flour. In addition, it projects a beginning inventory of 3,500 units and a closing inventory of 5,500 units. If the standard material cost of flour is $1.35 per pound, how much is the company's purchasing budget for flour for the month?","options":["$97,200","$112,387.50","$105,300","$101,250"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Total budgeted direct material cost is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Materials per Unit × Budgeted Price.” Budgeted production is calculated as “Budgeted Sales + Desired Ending Inventory – Budgeted Beginning Inventory.” Based on the figures provided, Bake-Fine would budget to produce 52,000 units this month (50,000 + 5,500 − 3,500). It would need 78,000 pounds of flour to produce these units (52,000 × 1.5). At a price of $1.35 per pound, it would cost $105,300 to purchase this flour (78,000 × $1.35). Overall, the budgeted cost to purchase flour for the month is $105,300 (52,000 × 1.5 × $1.35). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.032_1805","question":"Paradise Inc. is preparing its annual direct labor budget. The company's total budgeted direct labor cost is $616,000. Each unit requires 4 direct labor hours at $10 per direct labor hour. How many units is the company budgeting to produce?","options":["61,600","154,000","15,400","6,160"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Total budgeted direct labor cost is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Hours per Unit × Budgeted Wage Rate.” Rearranging the figures results in budgeted production as “Total Budgeted Direct Labor Cost ÷ (Budgeted Hours per Unit × Budgeted Wage Rate).” Based on these figures, Paradise budgeted to produce 15,400 units ($616,000 ÷ (4 × $10)). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.018_1805","question":"Abernathy Enterprises expects to produce and sell 14,286 units in February. Each unit costs $12.42 to produce and sells for $23.50. Abernathy sells its products to retail stores in sets of six. Each set of six costs Abernathy $1.25 to ship. In addition, sales commissions equal 5% of the gross profit for each unit. What will Abernathy's total selling and administrative variable expenses be for February?","options":["$7,914.44","$177,432.12","$25,771.94","$10,890.69"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Variable selling and administrative expenses consist of shipping costs and sales commissions. There will be a total of 2,381 shipments (14,286 units ÷ 6 units per shipment). If each costs $1.25, total shipping costs will be $2,976.25. Sales commissions are 5% of gross profit. Gross profit is selling price less production costs, or $11.08 per unit ($23.50 − $12.42). The commission is therefore $0.554 per unit ($11.08 × 5%) for a total of $7,914.44. The total variable selling and administrative expenses will be $10,890.69 ($2,976.25 + $7,914.44). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.055_1809","question":"Which of the following statements concerning a manufacturing overhead budget is correct?","options":["A production budget must be prepared before a manufacturing overhead budget since the manufacturing overhead budget is based on budgeted production.","A manufacturing overhead budget must be prepared before a production budget since the production budget is based on budgeted manufacturing overhead.","A sales budget must be prepared before a manufacturing overhead budget since the manufacturing overhead budget is based on budgeted sales.","A direct materials budget must be prepared before a manufacturing overhead budget since the manufacturing overhead budget is based on budgeted direct materials."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Correct. A manufacturing overhead budget is an estimate of the total expected manufacturing overhead costs to be incurred in a period. It is based on the number of units expected to be produced during the period. As a result, a production budget must be prepared before a manufacturing overhead budget can be prepared.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.019_1805","question":"Whiting Industries expects to produce and sell 3,180 units in May. Each unit costs $3.47 to produce and sells for $8.59. Whiting sells its products to retail stores in sets of four. Each set of four costs Whiting $1.00 to ship. In addition, sales commissions equal 3% of the gross profit for each unit. What will Whiting's total selling and administrative variable expenses be for May?","options":["$1,283.45","$3,668.45","$11,034.60","$795.00"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Variable selling and administrative expenses consist of shipping costs and sales commissions. There will be a total of 795 shipments (3,180 units ÷ 4 units per shipment). If each shipment costs $1.00, total shipping costs will be $795.00. Sales commissions are 3% of gross profit. Gross profit is selling price less production costs, or $5.12 per unit ($8.59 − $3.47). The commission is therefore $0.1536 per unit ($5.12 × 3%) for a total of $488.45. The total variable selling and administrative expenses will be $1,283.45 ($795 + $488.45). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.030_1805","question":"Katie's Cleaning Service has cleaning contracts for 15 apartments, 45 family homes, and 25 office buildings. She estimates that an apartment takes 4 hours to clean, a home takes 6 hours to clean, and an office building takes 10 hours to clean. Katie pays her cleaning staff $12.50/hour. If each location is cleaned once per week, how much does Katie need to budget for direct labor each month? Assume there are four weeks per month.","options":["$29,000","$7,250","$28,333","$7,083"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Budgeted direct labor is defined as the expected amount to be paid for direct labor to produce the budgeted number of units. It is based on budgeted production, the budgeted amount of direct labor hours required to produce one unit, and the budgeted wage rate to be paid. Katie's would require 60 hours to clean apartments (15 × 4), 270 to clean family homes (45 × 6), and 250 hours to clean office buildings (25 × 10) per week. This translates to 2,320 hours per month. Katie's would budget $29,000 for direct labor for these 2,320 hours (2,320 × $12.50). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.001_1802","question":"What is the relationship between the direct materials and direct labor budgets and the production budget?","options":["The direct materials and direct labor budgets depend on, and are a function of, the production budget.","The production budget depends on, and is a function of, the direct materials and direct labor budgets.","The direct materials budget, but not the direct labor budget, is a function of the production budget.","The direct materials and direct labor budgets have no relation with the production budget."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer correctly describes the relationship between the direct materials and direct labor budgets and the production budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.034_1805","question":"In early March, Percy's Pickled Snacks is preparing the budget for pickled beets for the upcoming quarter. Budgeted sales are 12,000 jars for April, 16,000 jars for May, and 19,000 jars for June. Each jar requires 1.2 pounds of beets and sells for $15. Percy requires ending Finished Goods Inventory equal to 25% of the following month's budgeted sales. If the price of beets is $2 per pound, what is Percy's budgeted cost for beets for May?","options":["$40,200","$33,500","$36,600","$30,500"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Total budgeted direct material cost is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Materials per Unit × Budgeted Price.” Budgeted production is calculated as “Budgeted Sales + Desired Ending Inventory − Budgeted Beginning Inventory.” The budgeted beginning inventory for May is 4,000 jars (16,000 × 25%) and the desired ending inventory for May is 4,750 jars (19,000 × 25%). Based on these figures, Percy would budget to produce 16,750 jars in May (16,000 + 4,750 – 4,000). It would need 20,100 pounds of beets to produce these jars (16,750 × 1.2). At a price of $2 per pound, it would cost $40,200 to purchase these beets (20,100 × $2). Overall, the budgeted cost for beets is $40,200 (16,750 × 1.2 × $2); therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.028_1805","question":"PB Popcorn manufactures gourmet caramel corn, cheese corn, and other flavors of popcorn. It expects sales of 280 cartons of popcorn in the next quarter and it plans to produce 266 cartons during the same quarter. Each carton requires 5 direct labor hours and employees are paid $18 per hour. Manufacturing overhead is applied at 120% of direct labor costs. How much is the total amount of budgeted direct labor for the quarter?","options":["$25,200","$28,728","$23,940","$49,140"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Budgeted direct labor is defined as the expected amount to be paid for direct labor to produce the budgeted number of units. It is based on budgeted production, the budgeted amount of direct labor hours required to produce one unit, and the budgeted wage rate to be paid. It is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Hours per Unit × Budgeted Wage Rate.” PB would budget $23,940 for direct labor to produce the budgeted production of 266 cartons (266 × 5 × $18). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.009_1805","question":"Fukui Electronics is preparing its budgeted production for the coming quarter. The company plans to produce 6,000 units and will require 18,000 pounds of direct materials for production. The total materials required for the quarter are 19,800 pounds. What are the direct materials per unit?","options":["3 pounds.","3.3 pounds.","0.33 pounds.","1.1 pounds."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The total materials required to be purchased for the quarter is calculated as “Materials needed for Production + Desired Ending Inventory − Expected Beginning Inventory.” Materials needed for production is calculated as “Expected Production × Direct Materials per Unit.” Rearranging this results in direct materials per unit being calculated as “Materials Needed for Production ÷ Expected Production.” In this example the direct materials per unit is 3.0 pounds (18,000 ÷ 6,000). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.033_1805","question":"Electra Manufacturing is preparing its annual direct labor budget. The company's total budgeted direct labor cost is $739,200. Each unit requires 2 direct labor hours at $10 per direct labor hour. How many units is the company budgeting to produce?","options":["147,840","73,920","369,600","36,960"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Total budgeted direct labor cost is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Hours per Unit × Budgeted Wage Rate.” Rearranging the figures results in budgeted production as “Total Budgeted Direct Labor Cost ÷ (Budgeted Hours per Unit × Budgeted Wage Rate).” Based on these figures, Electra budgeted to produce 36,960 units ($739,200 ÷ (2 × $10)). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.053_1809","question":"All of the following statements concerning a direct materials purchase budget are correct except:","options":["As budgeted production increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases.","As desired ending direct materials inventory decreases, the amount of materials to purchase increases.","As beginning direct materials inventory decreases, the amount of materials to purchase increases.","As the amount of direct materials required per unit increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. As desired ending direct materials inventory decreases, the amount of materials needed for the period decreases. As materials needed for the period decreases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases, not increases.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.025_1805","question":"PB Popcorn manufactures gourmet caramel corn, cheese corn, and other flavors of popcorn. It expects production of 266 cartons and sales of 280 cartons of popcorn in the next quarter. Each carton requires 5 direct labor hours and employees are paid $18 per hour. Manufacturing overhead is applied at 120% of direct labor cost. What is the total amount of budgeted manufacturing overhead for the quarter?","options":["$30,240","$23,940","$28,728","$58,968"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Manufacturing overhead refers to manufacturing costs that are not directly traceable to products. In this example manufacturing overhead is allocated as 120% of direct labor dollars. This means that overhead will be $1.20 for every $1.00 in direct labor. To produce 266 cartons, 1,330 hours of direct labor will be required. At a wage rate of $18 per hour, budgeted total direct labor dollars would be $23,940 (1,330 × $18). This results in budgeted manufacturing overhead of $28,728 ($23,940 × 120%). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.016_1805","question":"Rose Company is preparing its direct labor budget for May. Projections for the month are that 8,350 units are to be produced and that direct labor time is three hours per unit. If the labor cost per hour is $9, what is the total budgeted direct labor cost for May?","options":["$217,350","$221,400","$243,000","$225,450"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Expected direct labor cost is calculated as “Expected Production x Hours Needed per Unit x Expected Hourly Wage.” In this example it is 8,350 units × 3 hours per unit × $9 per hour. This results in expected direct labor cost of $225,450. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.003_1802","question":"Cut, Inc. (Cut) has developed the following production plan for its premium scissor line.","options":["$12,500.00 for October and $11,250.00 for November","$12,062.50 for October and $12,562.50 for November","$16,625.00 for October and $16,687.50 for November","$25,000.00 for October and $22,500.00 for November"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The following is Cut's direct material purchases budget for October and November.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.035_1805","question":"In early February, Heavenly Swimwear is preparing the budget for swimsuits for the summer season. Budgeted sales are 47,600 suits for May, 58,200 suits for June, and 43,500 suits for July. Each suit requires 1.3 yards of fabric. Each swimsuit sells for $22. Heavenly Swimwear requires ending Finished Goods inventory equal to 30% of the following month's budgeted sales. If the price of fabric is $2 per yard, what is Heavenly Swimwear's budgeted cost for fabric for June?","options":["$107,580","$139,854","$162,786","$125,220"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Total budgeted direct material cost is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Materials per Unit × Budgeted Price.” Budgeted production is calculated as “Budgeted Sales + Desired Ending Inventory – Budgeted Beginning Inventory.” The budgeted beginning inventory for June is 17,460 swimsuits (58,200 × 30%) and the desired ending inventory for June is 13,050 swimsuits (43,500 × 30%). Based on these figures, Heavenly would budget to produce 53,790 swimsuits in June (58,200 + 13,050 – 17,460). It would need 69,927 yards of fabric to produce these swimsuits (53,790 × 1.3). At a price of $2 per yard, it would cost $139,854 to purchase this fabric (69,927 × $2). Overall, the budgeted cost for fabric is $139,854 (53,790 × 1.3 × $2). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.031_1805","question":"James Company determines that 13,500 pounds of direct materials are needed for production in July. There are 800 pounds of direct materials on hand at July 1 and the desired ending inventory is 700 units. If the cost per unit of direct materials is $3, what is the budgeted total cost of direct materials purchases?","options":["$42,600","$40,200","$40,800","$40,500"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The total materials required to be purchased for a period is calculated as “Materials needed for Production + Desired Ending Inventory − Expected Beginning Inventory.” James needs to purchase 13,400 pounds of materials in July (13,500 + 700 − 800). At a cost of $3 per pound, James should budget to spend $40,200 in July to purchase direct materials (13,400 × $3). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.014_1805","question":"From July to December, Healing Hands Massage employs six full-time massage therapists, who each work 40 hours per week. However, they are busier from January to June as people redeem gift certificates that they received at Christmas and as people pamper themselves after school is out or before a wedding. In order to fill all their massage requests, they require 750 direct labor hours per week. How many more full-time massage therapists does Healing Hands need to hire for the period from January to June?","options":["12","13","10","15"],"answer":"B","explanation":"To get 750 labor hours per week, approximately 19 massage therapists are needed each week (750 ÷ 40). Since the company already has 6 full-time massage therapists, 13 more are needed. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.056_1809","question":"All of the following statements are correct except:","options":["Total variable costs change in direct proportion to changes in volume.","Total fixed costs remain the same as volume changes.","Fixed cost per unit decreases as volume increases.","Variable costs per unit increases as production volume increases."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Correct. One type of cost is a variable cost. Examples include direct materials, direct labor, and sales commissions. Total variable costs change in direct proportion to changes in volume. Since total variable costs increase at the same rate as volume increases, the variable costs per unit stays the same when volume increases. It does not increase.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.005_1805","question":"How does the selling and administrative expense budget differ from the direct labor budget?","options":["The direct labor budget is based on the number of units to be produced, whereas the selling and administrative expense budget is based on the number of units the company expects to sell.","The direct labor budget is based on the number of units the company expects to sell, whereas the selling and administrative expense budget it based on the number of units to be produced.","The direct labor budget is based on labor hours, whereas the selling and administrative expense budget is based on machine hours.","The direct labor budget is based on machine hours, whereas the selling and administrative expense budget is based on labor hours."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The selling and administrative budget details the expected amount to be paid for selling and administrative expenses. It is based on the expected units to be sold and the expected variable and selling administrative expenses. The direct labor budget details the amount of labor needed and the expected amount to be paid in wages to direct labor workers. It is based on the number of units expected to be produced, the expected amount of labor hours required to complete each finished unit, and the expected hourly wage to be paid. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.029_1805","question":"Gulfcoast Shrimp Packaging supplies frozen shrimp to grocery chains. It expects sales of 400 tons of shrimp in the next quarter and it plans to produce 380 tons during the same quarter. Each ton requires 50 direct labor hours and employees are paid $15 per hour. Manufacturing overhead is applied at 120% of direct labor costs. How much is the total amount of budgeted direct labor for the quarter?","options":["$300,000","$342,000","$585,000","$285,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Budgeted direct labor is defined as the expected amount to be paid for direct labor to produce the budgeted number of units. It is based on budgeted production, the budgeted amount of direct labor hours required to produce one unit, and the budgeted wage rate to be paid. It is calculated as “Budgeted Production × Budgeted Hours per Unit × Budgeted Wage Rate.” Gulfcoast would budget $285,000 for direct labor to produce the budgeted production of 380 tons (380 × 50 × $15). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.002_1802","question":"If an organization maintains an inventory of direct materials, how does this affect the direct materials budget?","options":["If an organization maintains an inventory of direct materials, the budgeted production needs will equal the quantity of direct materials that will need to be purchased. As a result, the relationship between production needs and materials to be purchased is: Production needs + Beginning inventory – Ending inventory = Materials to purchase.","If an organization maintains an inventory of direct materials, the budgeted production needs will equal the quantity of direct materials that will need to be purchased. As a result, the relationship between production needs and materials to be purchased is: Production needs = Materials to purchase.","If an organization maintains an inventory of direct materials, the budgeted production needs will not equal the quantity of direct materials that will need to be purchased. As a result, the relationship between production needs and materials to be purchased is: Production needs + Ending inventory = Materials to purchase.","If an organization maintains an inventory of direct materials, the budgeted production needs will not equal the quantity of direct materials that will need to be purchased. As a result, the relationship between production needs and materials to be purchased is: Production needs + Ending inventory – Beginning inventory = Materials to purchase."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This answer explains how maintaining an inventory of direct materials impacts the direct materials budget.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.051_1809","question":"All of the following statements concerning a direct materials purchase budget are correct except:","options":["As budgeted production decreases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases.","As desired ending direct materials inventory increases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases.","As beginning direct materials inventory increases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases.","As the amount of direct materials required per unit decreases, the amount of materials to purchase decreases."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correct. A direct materials purchases budget is an estimate of the quantity and cost of direct materials to be purchased in a period. The quantity to be purchased is based on materials needed for production, beginning direct materials inventory, and desired ending direct materials inventory. As desired ending direct materials inventory increases, the amount of materials needed for the period increases. As materials needed for the period increases, the amount of materials to purchase increases, not decreases.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.006_1802","question":"Dish Basics (DB) makes and sells two kinds of dishes: plates and bowls. The first-quarter production budget for each of the dishes is as follows:","options":["$115,920 for January, $123,795 for February, and $120,375 for March","$53,475 for January, $54,720 for February, and $55,650 for March","$121,845 for January, $128,145 for February, and $126,615 for March","$62,445 for January, $69,075 for February, and $64,725 for March"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The following is DB's direct labor budget for January, February, and March.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.002_1805","question":"Which of the following items is correctly paired with its location?","options":["Beginning direct materials – Production budget","Expected unit sales – Production budget","Total required units – Direct materials budget","Desired ending finished goods units – Sales budget"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The expected unit sales appears in the sales budget and the production budget. The production budget includes expected units to be sold, expected beginning inventory, and targeted ending inventory. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.pc.bud.013_1805","question":"In their “off” months, Sanford Accountants employs five full-time accountants who each work 40 hours per week. However, during tax season, Sanford hires additional accountants to help prepare tax returns for clients. In the month of March, Sanford will need to log 3,500 labor hours to complete tax returns. How many additional accountants does Sanford need to hire? Assume March has 22 work days.","options":["15","14","16","12"],"answer":"A","explanation":"To get 3,500 labor hours in March, approximately 159 hours are needed each day (3,500 ÷ 22). To get 159 hours each day, approximately 20 workers are needed each day (159 ÷ 8). Since the company already has 5 full-time workers, 15 more are needed. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.pc.bud.005_1802","question":"Fluffy, Inc. (Fluffy) makes and sells two kinds of marshmallows: regular and jumbo. The third-quarter production budget for each of the marshmallows is as follows:","options":["$12,330 for July, $14,850 for August, and $17,496 for September","$4,806 for July, $5,814 for August, and $5,310 for September","$17,136 for July, $20,664 for August, and $22,806 for September","$15,777 for July, $19,053 for August, and $19,368 for September"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The following is Fluffy's direct labor budget for July, August, and September.","unit":14,"subunit":"14.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0}];